Task:
A maintainer on the project aviboddu/NodeScript (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #29: Deduplicate and optimize the node execution plan

## Problem
`Script.CompileNodes()` builds `NodesToExecute` with a queue and `executionList.Contains(output)`. On converging graphs, a node can be enqueued more than once before it is added to the list, causing it to appear multiple times in the execution plan and potentially run multiple times in one cycle. The repeated linear `Contains` check also makes plan construction quadratic for larger graphs.

## Proposed work
- Build the reachable execution plan using a visited set marked when a node is enqueued.
- Preserve the current deterministic traversal order for valid acyclic graphs.
- Ensure each reachable node occurs exactly once in `NodesToExecute`.
- Add focused tests for fan-out/diamond graphs, including expected output and one-step execution behavior.
- Extend the benchmark suite with at least one converging graph scenario so scheduling overhead and regressions are measurable.

## Acceptance criteria
- A reachable node is executed at most once per `StepLine()` cycle.
- Valid disconnected nodes retain their current behavior.
- Execution-plan construction avoids repeated linear membership scans.
- Existing successful-script behavior remains unchanged.
- New tests and the full existing test suite pass.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Dependency execution plan: model a node script graph with typed
dependency edges and produce a reachable, deduplicated execution
plan via a walker.

Mirrors the issue: a naive queue-based plan builder re-enqueues a
node on every incoming convergent path (diamond), so the node runs
twice per StepLine cycle. The fix is to mark nodes at *enqueue*
time using a visited set carried by the walker, so each reachable
node appears at most once in the resulting plan.
"""

node Script {
    has name: str = "";
    has produced: int = 0;
    has visited_count: int = 0;
}

edge DependsOn { }

node Output {
    has name: str = "";
    has value: int = 0;
}

walker PlanBuilder {
    has visited: list[str] = [];
    has plan: list[Script] = [];

    def already_seen(name: str) -> bool {
        for n in self.visited {
            if n == name {
                return True;
            }
        }
        return False;
    }

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can gather with Script entry {
        if self.already_seen(here.name) {
            visit [-->];
            return;
        }
        self.visited.append(here.name);
        self.plan.append(here);
        visit [-->];
    }
}

walker Executor {
    can start with Root entry {
        print("=== Building execution plan ===");
        planner = PlanBuilder();
        root spawn planner;
        plan = planner.plan;
        print(f"Plan length: {len(plan)} node(s)");
        for n in plan {
            print(f"  - {n.name}");
        }
        print("=== Running one StepLine cycle ===");
        for node in plan {
            node.produced += 1;
            node.visited_count += 1;
            print(f"[step] {node.name} produced={node.produced}");
        }
        print("=== Verifying each reachable node ran at most once ===");
        for node in plan {
            assert node.visited_count == 1 , f"{node.name} ran {node.visited_count} times";
        }
        print("All reachable nodes executed exactly once.");
    }
}

with entry {
    # Diamond: Source -> A, B -> Sink. Sink has two parents and used
    # to be enqueued twice by the naive queue/Contains plan builder.
    source = Script(name="Source");
    branch_a = Script(name="BranchA");
    branch_b = Script(name="BranchB");
    sink = Script(name="Sink");
    sink_out = Output(name="SinkOut", value=0);

    source +>:DependsOn:+> branch_a;
    source +>:DependsOn:+> branch_b;
    branch_a +>:DependsOn:+> sink;
    branch_b +>:DependsOn:+> sink;

    # A second disconnected subgraph to show disconnected nodes are
    # handled the same as before.
    lone = Script(name="Lone");
    root ++> source ++> branch_a ++> branch_b ++> sink ++> sink_out;
    root ++> lone;

    root spawn Executor();
}
```

