Issue #412: [Roadmap][P3] Evaluate knowledge graph and graph database architecture

## What to build

Roadmap item to evaluate a structured knowledge graph and graph database option, such as Neo4j, for WISPACE agent knowledge retrieval. Compare graph retrieval with the existing curated RAG direction and determine whether a graph, vector, or hybrid architecture is justified by real product knowledge and relationship-query needs.

This issue is for discovery and architecture evaluation only. It must not introduce a graph database speculatively or make graph data authoritative for learner identity, personal facts, authorization, or side-effect decisions.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Identify concrete WISPACE knowledge use cases where explicit relationships, prerequisites, constraints, or provenance provide value beyond curated FAQ and vector retrieval.
- [ ] Define a minimal domain model for nodes, edges, source provenance, version, freshness, confidence, and conflict handling.
- [ ] Compare PostgreSQL-based relational/recursive queries and existing retrieval conventions with Neo4j or equivalent graph-database options, including operational complexity, latency, cost, backup/restore, observability, and failure recovery.
- [ ] Evaluate graph-only and hybrid graph-plus-vector retrieval boundaries, including when each path should be selected.
- [ ] Treat retrieved graph content as untrusted knowledge that cannot override server-derived learner facts, identity checks, authorization, prompt rules, or confirmation policies.
- [ ] Define tenant, learner, platform, and agent isolation; keep personal learner data out of the initial graph unless a separate privacy design explicitly justifies it.
- [ ] Define ingestion validation, schema evolution, duplicate/conflicting relationship handling, poisoning/injection defenses, and source update workflows.
- [ ] Define privacy, retention, unlink, deletion, export, and rollback requirements for any graph data that could become associated with a learner.
- [ ] Establish measurable evaluation criteria for retrieval precision, relationship correctness, stale-data suppression, grounding, latency, cost, and operational recovery.
- [ ] Record a concrete adoption trigger, migration/feature-flag plan, and rollback plan; no production graph database implementation is implied by this roadmap issue.

## Blocked by

None - roadmap discovery/implementation is intentionally deferred.

