Issue #2529: bug: recursive struct check E3061 is bypassed through a type alias, leaking a C compiler error

### Description

A struct that contains itself by value is rejected with `E3061`. Routing the self-reference through a type alias skips that check entirely — the typechecker emits no diagnostic, codegen produces a struct with a field of its own incomplete type, and the user sees a raw C compiler error.

This affects both direct self-reference and mutual recursion between two structs.

### Expected behavior

`E3061: struct 'Node' cannot contain itself by value; use a pointer field '^Node' for recursive types`, the same as the direct form.

### Actual behavior

Compilation reaches `cc`, which fails:

```
/tmp/gray_run-1.c:15:21: error: field has incomplete type 'GrayStruct_Node' (aka 'struct GrayStruct_Node')
   15 |     GrayStruct_Node next;
      |                     ^
/tmp/gray_run-1.c:14:8: note: definition of 'struct GrayStruct_Node' is not complete until the closing '}'
   14 | struct GrayStruct_Node {
      |        ^
1 error generated.
gray: C compilation failed
```

### Minimal reproduction — direct self-reference

```gray
const Node struct {
    next NodeAlias
    v int
}

alias NodeAlias = Node

do main() {
    println("hi")
}
```

### Minimal reproduction — mutual recursion

The same bypass defeats the cycle check between two structs:

```gray
const A struct {
    b BAlias
}
const B struct {
    a AAlias
}

alias AAlias = A
alias BAlias = B

do main() {
    println("hi")
}
```

```
/tmp/gray_run-1.c:16:18: error: field has incomplete type 'GrayStruct_B' (aka 'struct GrayStruct_B')
   16 |     GrayStruct_B b;
      |                  ^
/tmp/gray_run-1.c:13:16: note: forward declaration of 'struct GrayStruct_B'
   13 | typedef struct GrayStruct_B GrayStruct_B;
      |                ^
1 error generated.
gray: C compilation failed
```

### Contrast — both forms are caught without aliases

```gray
const Node struct {
    next Node
    v int
}

do main() {
    println("hi")
}
```

```
error[E3061]: struct 'Node' cannot contain itself by value; use a pointer field '^Node' for recursive types
  --> repro.gray:1:7
   |
  1 | const Node struct {
   |       ^
```

```gray
const A struct {
    b B
}
const B struct {
    a A
}

do main() {
    println("hi")
}
```

```
error[E3061]: struct 'A' cannot contain itself by value through 'B'; break the cycle with a pointer field '^B'
error[E3061]: struct 'B' cannot contain itself by value through 'A'; break the cycle with a pointer field '^A'
```

And the pointer form works as intended:

```gray
const Node struct {
    next ^Node
    v int
}

do main() {
    println("hi")   // compiles and runs
}
```


Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Recursive struct cycle detection: E3061 scenario modeled as a graph.

Two struct archetypes (Node, Peer) attempt to hold each other by value
through an alias edge. A walker walks the type graph and reports every
cycle it finds — the cycle policy must be explicit because walkers in
jac 0.36.1 do not dedupe visited nodes on their own.
"""

# ---- Node archetypes (modeling the structs) ----

node TypeDef {
    has name: str = "";
}

node StructType {
    has name: str = "";
    # Whether this struct already opts into the pointer-form fix.
    has uses_pointer: bool = False;
}

# An "alias" is a named indirection that the typechecker must follow
# when deciding whether a struct field is a by-value self-reference.
node AliasType {
    has name: str = "";
}

# ---- Typed edges ----

# "struct X has a field of alias A"
edge AliasField { }

# "alias A resolves to struct X"
edge ResolvesTo { }

# A plain parent/child link in the type graph (used only for ordering).
edge DeclaredBefore { }

# ---- Walker: cycle auditor ----

walker CycleAuditor {
    has findings: list[str] = [];
    has stack: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    # First stop: enter the type namespace, walk every declared struct.
    can enter_ns with TypeDef entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    # On a struct, descend through every alias-field it contains.
    # We do NOT auto-follow the alias here — we just record the field
    # edge and let the union ability on AliasType resolve it.
    can scan_struct with StructType entry {
        self.stack = self.stack + [here.name];
        visit [->:AliasField:->];
    }

    # Union trigger: this fires whether we land on a bare struct field
    # OR on an alias indirection, because the field edge from a struct
    # always points to an AliasType node, and we explicitly hop from
    # there to whatever the alias resolves to via a ResolvesTo edge.
    can resolve_alias with AliasType entry {
        visit [->:ResolvesTo:->];
    }

    # Final destination: another StructType. This is where the cycle
    # check actually runs. We compare against the stack of structs
    # we are currently inside.
    can check_cycle with StructType entry {
        verdict = "ok";
        for frame in self.stack {
            if frame == here.name {
                verdict = "CYCLE: " + here.name + " contains itself by value";
                break;
            }
        }
        if verdict == "ok" {
            print(f"[ok] {here.name} reached via alias chain");
        } else {
            print(f"[E3061] {verdict}");
            self.findings = self.findings + [verdict];
        }
    }
}

# ---- Walker: pointer-fix auditor ----
#
# The expected-behavior half of the issue: structs that break the cycle
# with a pointer field should compile and run, so this walker just
# confirms they are reachable from root and prints a green light.

walker PointerFormChecker {
    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can enter_ns with TypeDef entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can confirm with StructType entry {
        if here.uses_pointer {
            print(f"[compile-ok] {here.name} uses '^' field — no E3061");
        } else {
            print(f"[audit] {here.name} uses by-value fields — depends on E3061");
        }
    }
}

# ---- Demo wiring ----

with entry {
    ns = TypeDef(name="types");
    root ++> ns;

    # --- Case A: the BUGGY direct self-reference through an alias ---
    #
    # Node has a field "next" whose declared type is NodeAlias.
    # NodeAlias resolves back to Node. The typechecker should catch
    # this and emit E3061, but in the buggy build it does not.
    buggy_node = StructType(name="Node");
    ns +>:DeclaredBefore:+> buggy_node;
    next_alias = AliasType(name="NodeAlias");
    buggy_node +>:AliasField:+> next_alias;
    next_alias +>:ResolvesTo:+> buggy_node;

    # --- Case B: the BUGGY mutual recursion through aliases ---
    a_struct = StructType(name="A");
    b_struct = StructType(name="B");
    ns +>:DeclaredBefore:+> a_struct;
    ns +>:DeclaredBefore:+> b_struct;

    a_alias = AliasType(name="AAlias");
    b_alias = AliasType(name="BAlias");
    a_struct +>:AliasField:+> b_alias;
    b_alias +>:ResolvesTo:+> b_struct;

    b_struct +>:AliasField:+> a_alias;
    a_alias +>:ResolvesTo:+> a_struct;

    # --- Case C: the FIXED form using a pointer field ---
    fixed_node = StructType(name="NodeFixed", uses_pointer=True);
    ns +>:DeclaredBefore:+> fixed_node;
    fixed_alias = AliasType(name="NodeFixedAlias");
    fixed_node +>:AliasField:+> fixed_alias;
    # Pointer indirection is still represented by the same edge in the
    # type graph, but the struct opts into uses_pointer=True so the
    # pointer-form checker reports green.

    # --- Run the auditors ---
    auditor = CycleAuditor();
    root spawn auditor;
    print(f"cycle auditor findings: {auditor.findings}");

    checker = PointerFormChecker();
    root spawn checker;
}
```

