Issue #1187: SEK-G56 dcb: bind ordinary null materialized-view parameters through Dapper

# SEK-G56 dcb: bind ordinary null materialized-view parameters through Dapper

## Goal
Bind ordinary nullable materialized-view SQL parameters as SQL NULL without projection faults caused by Dapper inferring System.DBNull.

## Why This Slice Exists Now
Origin #1186 prevents event application/checkpoint progress. The operator approved continued development after independent design review.

## Current Observed State
At upstream 8189da6e, MvParamConverter.ToClrValue maps Null to DBNull.Value. The four executors and four query ports pass that value to Dapper, whose non-null value type inference rejects it. Reporter runtime evidence was on 10.17.0; source was independently verified unchanged through 10.20.0/main. This packet requires upstream runtime regression proof.

## Accepted Baseline You May Assume
Preserve serialized MvParamKind.Null. A non-Null kind with null ValueJson is invalid. Explicit application DBNull input is unsupported. Null binding is untyped and depends on query/provider inference.

## Target Repo / Path / Part
Repository: J-Tech-Japan/Sekiban. Target: dcb materialized-view converter, four SQL providers, dcb/tests, existing EN/JA docs and relevant CI. PR base: main.

## In Scope
Minimal Dapper boundary fix, real SQL regression/provider characterization, documents and CI coverage.

## Out Of Scope
Typed-null wire extension; active lifecycle (#1184); large-event transport (#1185); version bump, package publication or release.

## Standalone Child Issue Contract
Correct Null-to-Dapper mapping for ordinary nullable parameters across statement and query paths, prove actual PostgreSQL execution and all provider paths, preserve non-null/malformed-input semantics, and document measured untyped-null limits. Do not claim unsupported typed-null shapes are repaired; report any ordinary supported-case failure to design.

## Acceptance Criteria

- AC1: MvParamKind.Null maps to CLR null at the Dapper boundary. Serialized Null representation and non-Null Kind with null ValueJson rejection remain unchanged. Application-supplied DBNull.Value remains explicitly unsupported; non-null values and absent parameters retain behavior.
- AC2: Real PostgreSQL test through NativeMvApplyHost and the normal executor inserts non-null text, applies a persisted event with an anonymous nullable string property set to null, asserts SQL NULL, revision advance and committed projection checkpoint, then proves a later non-null update. Reverting the null conversion makes this test fail at the actual binding boundary.
- AC3: Exercise null through all four provider executors and all four query ports using the existing multi-provider fixtures. PostgreSQL includes an inferable query-port null. Characterize PostgreSQL uuid/timestamp/bytea and SQL Server non-text/varbinary plus MySQL/SQLite nullable text; record exact successes or provider limitations with executed commands and versions.
- AC4: Include a deliberately non-inferable PostgreSQL scalar such as SELECT @P and measure behavior rather than assuming failure. The contract is untyped SQL NULL where SQL/provider can infer type; a measured unsupported typed-null/query shape may be documented without extending the wire contract. Failure in an ordinary documented supported case requires a design report before acceptance.
- AC5: Add discriminating tests for present-null, absent parameter, non-null, explicit DBNull input, and malformed non-Null wire kind with null ValueJson. Preserve failure visibility; do not swallow projection errors or weaken checkpoint guarantees.
- AC6: Document untyped-null semantics, measured provider/query limitations and explicit DBNull rejection in EN/JA materialized-view documentation. Run relevant existing materialized-view suites on supported TFMs and ensure new regression tests are exercised by appropriate CI jobs; run git diff --check. Do not alter package versions or release artifacts.

## Verification
Use existing materialized-view PostgreSQL and multi-provider fixtures. Real Dapper execution is mandatory; converter-only tests do not prove AC2. Record failing-before/passing-after regression, provider versions/commands and supported TFM runs. Each test must discriminate old and corrected behavior; no tautological mocks.

