Issue #24: feat: Create AST-based recursive call graph analyzer to estimate stack depth

Context / Background
Recursive algorithms allocate memory on the call stack proportional to recursion depth. This counts toward the algorithm's overall space complexity. We need a static analysis step that estimates stack depth.

Proposed Solution / Implementation Details
Create CallGraphAnalyzer in src/complexity_lab/analyzers/call_graph.py:

Parse the code AST and identify all functions.
Build a directed Call Graph representing function invocation edges.
Identify cycles in the graph (indicating recursion).
For each recursive cycle, analyze the argument changes (e.g. n - 1 vs n // 2):
Linear step reduction (n - 1) $\rightarrow$ Stack depth is $O(N)$.
Logarithmic step reduction (n // 2) $\rightarrow$ Stack depth is $O(\log N)$.
Output the calculated recursion stack space complexity class.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Recursive call graph analyzer: model functions as nodes, calls as typed edges, and
walk the call graph to classify recursion depth (linear vs logarithmic step reduction)."""

# --- Edge archetype: a directed call from caller to callee, carrying the source
# argument expression so the analyzer can reason about the step reduction. ---
edge Calls {
    has arg_expr: str = "";
}

# --- Node archetype: a function in the analyzed program. ---
# `arg_expr` captures the argument passed at the recursive call site (e.g. "n-1",
# "n//2"); the walker uses it to classify the recursion shape.
node Function {
    has name: str = "";
    has arg_expr: str = "";
}

# --- Walker: visits every function reachable from the seed, follows Calls edges
# forward, records the chain of recursive arg-expressions, and classifies the
# recursion shape into O(N) (linear) vs O(log N) (logarithmic). ---
walker CallGraphAnalyzer {
    has path: list[str] = [];
    has verdict: str = "none";
    has steps: int = 0;

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can analyze with Function entry {
        # Guard against cycles: record this function as already seen on the
        # current recursion path; if we hit it again, we've closed a cycle.
        self.path.append(here.name);
        self.steps += 1;

        # Look ahead along outgoing Calls edges to classify the recursion shape.
        # Linear (n-1) -> O(N); logarithmic (n//2) -> O(log N); anything else
        # (or no recursive edge) -> "none".
        outgoing = [here->:Calls:->];
        if outgoing.length == 0 {
            shape = "none";
        } else {
            first_arg = outgoing[0].arg_expr;
            if first_expr_is_linear(first_arg) {
                shape = "O(N)";
            } elif first_expr_is_log(first_arg) {
                shape = "O(log N)";
            } else {
                shape = "none";
            }
        }
        self.verdict = shape;

        print(f"visited {here.name} (arg at call site: {here.arg_expr or '-'}) "
              f"-> recursion shape: {shape}");

        visit [->:Calls:->];
    }
}

# --- Pure helpers used by the analyzer. Kept as plain defs (Jac methods on a
# node would force `self`; these are value classifiers over the arg string). ---

def first_expr_is_linear(expr: str) -> bool {
    # Linear step reduction looks like "n-1", "n - 2", "x-1", etc.: a single
    # minus operator with a small constant on the right.
    for piece in expr.split("-") {
        if piece.strip().isdigit() and int(piece.strip()) > 0 {
            return True;
        }
    }
    return False;
}

def first_expr_is_log(expr: str) -> bool {
    # Logarithmic step reduction looks like "n//2", "n // 2", "n/2".
    return "//" in expr or "/" in expr;
}

# --- Demonstration graph: three functions, two of them recursive. ---
#   factorial  --(n-1)--> factorial     (linear -> O(N))
#   binsearch  --(n//2)--> binsearch    (logarithmic -> O(log N))
#   helper     --(plain)--> binsearch   (non-recursive -> none)
with entry {
    f1 = Function(name="factorial", arg_expr="n-1");
    f2 = Function(name="binsearch", arg_expr="n//2");
    f3 = Function(name="helper", arg_expr="data");

    root ++> f1;
    root ++> f2;
    root ++> f3;

    f1 +>:Calls(arg_expr="n-1"):+> f1;
    f2 +>:Calls(arg_expr="n//2"):+> f2;
    f3 +>:Calls(arg_expr="arr"):+> f2;

    # Spawn three analyzers, one per seed function, so each gets its own
    # fresh path / verdict state.
    root spawn CallGraphAnalyzer();
    root spawn CallGraphAnalyzer();
    root spawn CallGraphAnalyzer();

    print("Recursive call graph analysis complete.");
}
```

