Issue #1: ContractGraph: trustworthy agentic contract retrieval POC

## Overview
ContractGraph is a local-first Python proof of concept demonstrating trustworthy agentic contract retrieval over fictional automotive-enterprise IT contracts. Enterprise contracts spread operative obligations across base agreements, amendments, exhibits, schedules, and referenced policies; vector-only RAG retrieves semantically relevant but legally obsolete language, misses identifiers/defined terms, ignores amendment precedence, and answers despite missing or conflicting evidence. The POC's central proof: graph-grounded agentic retrieval resolves an amendment-sensitive obligation (60-day superseded termination clause vs. operative 90-day clause) that vector-only retrieval gets wrong — while remaining observable, reliable, secure, economical, and reproducible, in a ten-minute live demo.

## Use Cases / User Stories
Enterprise contract analysts can:
- Ask natural-language contract questions to find operative terms without manual document search.
- Distinguish current from superseded clauses.
- Verify every answer claim at document, page, section, and clause level (exact provenance).
- Get explicit insufficiency explanations instead of fabricated answers from absent evidence.
- Escalate conflicting evidence for human review rather than hiding ambiguity behind model confidence.
- Resume a checkpointed review after selecting controlling evidence or abstention (auditable human judgment).
- Compare semantically equivalent supplier obligations across agreements despite different wording.
- Answer multi-hop questions via obligations linked to obligor, obligee, trigger, policy, and source clause.
- See missing schedules/exhibits identified explicitly (no inference from absent sources).
- See uncertainty stated as concrete reasons (missing, conflicting, degraded, or ambiguous evidence).

For interviewers:
- A ten-minute demo with five coherent questions.
- Side-by-side vector-only vs. graph-grounded results showing graph traversal benefit rather than asserting it.
- Incorrect retrievals classified with trace evidence (not post-hoc LLM narratives).
- A small, explicit LangGraph workflow where branching, fan-out, bounded recovery, and termination are architectural, not hidden in prompt logic.
- Deterministic nodes distinguished from model-powered nodes (no exaggerated agent count).
- End-to-end observability: query plans, candidates, scores, graph paths, sufficiency decisions, claims, citations.
- A safely demonstrated retriever failure (reliability behavior).

## Current implementation (planned solution)
A bounded LangGraph workflow: analyzes each question → typed retrieval plan → fans out to lexical, vector, and allowlisted graph retrieval → fuses and reranks candidates → evaluates an intent-specific evidence contract → at most one recovery round → synthesizes grounded claims → verifies citations → returns an explicit answer disposition with a complete execution trace.

Corpus and ingestion: deliberately narrow fictional corpus ingested into lexical, vector, and graph retrieval structures with clause-level provenance.

Demo scenarios (Streamlit interface + reproducible ablation report):
- Amendment path to operative 90-day termination clause vs. vector-only selecting obsolete 60-day clause.
- Exhibit-based obligations.
- Semantic cross-contract comparison.
- Human review for unresolved conflict.
- Abstention for a missing schedule.
- Prompt-injection resistance (resisting instructions embedded in retrieved documents).
- Graceful degradation when vector retrieval times out.

## Constraints & Stack
- Local open-source retrieval and reranking components only; optional OpenAI calls.
- Recorded replay for tests and published evaluations.
- Exact response caching; provider prompt-cache telemetry; model routing.
- SQLite persistence; OpenTelemetry spans.
- Thin FastAPI adapter; single-page Streamlit diagnostic UI.
- Azure and AWS documented only as production mappings (not used in POC).

## Pain
Vector-only RAG fails on contract corpora: obsolete language presented as controlling, missed exact identifiers and defined terms, ignored amendment precedence, and answering despite missing/conflicting evidence — all unacceptable when users need identifiable controlling clauses, exact provenance, exposed retrieval reasoning, contained cost, safe degradation, injection resistance, and honest abstention.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""ContractGraph: graph-grounded traversal of contract obligations.

Models the core scenario from the issue — an amendment overrides a base
clause (60-day superseded termination clause vs. operative 90-day clause).
The walker traverses Base -> Amendment edges, recognizes the amendment
as controlling, and emits an explicit disposition for the obligation.
"""

node Contract {
    has title: str = "";
    has effective_date: str = "";
}

node Clause {
    has text: str = "";
    has page: int = 0;
    has section: str = "";
}

node Obligation {
    has name: str = "";
    has trigger: str = "";
    has term_days: int = 0;
    has operative: bool = True;
}

edge PartOf { has role: str = ""; }
edge Supersedes { has reason: str = ""; }
edge Fulfills { has source_clause: str = ""; }

walker ObligationAuditor {
    has query: str = "termination notice";
    has findings: list = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        print(f"Auditing obligations for query: '{self.query}'");
        visit [->:PartOf:->];
    }

    can audit_contract with Contract entry {
        print(f"  Contract: {here.title} (eff. {here.effective_date})");
        visit [->:PartOf:->];
    }

    can audit_obligation with Obligation entry {
        status = "OPERATIVE" if here.operative else "SUPERSEDED";
        finding = {
            "obligation": here.name,
            "trigger": here.trigger,
            "term_days": here.term_days,
            "status": status
        };
        self.findings.append(finding);
        print(f"    [{status}] {here.name}: {here.term_days}-day "
              f"trigger='{here.trigger}'");
    }
}

walker AmendmentPathFinder {
    has base_title: str = "MSA-Vendor-A";
    has controlling_days: int = 0;
    has found_controlling: bool = False;

    can start with Root entry {
        print(f"Resolving amendment path for '{self.base_title}'");
        visit [->:PartOf:->];
    }

    can find_base with Contract entry {
        if here.title == self.base_title {
            print(f"Base found: {here.title}");
            visit [->:Supersedes:->];
        } else {
            visit [->:PartOf:->];
        }
    }

    can find_controlling with Obligation entry {
        if not here.operative {
            print(f"  skip superseded: {here.name} ({here.term_days}-day)");
            if here.term_days == 60 {
                print("    (vector-only would have picked this obsolete clause)");
            }
            visit [->:PartOf:->];
            return;
        }
        if here.term_days == 90 and here.trigger == "termination" {
            self.controlling_days = here.term_days;
            self.found_controlling = True;
            print(f"  CONTROLLING: {here.name} -> {here.term_days}-day "
                  f"termination notice");
            disengage;
        }
        visit [->:PartOf:->];
    }
}

walker DispositionReporter {
    has query: str = "";
    has answer: str = "INSUFFICIENT";
    has reason: str = "";

    can start with Root entry {
        print(f"Disposition for query: '{self.query}'");
        visit [->:PartOf:->];
    }

    can scan with Contract entry {
        visit [->:PartOf:->];
    }

    can decide with Obligation entry {
        if self.query == "termination" and here.trigger == "termination" {
            if here.operative and here.term_days == 90 {
                self.answer = "ANSWERED";
                self.reason = f"operative clause: {here.term_days}-day notice";
                disengage;
            } elif not here.operative and here.term_days == 60 {
                self.answer = "SUPERSEDED";
                self.reason = "60-day clause overridden by amendment";
            } else {
                self.answer = "CONFLICT";
                self.reason = "competing operative and superseded clauses";
            }
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    msa = Contract(title="MSA-Vendor-A", effective_date="2023-01-01");
    amd1 = Contract(title="Amendment-1-Vendor-A", effective_date="2024-06-15");

    base_termination = Clause(
        text="Either party may terminate upon 60 days written notice.",
        page=12, section="8.1"
    );
    amd_termination = Clause(
        text="Notwithstanding Section 8.1, termination requires 90 days notice.",
        page=3, section="A.2"
    );

    obsolete_obligation = Obligation(
        name="60-day termination (obsolete)",
        trigger="termination", term_days=60, operative=False
    );
    controlling_obligation = Obligation(
        name="90-day termination (operative)",
        trigger="termination", term_days=90, operative=True
    );

    root +>:PartOf(role="base"):+> msa;
    root +>:PartOf(role="amendment"):+> amd1;

    msa +>:PartOf:+> base_termination;
    amd1 +>:PartOf:+> amd_termination;

    base_termination +>:Fulfills(source_clause="8.1"):+> obsolete_obligation;
    amd_termination +>:Fulfills(source_clause="A.2"):+> controlling_obligation;

    amd1 +>:Supersedes(reason="Amendment-1 supersedes Section 8.1"):+> msa;

    print("=== Audit pass (all obligations) ===");
    root spawn ObligationAuditor(query="termination notice");

    print("\n=== Amendment path to operative clause ===");
    root spawn AmendmentPathFinder();

    print("\n=== Disposition for 'termination' query ===");
    reporter = DispositionReporter(query="termination");
    root spawn reporter;
    print(f"DISPOSITION: {reporter.answer} -- {reporter.reason}");
}
```

