Issue #86: [Maintenance] CI/CD: License audit on transitive dependencies (dotnet-licenses / FOSSA)

## Scope

Dependabot tracks dependency *versions* (and CVE alerts). It does not check the *licenses* of transitive dependencies. A new transitive dependency with an incompatible license (GPL, AGPL, OSL, business-source) silently leaks into a derivative work distributed under MIT — a real procurement / legal exposure for downstream consumers.

The deferred work is a license-audit workflow using `dotnet-licenses` / FOSSA / Snyk Open Source / similar that enumerates every transitive package, captures its declared license, and fails the build on incompatible / unknown licenses.

## Acceptance criteria

- A `license-audit.yaml` workflow runs on PR + scheduled.
- Findings produce a `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` (or similar) generated automatically per release; license attribution is shipped in the NuGet package per the licenses' requirements.
- A documented allowlist (MIT / Apache-2.0 / BSD-2-Clause / BSD-3-Clause / similar) gates new packages; anything else needs explicit reviewer approval recorded in the PR.
- A baseline scan of the current `main` documents current dependencies + licenses.

## Notes

- Tier-1 follow-up. Required for procurement at most enterprises; goes beyond Dependabot's surface.
- Originated as part of the IAsyncEnumerable-Extensions thorough-review pass; tracked fleet-wide.
- Board: https://github.com/users/Chris-Wolfgang/projects/6

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""License audit workflow: walk the transitive dependency graph, classify licenses, and report incompatibilities.

Models the scenario from issue #86 where a maintainer needs to audit licenses of every
transitive dependency before distributing an MIT-licensed derivative work. The graph
holds packages connected by `DependsOn` edges; a walker recursively descends, applies
the allowlist policy, and reports incompatible / unknown licenses — replacing a
hand-rolled recursion over `parent_id` or adjacency maps.
"""

node Package {
    has name: str = "";
    has version: str = "";
    has license: str = "";
    has approved: bool = False;
}

edge DependsOn {
    has scope: str = "runtime";
}

walker LicenseAuditor {
    has visited: list[str] = [];
    has allowlist: list[str] = ["MIT", "Apache-2.0", "BSD-2-Clause", "BSD-3-Clause"];
    has findings: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        print("Starting license audit of transitive dependency graph");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can audit with Package entry {
        # Cycle guard: explicit in walker (jac 0.36.1 has no built-in once-per-node).
        if here.name in self.visited {
            print(f"  [skip] {here.name} already audited (cycle guard)");
            disengage;
        }
        self.visited.append(here.name);

        verdict = self.classify(here);
        marker = "OK" if verdict == "allow" else "FLAG";
        print(f"  [{marker}] {here.name}@{here.version} :: {here.license} -> {verdict}");
        if verdict != "allow" {
            note = f"{here.name}@{here.version} ({here.license}) is {verdict}";
            self.findings.append(note);
        }

        # Walk transitive dependencies via typed edge.
        visit [->:DependsOn:->];
    }

    def classify(pkg: Package) -> str {
        declared = pkg.license;
        if declared == "" {
            return "unknown";
        }
        if declared in self.allowlist {
            return "allow";
        }
        if declared in ["GPL-3.0", "AGPL-3.0", "OSL-3.0", "BUSL-1.1"] {
            return "incompatible";
        }
        return "unknown";
    }
}

with entry {
    # Application root under MIT.
    app = Package(name="LogCompressor", version="1.0.0", license="MIT", approved=True);

    # Direct dependencies.
    serilog = Package(name="Serilog", version="3.1.1", license="Apache-2.0", approved=True);
    newtonsoft = Package(name="Newtonsoft.Json", version="13.0.3", license="MIT", approved=True);
    cliwrap = Package(name="CliWrap", version="3.7.0", license="MIT", approved=True);

    # Transitive: one clean Apache dep, one problematic GPL dep reached via two paths.
    system_text_encodings_web = Package(name="System.Text.Encoding.Web", version="7.0.0",
                                         license="Apache-2.0", approved=True);
    gpl_leak = Package(name="GPLLeak", version="0.1.0", license="GPL-3.0", approved=False);

    # Another transitive of unknown license to demonstrate the unknown bucket.
    mystery_pkg = Package(name="MysteryPkg", version="0.0.1", license="", approved=False);

    # Build the dependency tree. Use typed edges so the auditor can scope its walk.
    root ++> app;
    app +>:DependsOn(scope="direct"):+> serilog;
    app +>:DependsOn(scope="direct"):+> newtonsoft;
    app +>:DependsOn(scope="direct"):+> cliwrap;
    serilog +>:DependsOn(scope="transitive"):+> system_text_encodings_web;
    newtonsoft +>:DependsOn(scope="transitive"):+> gpl_leak;
    # Second path to the GPL leak to exercise the cycle/visited guard.
    cliwrap +>:DependsOn(scope="transitive"):+> gpl_leak;
    serilog +>:DependsOn(scope="transitive"):+> mystery_pkg;

    auditor = LicenseAuditor();
    root spawn auditor;

    print(f"\nAudit complete: visited {len(auditor.visited)} packages, "
          f"{len(auditor.findings)} flagged");
    for finding in auditor.findings {
        print(f"  - {finding}");
    }
    print("\nRequired action: reviewer approval recorded in PR for any non-allowlisted license.");
}
