Task:
A maintainer on the project vishaltandale00/relayer-graphcomplete (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #228: Wayfinder: Temporal recursive GraphComplete

## Destination

Deliver the product contract in #227: `complete()` is a recursive graph-semantic function whose root and child invocations each expose a durable current pointer, publish coherent working graphs over time, and resolve to an agent-chosen final layer without adding a GraphComplete scheduler or exposing private reasoning.

This map is design and delivery navigation. It does not authorize implementation.

## Notes

- The graph is both the agent's temporal user-facing medium and its coherent final output. Finalization may reorganize or synthesize the work, but it should not be the first time useful work appears.
- Each active `complete()` has one current pointer. The root current supplies the default active-turn presentation; child currents supply live entry points into recursively invoked work.
- `current.advance(layer)` is an explicit agent judgment. GraphComplete validates and atomically publishes the owned reachable closure, then moves the pointer.
- Temporal continuity means previously visible work remains graph-natively accessible. It does not imply a hidden forced edge or a separate History UI.
- Current pointers progress asynchronously bottom-up. Child currents inform parent synthesis, while each agent remains responsible for choosing its own current and final presentation.
- Human interactions and agent-authored recursive code invoke one `complete(inputGraph)` function. Agent code decides what to invoke, inspect, search, and await; the harness associates calls with provider execution. GraphComplete owns completion identity, authority, current publication, resolution, terminal state, and acceptance.
- The narrow single-root experiment is a compatible slice of this model, but the destination includes recursively scoped completions and must not bake shared mutable draft assumptions into the foundation.

## Decisions so far

- [Focused product contract: #227](https://github.com/vishaltandale00/relayer-graphcomplete/issues/227)
  - `complete()` is the recursive semantic function.
  - Every invocation owns a live current pointer beginning at its interaction node.
  - A successful call returns an agent-determined layer, which becomes its final current.
  - Advances publish an immutable owned closure atomically and preserve graph-native access to prior published work.
  - User view follows an advance only when it is still showing that pointer's previous current.
- Recursive work is represented by interaction nodes and one `invoke` action kind, regardless of whether the trigger is a user or an agent.
- Invoke actions target stable completion identity. They resolve to the live child current while active and the returned final layer after success, even if the parent has already completed.
- Parent completion neither waits for nor settles an active child. Incorporation is a parent-agent judgment, not a graph integrity rule.
- Each `complete(inputGraph)` call has local write authority, a durable current pointer, a terminal result promise, and appropriately scoped graph read/search authority. Parent and child presentations are not mutually writable.
- Existing breadcrumb navigation remains the way to move back up the graph hierarchy; live-current resolution extends the destination semantics rather than adding a new navigation gesture.
- [Graph search #52](https://github.com/vishaltandale00/relayer-graphcomplete/issues/52) is an external dependency and exclusively owns search semantics. This map consumes its authority-aware freshness conformance contract.
- [Graph-addressed communication #224](https://github.com/vishaltandale00/relayer-graphcomplete/issues/224) is intentionally deferred. This map does not decide send, routing, propagation, or subscriptions.

## Frontier issue map

GitHub issue links and the dependency graph below record the resolved design DAG and its next implementation task. Issues #233-#237 are resolved with the #234/#235 corrections; #268 plans agent-invoked `complete(inputGraph)` binding, and #52 rema

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Temporal recursive GraphComplete demo.

Models a small slice of the vishaltandale00/relayer-graphcomplete design
(#228 -> #227): a `complete()` function whose root and child invocations
each expose a durable current pointer, publish coherent working graphs
over time, and resolve to an agent-chosen final layer.

We lift the hand-rolled recursion into native Jac archetypes:

  * Interaction  -- a node representing one `complete()` invocation.
  * Layer        -- a published, immutable working graph (a snapshot).
  * InvokeEdge   -- a typed edge recording the parent->child invoke action.
  * PublishEdge  -- a typed edge from an interaction to each layer it owns.

A walker (`Curator`) advances an interaction's current pointer layer by
layer, publishing an immutable reachable closure at each step, then
resolves to an agent-chosen final layer. A second walker (`Resolver`)
walks the invoke tree bottom-up, letting each child inform the parent's
synthesis -- the temporal-recursive shape the issue complains about.
"""

node Layer {
    has title: str = "";
    has depth: int = 0;
    has finalized: bool = False;
    has note: str = "";
}

node Interaction {
    has label: str = "";
    has role: str = "root";
    has current: str = "";
    has final_layer: str = "";
    has published_count: int = 0;
}

edge InvokeEdge {
    has kind: str = "invoke";
}

edge PublishEdge {
    has step: int = 0;
    has reachable: int = 0;
}

walker Curator {
    has interaction_label: str = "";
    has layer_titles: list[str] = [];
    has chosen_final: str = "";

    can begin with Root entry {
        for i in self.layer_titles {
            target = Interaction(label=self.interaction_label, role="root");
            root +>:InvokeEdge:+> target;
            new_layer = Layer(title=i, depth=0, note=f"step-{i}");
            target +>:PublishEdge(step=0, reachable=1):+> new_layer;
            target.current = i;
            target.published_count = 1;
            print(f"[{target.label}] published '{new_layer.title}' (current -> {target.current})");
        }
        final_choice = self.chosen_final if self.chosen_final else self.layer_titles[-1];
        for child in [->:InvokeEdge:->] {
            if isinstance(child, Interaction) and child.label == self.interaction_label {
                child.final_layer = final_choice;
                for lyr in [->:PublishEdge:->] {
                    if isinstance(lyr, Layer) and lyr.title == final_choice {
                        lyr.finalized = True;
                        print(f"[{child.label}] resolved to final layer '{lyr.title}'");
                    }
                }
            }
        }
