A maintainer on the project elizaOS/eliza (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #22896: [agent] Build a canonical resettable synthetic-world test platform

## Project / Coordination

- Project board: add to the active engineering board during claim
- Parent tracker or epic: this issue
- Related work: #22293 and draft PR #22359 own truthful scenario evidence and provider-qualified canaries; #8801 and #8802 established narrower keyless and user-surface coverage gates
- Suggested lane tag: synthetic-world-epic

## Contribution Provenance

AI assistance is self-reported provenance, not a request for hidden reasoning.

- AI assistance: yes
- AI provider/model: openai / gpt-5.6-sol
- Client / agent tooling: Codex Desktop
- Contribution skill revision: elizaOS/eliza@63bce535e32738ad07e39552ed0cf16e36fa8dd9:packages/skills/skills/contribute-to-eliza
- Attribution status: self-reported

## Problem

elizaOS has strong but fragmented test foundations: a real PGlite-backed scenario runtime, strict deterministic model fixtures, scenario Mockoon and in-process provider fixtures, cloud Hetzner and control-plane mocks, a resettable Cloud Playwright stack, and keyless coverage ratchets. They do not yet form one complete synthetic world in which every plugin action, provider, service, evaluator, event, route, connector, model adapter, scheduler, notification path, and background process can run with production-shaped data and deterministic reset.

Audit findings on 2026-08-20:

- 103 plugin package manifests exist. Static source declares runtime surfaces in 48 action-bearing, 41 provider-bearing, 46 service-bearing, 4 evaluator-bearing, 11 event-bearing, 26 route-bearing, and 5 model-bearing plugins.
- Only 33 plugin packages are referenced by any scenario source. At least 24 packages with declared runtime surfaces have no scenario reference.
- The existing keyless gate covers 28 of 42 action or connector surface plugins and baselines 14 uncovered plugins. It does not inventory providers, services, evaluators, events, or models.
- The broad surface matrix is green for 39 commands and 18 of 22 route plugins, with 4 route exemptions, but it does not prove mock-world executability.
- Scenario mocks and Cloud mocks are separate stacks with separate state, reset, and observability contracts.
- Simulated scenarios explicitly disable the activity tracker, proactive agent, and LifeOps scheduler; tick turns manually invoke only a narrow scheduling seam.
- The scenario schema has strict fixture machinery, but no first-class per-scenario model-fixture manifest. The Cloud mock LLM is fixed or context-echo only.
- The scenario runner has no suite-level live-model 3/3 stability mode. A separate Vitest helper records stochastic tiers and defaults to 2/3 acceptance.
- Provider mock documentation already lists fidelity gaps across pagination, streaming, OAuth refresh, webhook signatures and retries, rate limits, partial failures, media, attachment, lifecycle, and protocol variants.

## Goal

Create one versioned, resettable, observable synthetic world that boots the real elizaOS runtime and production clients against protocol-faithful local services. The same scenario definition must run in two model modes:

1. strict mock-model mode, where every expected model call and best-case response is registered and unmatched or unused fixtures fail;
2. real-model mode over the same mock world, where each scenario runs in three isolated attempts, reports first-pass and 3/3 stability, and flags every result below 3/3 for agent context or product repair.

The platform must run locally, in PR keyless lanes, and in Cloud E2E without external provider credentials. Provider-qualified live evidence remains a separate, fail-closed trust class owned by #22293.

## Child Workstreams

Dependency-aware child issues:

- [ ] #22897 — canonical runtime-surface inventory and coverage ratchet; begins immediately and supplies ownership and gap data to every lane.
- [ ] #22898 — versioned world fixtures, virtual clock, reset, snapshot, faults, and observation ledger; begins immediately after agreeing the minimal inventory schema.
- [ ] #22899

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Synthetic-world test platform: canonical surface inventory with a coverage walker.

Models the audit findings from elizaOS issue #22896 as a small object-spatial
graph. Plugin packages declare which runtime surfaces they expose
(actions, providers, services, evaluators, events, routes, models). Each
package is connected to the surfaces it carries via typed edges. A walker
walks the inventory, computes coverage against a scenario reference set,
flags packages with declared surfaces but no scenario reference, and
reports the gap — the same traversal the issue says is hand-rolled today.
"""

node Surface {
    has name: str = "";
    has kind: str = "";
}

node Package {
    has name: str = "";
    has has_action: bool = False;
    has has_provider: bool = False;
    has has_service: bool = False;
    has has_evaluator: bool = False;
    has has_event: bool = False;
    has has_route: bool = False;
    has has_model: bool = False;
}

node Scenario {
    has name: str = "";

    def declares(name: str) -> bool {
        for s in self.surfaces {
            if s.name == name {
                return True;
            }
        }
        return False;
    }

    def surfaces -> list {
        out: list = [];
        for s in [->:Covers:->] {
            out.append(s);
        }
        return out;
    }
}

edge Covers { }

walker Auditor {
    has covered_count: int = 0;
    has uncovered_packages: list = [];
    has total_packages: int = 0;
    has total_surfaces: int = 0;
    has surface_coverage: dict = {};
    has visited: list = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can inventory with Package entry {
        self.total_packages += 1;
        surfaces_held: list = [];
        if here.has_action {
            surfaces_held.append("action");
        }
        if here.has_provider {
            surfaces_held.append("provider");
        }
        if here.has_service {
            surfaces_held.append("service");
        }
        if here.has_evaluator {
            surfaces_held.append("evaluator");
        }
        if here.has_event {
            surfaces_held.append("event");
        }
        if here.has_route {
            surfaces_held.append("route");
        }
        if here.has_model {
            surfaces_held.append("model");
        }
        if not surfaces_held {
            return;
        }
        scenario = self.pick_scenario();
        if scenario is None {
            gap = Package(name=here.name, has_action=here.has_action,
                          has_provider=here.has_provider,
                          has_service=here.has_service,
                          has_evaluator=here.has_evaluator,
                          has_event=here.has_event,
                          has_route=here.has_route,
                          has_model=here.has_model);
            self.uncovered_packages.append(gap);
            return;
        }
        self.covered_count += 1;
        for kind in surfaces_held {
            current = self.surface_coverage[kind];
            if current is None {
                current = 0;
            }
            self.surface_coverage[kind] = current + 1;
        }
        report {"package": here.name, "scenario": scenario.name, "kinds": surfaces_held};
    }

    can catalog with Surface entry {
        self.total_surfaces += 1;
    }

    can summarize with Scenario entry {
        self.summarize();
        disengage;
    }

    def pick_scenario -> object {
        for s in [-->] {
            if s.__class__ == Scenario {
                return s;
            }
        }
        return None;
    }

    def summarize {
        print("=== Synthetic-world coverage audit ===");
        print(f"packages surveyed : {self.total_packages}");
        print(f"surfaces registered: {self.total_surfaces}");
        print(f"packages covered   : {self.covered_count}");
        gap = len(self.uncovered_packages);
        print(f"packages uncovered : {gap}");
        if gap > 0 {
            print("gap list:");
            for p in self.uncovered_packages {
                print(f"  - {p.name}");
            }
        }
        print("per-kind coverage:");
        for kind in ["action", "provider", "service", "evaluator", "event", "route", "model"] {
            count = self.surface_coverage[kind];
            shown = count if count is not None else 0;
            print(f"  {kind:9s}: {shown}");
        }
    }
