Task:
A maintainer on the project erraggy/oastools (Go) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #488: parser: generated DeepCopy is not cycle safe and overflows the stack

## Summary

The generated deep copy recurses without tracking what it has already copied, so a document whose pointer graph contains a cycle overflows the stack instead of returning a copy. This is true for every type the generator emits, not one of them.

Decoding cannot produce a cycle, since neither JSON nor YAML can express one, so this only reaches documents assembled in Go: the builder, tests, and anything constructing a document programmatically before handing it to another package.

## Reproduction

A schema that contains itself:

```go
s := &parser.Schema{Type: "object", Properties: map[string]*parser.Schema{}}
s.Properties["self"] = s
_ = s.DeepCopy()
```

```
runtime: goroutine stack exceeds 1000000000-byte limit
fatal error: stack overflow
```

The same holds for an encoding nested under itself, and for a media type that reaches one:

```go
e := &parser.Encoding{Encoding: map[string]*parser.Encoding{}}
e.Encoding["self"] = e
_ = e.DeepCopy()

mt := &parser.MediaType{Encoding: map[string]*parser.Encoding{"part": e}}
_ = mt.DeepCopy()
```

## Where it bites

The joiner treats cyclic pointer graphs as possible: `SchemaRewriter.rewriteSchema` and `rewriteEncoding` both keep a visited set precisely so a cycle terminates the traversal. Copying has no equivalent, so a document the rewriter handles fine can still crash the copy.

Reached today from:

- `joiner`, which deep copies a top level entry before rewriting its references (#480), so a cyclic document that also needs a rename crashes where it previously rewrote successfully
- `joiner`, which deep copies a document handed in at two positions (#481)
- anything else calling the generated methods on a Go assembled document

## Expected

`DeepCopy` returns a copy whose cycles are preserved as cycles, or the codebase states that cyclic documents are unsupported and the callers that guard traversals stop implying otherwise.

## Fix direction

The generator would thread a visited map through `DeepCopyInto`, keyed by source pointer and holding the copy already made, so reaching a node twice returns the existing copy rather than recursing. That preserves cycle structure rather than just avoiding the crash, and it matches what the rewriter's visited sets already assume about these graphs.

The cost is one map allocation per top level copy and a lookup per node. Worth measuring on the corpus specs, since deep copy sits on the joiner's rewrite path.

## Notes

`internal/driftguard` reflects over parser structs to catch a new field missing one of its homes. Whatever shape the fix takes, the visited threading is another home a new pointer-bearing field has to reach.

## Environment

- oastools 758dc34, reproduced on go1.26.5 darwin/arm64

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Cycle-safe DeepCopy over a small OAS-like schema/encoding graph.

Models the scenario from oastools/parser issue #488: DeepCopy on a
hand-assembled cyclic document must not blow the stack. The fix is a
visited map keyed by source identity threaded through the copy, so a
cycle reuses the already-made copy instead of recursing forever.

We lift that machinery into native Jac: schema/encoding/media-type are
node archetypes, the contains-relationship is a typed edge, and the
copy walker carries the visited map as typed ``has`` state with one
exit ``report``.
"""

# ---------- nodes ----------

node Schema {
    has name: str = "";
    has type_kind: str = "";
}

node Encoding {
    has name: str = "";
}

node MediaType {
    has name: str = "";
}

# ---------- edges ----------

# Typed "contains" edge. In oastools this is the named sub-field that
# owns the child (e.g. Schema.Properties, Encoding.Encoding,
# MediaType.Encoding). Carries the field name so the demo can show it.
edge Contains {
    has field_name: str = "";
}

# ---------- walker: cycle-safe deep copy ----------

walker DeepCopier {
    # Visited map: original node identity (Python ``id``) -> copy already made.
    # Domain logic preserved verbatim from the issue's fix direction.
    has visited: dict = {};

    # The final deep-copied graph, reported once at end.
    has report: list = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        # Copy every top-level child of root, then aggregate.
        children = [-->];
        for child in children {
            cp = DeepCopier(visited=self.visited);
            cp.spawn(child);
            self.report = self.report + cp.report;
        }
        # Bubble out to root.
        disengage;
    }

    # Union trigger so one ability serves Schema and Encoding alike.
    # Every node type participating in the union declares ``name``;
    # anything deeper than a container is not in the union, so the
    # walker halts at leaves naturally.
    can copy_node with Schema | Encoding | MediaType entry {
        src_id = id(here);

        if self.visited.get(src_id) is not None {
            # Already copied: reuse the copy to preserve the cycle.
            return;
        }

        # Build the fresh copy (same archetype, same field payload).
        if here isinstance Schema {
            new_node = Schema(name=here.name, type_kind=here.type_kind);
        } elif here instanceof Encoding {
            new_node = Encoding(name=here.name);
        } else {
            new_node = MediaType(name=here.name);
        }
        self.visited[src_id] = new_node;

        # Recurse into every typed ``Contains`` child, threading the
        # visited map so cycles terminate.
        kids = [->:Contains:->];
        for k in kids {
            sub = DeepCopier(visited=self.visited);
            sub.spawn(k);
            new_node +>:Contains(field_name=k.field_name):+> sub.report[0];
        }

        self.report = self.report + [new_node];
        disengage;
    }
}

# ---------- cycle-detecting reachability walker ----------

# Mirrors ``SchemaRewriter.rewriteSchema``'s visited set: a graph the
# rewriter handles must also survive a copy. We exercise the same
# assumption here so the demo closes the loop on the issue's note.
walker CycleReach {
    has seen: dict = {};
    has order: list = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [->:Contains:->];
    }

    can walk with Schema | Encoding | MediaType entry {
        nid = id(here);
        if self.seen.get(nid) is not None {
            return;
        }
        self.seen[nid] = True;
        self.order = self.order + [here.name];
        visit [->:Contains:->];
    }
}

# ---------- demo wiring ----------

with entry {
    # Build a schema whose Properties map points back to itself:
    #   s.Properties["self"] = s
    s = Schema(name="root-schema", type_kind="object");
    s +>:Contains(field_name="self"):+> s;

    # An encoding that also points to itself, plus a media type that
    # reaches it (matches the second reproduction in the issue).
    e = Encoding(name="root-encoding");
    e +>:Contains(field_name="self"):+> e;

    mt = MediaType(name="root-media");
    mt +>:Contains(field_name="part"):+> e;

    # One safe (acyclic) branch to confirm ordinary graphs still copy.
    leaf = Schema(name="leaf", type_kind="string");
    parent = Schema(name="parent", type_kind="object");
    parent +>:Contains(field_name="leaf"):+> leaf;

    root ++> s;
    root ++> mt;
    root ++> parent;

    # --- (a) cycle-safe DeepCopy ------------------------------------
    copier = DeepCopier();
    copier.spawn(root);
    print(f"copied {len(copier.report)} top-level roots with "
          f"{len(copier.visited)} nodes in the visited map");

    # --- (b) reachability check under the same assumption -----------
    reach = CycleReach();
    reach.spawn(root);
    print(f"reach order: {reach.order}");

    # --- (c) explicit cycle proof -----------------------------------
    # s -> s means after one step we should land back on s, not recurse.
    one_step = [->:Contains:->];
    print(f"s has {len(one_step)} Contains edges; target name = "
          f"{one_step[0].name if len(one_step) > 0 else 'none'}");
}
```

