Task:
A maintainer on the project kynahyon/envpatch (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #11: envInterpolator: circular reference between variables causes infinite loop

## Bug Report

When using `envInterpolator` with `.env` files that contain circular variable references (e.g., `A=${B}` and `B=${A}`), the interpolation process hangs indefinitely instead of detecting the cycle and throwing a descriptive error.

**Steps to reproduce:**
```typescript
import { envInterpolator } from './src/interpolation';

const env = {
  A: '${B}',
  B: '${A}',
};

// This never resolves — process hangs
const result = envInterpolator.interpolate(env);
```

**Expected behavior:** `interpolate()` should detect the circular dependency and throw an error like `CircularReferenceError: Circular reference detected: A -> B -> A`. A maximum recursion depth guard or a visited-set check during resolution would fix this.

**Environment:**
- Node.js 20.11.0
- envpatch main branch (commit `a3f82c1`)

I looked at `src/interpolation/envInterpolator.ts` and it appears the recursive `resolve()` function has no cycle detection — it just keeps following references until the call stack overflows or the event loop stalls. Happy to submit a PR if the approach (visited set vs. depth limit) is agreed upon.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""envInterpolator cycle detection.

Models the issue: interpolating an environment dictionary whose variables
refer to each other in a cycle (A -> B -> A) must be detected instead of
recursing forever. Variables are node archetypes, ``Depends`` is a typed
edge, and the ``Resolver`` walker performs the depth-first interpolation
with a per-walk visited set so any cycle raises an error instead of
hanging the process.
"""

node Var {
    has name: str = "";
    has raw: str = "";
    has resolved: bool = False;
}

edge Depends {
    has seq: int = 0;
}

walker CircularReferenceError {
    has message: str = "";

    can fail with Var entry {
        print(self.message);
        disengage;
    }
}

walker Resolver {
    has visiting: list[str] = [];
    has cache: dict[str, str] = {};

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can resolve with Var entry {
        # Walk back home so we never re-enter the same node twice in
        # one traversal without recording it on the stack.
        visiting_set = [v for v in self.visiting];
        if here.name in visiting_set {
            cycle = " -> ".join(self.visiting + [here.name]);
            err = CircularReferenceError(message=f"CircularReferenceError: {cycle}");
            err spawn here;
            disengage;
        }
        self.visiting = self.visiting + [here.name];

        # Pull the freshly-computed value off the cache, or walk the
        # dependency edges depth-first.
        cached = self.cache.get(here.name) if here.name in self.cache else None;
        if cached is not None {
            value = cached;
        } else {
            value = here.raw;
            children = [here->:Depends:->];
            for child in children {
                # Recurse into the dependency by spawning a fresh walker
                # rooted at the child; the cached value lands back on
                # ``self.cache`` when the child reports back.
                child_resolver = Resolver(visiting=self.visiting, cache=self.cache);
                child_resolver spawn child;
            }
            value = self.cache.get(here.name) if here.name in self.cache else here.raw;
            self.cache[here.name] = value;
        }

        here.resolved = True;
        self.visiting = self.visiting[:-1] if len(self.visiting) > 0 else [];
        print(f"{here.name} = {value}");
    }
}

with entry {
    a = Var(name="A", raw="\${B}");
    b = Var(name="B", raw="\${A}");
    a +>:Depends:+> b;
    root ++> a;

    resolver = Resolver();
    resolver spawn root;
}
