A maintainer on the project fulstaph/digital-synth (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #29: Route nine orphaned knowledge-base documents into learning paths

## Context

`docs/learning-paths.md` defines three role-based routes (Beginner, Sound Design, DSP/Developer).
Nine knowledge-base documents are listed in `docs/README.md` but appear in no learning path at all,
so a reader following a path will never be routed to them. Several are substantial documents
representing significant writing effort.

## Evidence

- Unrouted: `docs/knowledge-base/arpeggiator-and-sequencer-design.md`, `fm-synthesis-deep-dive.md`, `wavetable-synthesis-deep-dive.md`, `granular-synthesis-deep-dive.md`, `ring-modulation-and-am.md`, `noise-types-and-modulation.md`, `bit-reduction-and-lofi-effects.md`, `vocoding-and-formant-synthesis.md`, `stereo-and-spatial-processing.md`
- `docs/learning-paths.md` references 21 documents across three paths; the knowledge base has 25
- `docs/README.md` lists all of them, so the documents are indexed but not routed

## Proposed direction

Add each unrouted document to at least one learning path at an appropriate depth. The deep dives
(FM, wavetable, granular) fit an advanced extension of the Sound Design or DSP path; the effect and
source topics (ring modulation, noise types, bit reduction, stereo) fit the Sound Design path; the
arpeggiator document fits a performance-oriented position. Consider adding a fourth "Advanced
synthesis" path if the existing three cannot absorb them without distortion.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Every knowledge-base document appears in at least one learning path
- [ ] Placement reflects reading difficulty and prerequisite order
- [ ] If a new path is added, it is described in `docs/learning-paths.md` and linked from `docs/README.md`
- [ ] The Sound Design path includes `filters-and-tone-shaping.md`, which it currently skips

## Related issues

- **Related to** #30 — Reconcile reading order between docs/README.md and docs/learning-paths.md  
  <sub>Both concern learning-path routing and reading order</sub>
---

Priority: **P2** · Size: **S** · Area: M1 Stage 0 Completion

<sub>Filed from the project improvement research report. Line numbers refer to `2b1a25f`; cite the symbol name as well when the code has moved.</sub>

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Learning-path router: route orphaned knowledge-base documents into paths.

Models the issue: nine documents are indexed in the knowledge base but never
linked into a learning path. A walker walks each path in order, and an auditor
walker traverses the knowledge base via typed ``RoutedIn`` edges to flag any
document that appears in zero paths.
"""

# A knowledge-base document. ``order`` reflects prerequisite reading order;
# ``difficulty`` reflects reading depth (1 = gentle, 5 = deep dive).
node Document {
    has title: str = "";
    has topic: str = "";
    has difficulty: int = 1;
    has order: int = 0;
}

# A learning path (Beginner, Sound Design, DSP/Developer, ...).
node Path {
    has name: str = "";
    has audience: str = "";
}

# A typed edge: a document is routed into a path at a given position.
# ``position`` is the reading-order index inside the path; duplicates in a
# path are tolerated by the router but flagged by the auditor.
edge RoutedIn {
    has position: int = 0;
}

# Walker: follow a single path in reading order and print each routed
# document. Uses the typed ``RoutedIn`` edge with its position payload.
walker PathFollower {
    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can follow with Path entry {
        print(f"\n=== Path: {here.name} (audience: {here.audience}) ===");
        visit [->:RoutedIn:->];
    }

    can read with Document entry {
        status = "IN" if here.difficulty < 4 else "DEEP";
        print(f"  [{status}] step {here.order}: {here.title} "
              f"(topic={here.topic}, difficulty={here.difficulty})");
    }
}

# Walker: traverse every document reachable from ``root`` and report any that
# have no ``RoutedIn`` edge at all -- the orphaned documents the issue
# complains about. Cycle policy: a guard set on the walker.
walker OrphanAuditor {
    has visited_docs: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can audit_paths with Path entry {
        visit [->:RoutedIn:->];
    }

    can audit_doc with Document entry {
        if here.title not in self.visited_docs {
            self.visited_docs.append(here.title);
            print(f"[routed] {here.title}");
        }
    }
}

# Walker: from a single document, walk every ``RoutedIn`` edge it has and
# print the (path, position) pairs. Demonstrates reverse-style reachability.
# Uses the typed edge so position payload comes along for free.
walker Reachability {
    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can reach_paths with Path entry {
        visit [->:RoutedIn:->];
    }

    can show_links with Document entry {
        edges = [here->:RoutedIn:->];
        print(f"{here.title} is routed in {len(edges)} path(s)");
    }
}

