Issue #238: Consolidate VR performance settings into one place (DRY, single source of truth)

## Overview

VR stereo-reprojection performance settings are currently split across two tiers: the central G-buffer level control (`VRStereoOptimizations` — "Stereo Reprojection" mode combo in the VR feature panel) and per-effect controls inside individual features (SSS "VR Stereo Sync" + "VR Stereo Reprojection"; SSGI "VR Stereo Reprojection"). Each per-effect toggle is dev-mode-gated and only appears in its own feature's panel. A VR user hunting for perf toggles must visit every feature and enable developer mode. This issue proposes a structural consolidation: keep each toggle owned by its feature, but surface every VR perf toggle in a central VR panel section via a shared registry — one state, multiple views.

## Use Cases

- A VR user finds all stereo-reprojection perf toggles in one central panel without enabling developer mode.
- A user toggles the same setting contextually from the feature's own panel; the central panel reflects the change (single backing state, no drift).
- SSS/SSGI reprojection, now default-on for performance, can be disabled by normal users through a non-debug UI path.

## Principle: one state, multiple views

- Duplicating the *bool* is the anti-pattern (two states that drift).
- Duplicating the *view* is the pattern (two controls bound to one bool).
- Each toggle stays **owned by its feature** (single backing var — already true).
- It surfaces in **both** the feature's own panel (contextual) and a central VR panel section (discoverability), bound to the same state.

## Current implementation

- **G-buffer level (central):** `VRStereoOptimizations` — "Stereo Reprojection" mode combo in the VR feature panel.
- **Per-effect level:** SSS "VR Stereo Sync" + "VR Stereo Reprojection"; SSGI "VR Stereo Reprojection" — each gated behind `IsDeveloperMode()`, each only in its own feature's panel.
- Terminology was already unified in #236/#237 (all "Stereo Reprojection", "Eye 0 (left)/Eye 1 (right)").

## In-codebase precedent

- **`CSEditor` overlay analog:** the weather details window uses a `ShowInOverlay` bool + shared `Draw*()` functions rendered from both the settings menu and the in-world overlay — one widget, one state, two mount points. This issue generalizes that from one widget to a cross-feature registry.
- **Restart-gated settings:** `Util::Settings::BootSnapshot` + `kRestartFields` metadata drive banners + MCP introspection — features expose descriptors, a central renderer consumes them. Mirror that pattern here.

## Design

1. **Descriptor + registry.** Each feature registers its VR-relevant toggles as `{ label, tooltip, bool* state, availability() }`. The central VR panel iterates the registry and renders each; the feature panel renders its own from the same descriptor. No hardcoded per-feature list in the VR panel; checkbox/tooltip/gate rendering written once (`Util::UI::DrawVRToggle(entry)`).
2. **Central VR panel section** in `VRStereoOptimizations` grouping the G-buffer "Stereo Reprojection" mode with the per-effect toggles.
3. **Graduate out of dev-mode.** Toggles go default-on for performance (measured: SSS -37% pipeline cost; SSGI GI-march -37/-43/-46%, up to 0.73ms at Reference), so users need a normal (non-debug) way to turn them off. Remove the `IsDeveloperMode()` gate as part of this.
4. **Default-on flip** for SSS/SSGI reprojection. Keep the debug disocclusion view dev-only.
5. **Mode-combo consolidation (optional, nice-to-have):** SSS's two mutually-exclusive checkboxes (Sync = reconcile both eyes; Reprojection = skip one eye) could become one combo [Off | Sync | Reprojection], matching the global combo shape. Needs a settings migration; can be a second step.

## Out of Scope

- The per-effect reprojection features themselves (#236, #237) — those land first; this is a UX layer on top and must not block them.
- Terminology (done in #236/#237).

## Related

- #229 (shared reprojection initiative), #236, #237

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""VR performance settings: a central panel consumes a per-feature registry
of toggle descriptors. One backing bool per setting, rendered in both the
feature's own panel and the central VR panel — single source of truth,
multiple views."""

# ---------- Domain nodes ----------

node Setting {
    has label: str = "";
    has tooltip: str = "";
    has enabled: bool = False;
    has developer_only: bool = False;
    has owner: str = "";
}

node Panel {
    has name: str = "";
}

# ---------- Relationships ----------

edge Owns {
    # Feature -> Setting: the feature owns the canonical bool.
    has since: str = "v1";
}

edge Mounts {
    # Panel -> Setting: the panel renders the setting (one of the views).
}

edge BelongsTo {
    # Setting -> Panel: the setting's contextual home panel.
}

# ---------- Central registry + renderer ----------

node VRRegistry {
    # The single source of truth for "what VR perf toggles exist".
    # The central VR panel renders all settings reachable via Mounts.
    has panel_name: str = "VR Performance";

    can render with Root entry {
        print(f"=== {self.panel_name} (central panel) ===");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can list_settings with Setting entry {
        gate = " [dev-only]" if here.developer_only else "";
        state = "ON" if here.enabled else "OFF";
        print(f"  [{state}]{gate} {here.label} — {here.tooltip} (owned by {here.owner})");
        # Walker has no built-in cycle guard in jac 0.36.1; explicit policy:
        # central panel renders this setting and stops — no further descent.
    }
}

# A contextual panel — e.g. the SSS or SSGI feature panel that hosts its own toggle(s).
walker FeaturePanel {
    has panel_name: str = "";
    has owner_name: str = "";

    can start with Panel entry {
        print(f"--- {here.name} (contextual panel) ---");
        # From the panel, traverse to the settings mounted on it (its own view).
        visit [here ->:Mounts:->];
    }

    can show_toggle with Setting entry {
        gate = " [dev-only]" if here.developer_only else "";
        state = "ON" if here.enabled else "OFF";
        print(f"  [{state}]{gate} {here.label} — {here.tooltip}");
        visit [-->];
    }
}

# ---------- Demo wiring ----------

with entry {
    # Settings (canonical state lives here, one bool per setting).
    sss_sync = Setting(
        label="SSS VR Stereo Sync",
        tooltip="Reconcile both eyes for subsurface scattering",
        enabled=True,
        developer_only=False,
        owner="SSS"
    );
    sss_reproj = Setting(
        label="SSS VR Stereo Reprojection",
        tooltip="Skip one eye for SSS",
        enabled=True,
        developer_only=False,
        owner="SSS"
    );
    ssgi_reproj = Setting(
        label="SSGI VR Stereo Reprojection",
        tooltip="Skip one eye for SSGI",
        enabled=True,
        developer_only=False,
        owner="SSGI"
    );
    debug_disocc = Setting(
        label="Disocclusion debug view",
        tooltip="Developer-only visualizer",
        enabled=False,
        developer_only=True,
        owner="Core"
    );

    # Feature panels (the contextual view: feature panel mounts its own settings).
    sss_panel = Panel(name="SSS Feature Panel");
    ssgi_panel = Panel(name="SSGI Feature Panel");
    debug_panel = Panel(name="Debug Panel");

    # Central VR panel (the discoverability view: mounts everything).
    central = VRRegistry(panel_name="VR Performance");

    # Ownership: feature owns its setting (typed edge).
    sss_panel +>:Owns(since="pre-consolidation"):+> sss_sync;
    sss_panel +>:Owns:+> sss_reproj;
    ssgi_panel +>:Owns:+> ssgi_reproj;
    debug_panel +>:Owns:+> debug_disocc;

    # Mounts: each panel renders its own settings (contextual view).
    sss_panel +>:Mounts:+> sss_sync;
    sss_panel +>:Mounts:+> sss_reproj;
    ssgi_panel +>:Mounts:+> ssgi_reproj;
    debug_panel +>:Mounts:+> debug_disocc;

    # The central VR registry mounts ALL VR-relevant settings (central view).
    central +>:Mounts:+> sss_sync;
    central +>:Mounts:+> sss_reproj;
    central +>:Mounts:+> ssgi_reproj;
    # Debug disocclusion stays dev-only — central panel still lists it
    # but tags it so normal users can ignore / hide it.

    # Setting -> its contextual panel (for cross-reference).
    sss_sync +>:BelongsTo:+> sss_panel;
    sss_reproj +>:BelongsTo:+> sss_panel;
    ssgi_reproj +>:BelongsTo:+> ssgi_panel;
    debug_disocc +>:BelongsTo:+> debug_panel;

    # ---- Demonstrate: central panel renders the registry ----
    root spawn central;

    # ---- Demonstrate: each feature panel renders its own toggles ----
    sss_panel spawn FeaturePanel(panel_name="SSS Feature Panel", owner_name="SSS");
    ssgi_panel spawn FeaturePanel(panel_name="SSGI Feature Panel", owner_name="SSGI");
    debug_panel spawn FeaturePanel(panel_name="Debug Panel", owner_name="Core");

    # ---- Demonstrate: flipping the backing bool in one place updates both views ----
    print("\n--- User toggles SSS Reprojection OFF in the central panel ---");
    sss_reproj.enabled = False;
    central spawn VRRegistry(panel_name="VR Performance");
}
