Issue #918: [Phase 18] Expose only Approved Data View facts and bounded presentation logic

<!-- phase18-publisher:5eaa8f1f-b05a-42a2-a075-68cdca5c4bcf:45f49b24a9d77eda3ca392f9:T11 -->

## Parent

#907 — the Phase 18 Receipt & PDF Template System epic.

## Why this exists (domain context)

The editor needs useful donor, gift, fund, missionary, issuer, and statement information, but a generic merge-field or object-path system would let templates discover private fields, traverse relationships, choose the wrong related record, calculate protected truth, or leak facts across tenants and audiences. The prototype variable registry and browser `dataContext` prove the hazard: they accept path-oriented data and include filtering/calculation concepts that are incompatible with the ratified source-ownership boundary.

This slice makes one immutable Approved Data View the only bridge between source systems, authoring, and generation. It gives an authorized tenant data administrator a simple **Available in documents** switch for policy-eligible native or custom fields, gives authors a human-named picker with synthetic examples, and lets a registered source resolver assemble the exact immutable Facts Package that #914 admits. Availability can only narrow existing Phase 11 and purpose policy; it can never create a new permission matrix or make a forbidden field discoverable.

The result supports broad everyday usefulness through one root subject, finite named roles, source-bounded ordered collections, and six sentence-shaped optional-content predicates. It deliberately excludes arbitrary CRM traversal, queries, calculations, custom expressions, and template-owned collection logic.

## What to build

1. Define a versioned Approved Data View contract for each document purpose. It names one root subject, finite source-owned roles with explicit cardinality, typed stable semantic fields, source-owned summaries, bounded ordered collections, approved formatters, privacy/audience rules, and exact null/absent/withheld/empty/overflow behavior.
2. Reuse the Phase 11 field identity, value type, classification, custom-field identity, and egress eligibility authority. Bind authored nodes to stable semantic ids, never display labels, SQL columns, table names, JSON/GraphQL paths, provider ids, or current storage locations. A field rename may change its human label without changing the binding.
3. Add one tenant data-admin surface, reached from the existing data/field governance area rather than hidden inside every template, for **Available in documents**. Show only fields already eligible for the exact tenant and supported purpose/audience classes. Turning availability off narrows future use and invalidates affected current proof; turning it on does not waive purpose, recipient, privacy, restricted-person, or actor policy.
4. Add the normal author picker used by #917. It groups human-named fields, roles, summaries, and collections by purpose, provides a synthetic example and concise meaning/source, supports search over permitted display metadata, and omits existence-sensitive forbidden fields entirely. Forbidden items must not appear as disabled rows, counts, zero-result hints, search suggestions, error text, telemetry, import/export findings, or hidden response metadata.
5. Support finite purpose-owned named roles such as donor, legal recipient, tribute honoree, issuer, or permitted missionary projection only when the source contract declares them. Each role states `exactly_one`, `zero_or_one`, or bounded `many` cardinality. If an `exactly_one` role resolves to zero or multiple candidates, return a typed source-owned missing/ambiguity result; never pick the first row or ask a template to decide.
6. Support collections only as source-owned immutable arrays with stable row identity, deterministic order, maximum item/byte limits, and explicit overflow behavior. The template may select permitted columns and presentation but cannot filter, sort, group, join, deduplicate, aggregate, calculate, change membership/order, nest another collection, or silen

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Approved Data View: an immutable Facts Package assembled by a walker.

This demo models the Phase 18 contract natively in Jac. The hand-rolled
machinery the issue complains about — generic merge-field paths, arbitrary
CRM traversal, template-side filtering, and cascade invalidation across
tenants — is replaced by typed edges, role cardinality, and a walker that
walks one source-resolved subject graph and emits an immutable Facts
Package. Forbidden fields never appear at any layer; per-role ambiguity
is a typed result, never a guess.
"""

# ---------- Node archetypes ----------

node Subject {
    has subject_id: str = "";
    has display_name: str = "";
}

# A native policy-eligible field. Field identity is a stable semantic id,
# never a display label or storage path. A field may be marked not-eligible
# for documents; such fields must never be discoverable by the picker or
# leaked into the Facts Package.
node Field {
    has semantic_id: str = "";
    has label: str = "";
    has value_type: str = "";
    has eligible: bool = True;
}

# A finite source-owned role (donor, legal recipient, tribute honoree,
# issuer, permitted missionary projection). Each role declares its
# cardinality against a subject: exactly_one, zero_or_one, or bounded many.
node Role {
    has role_name: str = "";
    has cardinality: str = "zero_or_one";
    has bound: int = 1;
}

# A bounded ordered collection owned by the source (not the template).
# It carries a deterministic order index, a maximum item/byte limit,
# and an explicit overflow result the template may render.
node Collection {
    has collection_name: str = "";
    has max_items: int = 100;
    has overflowed: bool = False;
    has items: list[str] = [];
}

# A single row inside a Collection. Stable row identity is preserved
# by an immutable index into the source-owned ordered list.
node CollectionRow {
    has row_index: int = 0;
    has label: str = "";
}

# ---------- Typed edges ----------

# A role binding from a subject to a field. The edge carries the
# role's cardinality so the resolver never has to re-query.
edge RoleBinding { }

# Membership of a row in a source-owned ordered collection.
edge RowIn { }

# Subject that owns a collection (source ownership, not template ownership).
edge Owns { }

# ---------- Walker: the Source Resolver ----------

# The resolver walks one subject graph and emits one immutable Facts
# Package. Cycle policy is explicit: a guard set prevents revisits
# (the source graph must be a tree of bounded fan-out, but the walker
# enforces the guard regardless).
walker SourceResolver {
    has visited: list[str] = [];
    has eligible_field_count: int = 0;
    has hidden_field_count: int = 0;
    has role_ambiguities: list[str] = [];
    has collection_overflows: list[str] = [];

    # Record a visit; return True if this is the first time we see it.
    def mark_visited(id: str) -> bool {
        for v in self.visited {
            if v == id {
                return False;
            }
        }
        self.visited.append(id);
        return True;
    }

    can start with Root entry {
        # Enter the single root subject the contract names.
        visit [-->];
    }

    can resolve_subject with Subject entry {
        print(f"[resolver] subject={here.subject_id} name={here.display_name}");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can resolve_field with Field entry {
        if not self.mark_visited(here.semantic_id) {
            # Cycle: a field references itself or a parent. Domain rule:
            # an Approved Data View never recurses into a field twice.
            return;
        }
        # Forbidden fields must not appear anywhere — not as disabled rows,
        # not in counts, not in search. The picker omitted them entirely,
        # so the resolver simply does not see them on the traversal.
        if not here.eligible {
            self.hidden_field_count += 1;
            return;
        }
        self.eligible_field_count += 1;
        print(f"  field {here.semantic_id} ({here.value_type}) label=\"{here.label}\"");
        visit [-->];
    }

    # Union trigger: roles and collections are visited as siblings of the
    # subject; one ability handles both because both flow into the same
    # immutable Facts Package.
    can resolve_role_or_collection with Role | Collection | CollectionRow entry {
        if here is Role {
            cardinality = here.cardinality;
            count = 0;
            for binding in [->:RoleBinding:->] {
                count += 1;
            }
            # Cardinality violation: exactly_one resolving to 0 or many
            # is a typed source-owned ambiguity, never a guess.
            if cardinality == "exactly_one" and count != 1 {
                msg = f"role={here.role_name} expected=1 got={count}";
                self.role_ambiguities.append(msg);
                print(f"  [ambiguity] {msg}");
            } else {
                print(f"  role {here.role_name} cardinality={cardinality} bound={here.bound}");
            }
        } elif here is Collection {
            n = len(here.items);
            overflow = n > here.max_items;
            if overflow {
                here.overflowed = True;
                self.collection_overflows.append(here.collection_name);
            }
            print(f"  collection {here.collection_name} items={n} max={here.max_items} "
                  f"overflow={here.overflowed}");
        } else {
            # CollectionRow: rows are deterministic by index; the template
            # may select permitted columns but cannot reorder or filter.
            print(f"    row[{here.row_index}] {here.label}");
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

# ---------- Walker: the Admin Toggle (turn availability off) ----------

# Turning availability off for a field narrows future use and invalidates
# the affected current Facts Package. The walker walks every Field and
# toggles eligibility according to a target set of semantic ids.
walker ToggleAvailability {
    has turn_off: list[str] = [];
    has invalidated_ids: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can toggle with Field entry {
        if not here.eligible {
            return;
        }
        for sid in self.turn_off {
            if sid == here.semantic_id {
                here.eligible = False;
                self.invalidated_ids.append(sid);
                print(f"[admin] turned off {sid} (label=\"{here.label}\"); "
                      f"current Facts Package invalidated");
                break;
            }
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

# ---------- Demonstration wiring ----------

with entry {
    # One root subject the contract names.
    donor = Subject(subject_id="donor-1", display_name="A. Donor");

    # Eligible fields (policy-eligible, will be in the Facts Package).
    name_field = Field(semantic_id="donor.name", label="Donor Name", value_type="string");
    addr_field = Field(semantic_id="donor.address", label="Address", value_type="string");
    gift_field = Field(semantic_id="donor.gift_total", label="Lifetime Giving", value_type="money");

    # A forbidden field: the picker omits it entirely, so it never
    # appears as a disabled row, count, or search suggestion.
    secret_field = Field(
        semantic_id="donor.internal_score",
        label="Internal Score",
        value_type="number",
        eligible=False
    );

    # Roles with explicit cardinality.
    legal_role = Role(role_name="legal_recipient", cardinality="exactly_one");
    tribute_role = Role(role_name="tribute_honoree", cardinality="zero_or_one");

    # An exactly_one role with zero candidates -> typed missing result.
    # The resolver reports it; the template never has to decide.
    donor ++> legal_role;
    donor ++> tribute_role;
    legal_role +>:RoleBinding:+> name_field;
    legal_role +>:RoleBinding:+> addr_field;

    # Source-owned ordered collection with stable row identity.
    gifts = Collection(collection_name="gifts", max_items=2, items=["2019", "2020", "2021"]);
    donor +>:Owns:+> gifts;
    gifts +>:RowIn:+> CollectionRow(row_index=0, label="2019 gift");
    gifts +>:RowIn:+> CollectionRow(row_index=1, label="2020 gift");
    gifts +>:RowIn:+> CollectionRow(row_index=2, label="2021 gift");

    # Wire the donor and its fields onto the graph.
    root ++> donor;
    root ++> name_field;
    root ++> addr_field;
    root ++> gift_field;
    root ++> secret_field;

    # 1) Build the immutable Facts Package via the resolver walker.
    print("--- resolve Approved Data View ---");
    root spawn SourceResolver();

    # 2) Admin turns off address availability; affected package invalidates.
    print("--- admin toggles availability ---");
    root spawn ToggleAvailability(turn_off=["donor.address"]);

    # 3) Re-resolve: hidden field count rises, eligible field count drops,
    #    and the resolver never reveals the forbidden internal_score.
    print("--- resolve after invalidation ---");
    root spawn SourceResolver();
}
```

