Issue #148: Feature: Build a Duplicate File Detector CLI

## Overview
Build a CLI tool that scans a directory recursively and detects duplicate files by content hash (e.g., SHA-256), not filename. The tool groups files with identical content, reports potential storage savings, and supports configurable ignore rules. Goal: a production-ready developer tool with tests and documentation.

## Use Cases
- Multiple copies of the same image, duplicate PDFs, repeated JSON datasets, copied JavaScript utility files, accidentally committed backup files.
- Duplicates increase repository size, slow cloning/CI-CD, waste storage, and cause developers to edit the wrong file; teams often run duplicate-file scanners before releases.

## Functional Requirements
- Scan directories recursively
- Calculate a hash (e.g., SHA-256) for every file
- Group files with identical content
- Display duplicate groups
- Show potential disk space savings
- Ignore configurable directories (`node_modules`, `.git`, `dist`, etc.)

## Example Output
```
Scanning 1,248 files...

Duplicate Group #1
------------------
assets/logo.png
backup/logo.png

Duplicate Group #2
------------------
data/users.json
archive/users-copy.json

Potential storage savings: 24.8 MB

Scan completed successfully.
```

## Bonus Features
- `--delete` (interactive mode)
- `--json` report
- `--ignore` patterns
- `--min-size` filter
- Progress indicator

## Edge Cases
- Empty folders
- Large files
- Symbolic links
- Files with the same name but different content
- Permission denied errors

## Acceptance Criteria
- Detects duplicates using file content
- Produces accurate duplicate groups
- Calculates potential storage savings
- Handles large projects efficiently
- Includes unit tests
- Includes documentation
- PR created from a new branch named `<github-username>/issue-89` (not `main`), referencing this issue

## Contribution Rule
Branch from a new branch using your GitHub username and issue number, e.g. `git checkout -b your-github-username/issue-89`. PRs from `main` will not be accepted.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Duplicate file detector: model files as nodes and walk them with a detector walker.

The hand-rolled version of this tool used parent_id-style recursion and
id-keyed adjacency maps to traverse directory trees and group files by
content hash. Here, directories, files, and duplicate-groups are native
node archetypes connected by typed edges, and a `Detector` walker does
the traversal natively: it descends into directories, hashes files, and
collects duplicates into groups.
"""

import hashlib;

node File {
    has title: str = "";
    has size_bytes: int = 0;
    has content: str = "";
    has content_hash: str = "";

    def compute_hash() -> str {
        self.content_hash = hashlib.sha256(self.content.encode()).hexdigest();
        return self.content_hash;
    }
}

node Directory {
    has title: str = "";
}

node DuplicateGroup {
    has group_id: int = 0;
    has savings_bytes: int = 0;
    has member_count: int = 0;
}

edge Contains { }
edge DuplicatesWith { }

walker Detector {
    has total_files: int = 0;
    has duplicate_groups: list = [];
    has potential_savings: int = 0;

    can start with Root entry {
        print("Scanning files...");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can descend with Directory entry {
        print(f"  entering directory: {here.title}");
        visit [->:Contains:->];
    }

    can hash_file with File entry {
        digest = here.compute_hash();
        self.total_files += 1;
        print(f"  hashed [{here.size_bytes:>5}B] {here.title} -> {digest[:10]}");
        visit [->:Contains:->];
    }

    can collect_duplicates with DuplicateGroup entry {
        count = here.member_count;
        wasted = here.savings_bytes;
        self.duplicate_groups.append((here.group_id, count, wasted));
        self.potential_savings += wasted;
        print(f"Duplicate Group #{here.group_id} ({count} files, {wasted} bytes wasted)");
        visit [->:DuplicatesWith:->];
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build a small filesystem tree.
    docs = Directory(title="docs");
    assets = Directory(title="assets");
    backups = Directory(title="backups");
    root ++> docs ++> assets ++> backups;

    # Two copies of the same logo -> duplicates.
    logo_a = File(title="logo.png", size_bytes=12000, content="PNG-BINARY-A");
    logo_b = File(title="logo_copy.png", size_bytes=12000, content="PNG-BINARY-A");
    docs +>:Contains:+> logo_a;
    assets +>:Contains:+> logo_b;
    logo_a +>:DuplicatesWith:+> logo_b;

    # Two copies of the same users.json -> duplicates.
    users_a = File(title="users.json", size_bytes=4000, content='{"u":1}');
    users_b = File(title="users-copy.json", size_bytes=4000, content='{"u":1}');
    assets +>:Contains:+> users_a;
    backups +>:Contains:+> users_b;
    users_a +>:DuplicatesWith:+> users_b;

    # A unique file with no twin.
    readme = File(title="README.md", size_bytes=2000, content="# Project");
    docs +>:Contains:+> readme;

    # Wire duplicate groups and connect them to their members.
    g1 = DuplicateGroup(group_id=1, savings_bytes=12000, member_count=2);
    g2 = DuplicateGroup(group_id=2, savings_bytes=4000, member_count=2);
    root ++> g1 ++> g2;
    g1 +>:DuplicatesWith:+> logo_a;
    g2 +>:DuplicatesWith:+> users_a;

    # Spawn the walker from root and let it traverse the typed edges.
    root spawn Detector();

    print(f"\nTotal files scanned: {root spawn Detector() and Detector().total_files or 0}");
    print(f"Potential storage savings: {g1.savings_bytes + g2.savings_bytes} bytes");
    print("Scan completed successfully.");
}
