### Initialization and frozen facts

1. Initialize from either one fully validated #7322 prior graph or an explicit cold reason, a bounded set of typed current roots, and one frozen current-direct-input resolver/table. A malformed/incompatible prior graph is rejected by #7322 and becomes a typed cold start; no partially decoded node is trusted.
2. Canonicalize root requests by full typed root/query identity. Exact duplicates collapse; conflicting duplicate root names fail. The current-input resolver returns one exact typed identity, `Missing`, or a stable error for each logical direct-input key and must return the same answer on repeats. A conflicting repeat aborts planning as a stale resolver.
3. Use prior graph adjacency/reverse-edge/topology APIs and compact planner-owned state indexed by validated node identity. No source path, pointer, live AST/type, short digest, or map iteration order becomes scheduling identity.

### Prior-candidate validation

4. To validate a prior candidate, compare every non-child trace edge with the frozen current fact of the same typed key. Resolve prior child-result edges first in deterministic structural order. A candidate becomes reusable only if every direct fact matches and every child resolves successfully to the exact semantic output identity recorded on its edge.
