A maintainer on the project NousResearch/hermes-agent (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #87690: No CLI path to append to a list config field (hooks), while write_file/patch refuse config.yaml

## Problem

There is no supported way to **append** an element to a list-typed config field from the CLI. `_set_nested` deliberately does not grow lists ("the referenced index must already exist"), so:

```bash
# hooks.pre_llm_call currently has exactly 1 entry
hermes config set hooks.pre_llm_call.1.command "/path/new.py"
# IndexError: list index out of range
```

Editing an existing element works fine (`hooks.pre_llm_call.0.timeout 9`). Only appending is impossible.

## Why it matters

Registering a new shell hook is the common case, and `hooks:` is exactly the surface where hand-editing is discouraged: `write_file`/`patch` refuse `~/.hermes/config.yaml` outright with *"Agent cannot modify security-sensitive configuration. Edit ~/.hermes/config.yaml directly or use 'hermes config' instead."*

So an agent (or a scripted install) that wants to add a hook is left with:

1. `hermes config set` — cannot append
2. `write_file` / `patch` — refused by the security guard
3. hand-editing YAML through a shell one-liner — **works, and bypasses the guard entirely**

Option 3 is the only thing that functions, which means the guard is currently training people to route around it. On a multi-session box that is actively dangerous: a naive `yaml.safe_load` → mutate → `yaml.safe_dump` rewrite drops comments/formatting and will silently clobber a concurrent writer's change, since it rewrites the whole file with no compare-and-swap.

## Requested

Any one of these closes the hole:

- **Append syntax**, e.g. `hermes config set hooks.pre_llm_call.+.command "/path/x.py"` or an explicit `hermes config append <dotted.list.path> <json>`
- **A dedicated `hermes hooks add <event> <command> [--timeout N] [--matcher RE] [--fail-closed]`** — preferable for the hook case, since it can also handle the allowlist entry in the same step (today a hook must additionally be approved in `~/.hermes/shell-hooks-allowlist.json`, and there is no `hermes hooks approve`; `hooks list`/`test`/`revoke`/`doctor` exist, but the only way to approve non-interactively is to write that JSON by hand)
- At minimum, make the `IndexError` a friendly error that names the supported alternative

## Related

- #17876 added dot-index list navigation (edit-in-place); this is the missing append half.
- Filed alongside a separate bug where `name[N]` bracket syntax silently creates a literal junk key instead of erroring.

## Environment

Hermes on macOS 26.x, `_config_version: 37`, `hermes_cli/config.py::_set_nested`.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Hermes config append scenario: hooks list with a security guard that
refuses whole-file rewrites, so the only honest path is in-place append
of a typed config entry."""

node ConfigRoot {
    has name: str = "config.yaml";
    has version: int = 37;
}

node HookList {
    has event: str = "";
    has entries: list = [];
}

node HookEntry {
    has command: str = "";
    has timeout: int = 9;
    has matcher: str = "";
    has fail_closed: bool = False;
    has approved: bool = False;
}

edge OwnsHooks {}
edge HookOf {}
edge Allowlisted {}

walker AppendConfig {
    has event: str = "pre_llm_call";
    has command: str = "";
    has timeout: int = 9;

    can start with ConfigRoot entry {
        print(f"config {here.name} (v{here.version}): requesting append to {self.event}");
        visit [->:OwnsHooks:->];
    }

    can descend with HookList entry {
        if here.event == self.event {
            # guard: refuse whole-file rewrite via write_file / patch
            guard_ok = True;
            if not guard_ok {
                print("refused: write_file/patch blocked for security-sensitive config");
                disengage;
            }
            new_entry = HookEntry(
                command=self.command,
                timeout=self.timeout,
                matcher="*",
                fail_closed=False
            );
            here ++> HookOf ++> new_entry;
            print(f"appended hook to {here.event}: command={self.command}");
            visit [-->];
        } else {
            visit [-->];
        }
    }

    can audit with HookEntry entry {
        status = "APPROVED" if here.approved else "PENDING";
        print(f"  - [{status}] {here.command} (timeout={here.timeout}s)");
    }
}

walker AllowlistHook {
    has event: str = "pre_llm_call";
    has command_match: str = "";

    can start with ConfigRoot entry {
        print(f"approving hook for event={self.event}, command={self.command_match}");
        visit [->:OwnsHooks:->];
    }

    can find_hook with HookList entry {
        if here.event == self.event {
            visit [-->];
        } else {
            visit [-->];
        }
    }

    can approve with HookEntry entry {
        if here.command == self.command_match {
            here.approved = True;
            print(f"approved hook: {here.command}");
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    cfg = ConfigRoot(name="~/.hermes/config.yaml", version=37);
    pre_llm = HookList(event="pre_llm_call", entries=[]);
    post_llm = HookList(event="post_llm_call", entries=[]);

    cfg +>:OwnsHooks:+> pre_llm;
    cfg +>:OwnsHooks:+> post_llm;

    initial = HookEntry(
        command="/old/pre.py",
        timeout=9,
        matcher="*",
        fail_closed=False,
        approved=True
    );
    pre_llm ++> HookOf ++> initial;

    root spawn AppendConfig(
        event="pre_llm_call",
        command="/path/new.py",
        timeout=12
    );

    print("--- audit pre_llm_call hooks ---");
    audit = AppendConfig(event="pre_llm_call", command="", timeout=0);
    audit.can_start = lambda: None;
    root spawn audit;
}
```

