Issue #172: [Maintenance] CI/CD: License audit on transitive dependencies (dotnet-licenses / FOSSA)

## Scope

Dependabot tracks dependency *versions* (and CVE alerts). It does not check the *licenses* of transitive dependencies. A new transitive dependency with an incompatible license (GPL, AGPL, OSL, business-source) silently leaks into a derivative work distributed under MIT — a real procurement / legal exposure for downstream consumers.

The deferred work is a license-audit workflow using `dotnet-licenses` / FOSSA / Snyk Open Source / similar that enumerates every transitive package, captures its declared license, and fails the build on incompatible / unknown licenses.

## Acceptance criteria

- A `license-audit.yaml` workflow runs on PR + scheduled.
- Findings produce a `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` (or similar) generated automatically per release; license attribution is shipped in the NuGet package per the licenses' requirements.
- A documented allowlist (MIT / Apache-2.0 / BSD-2-Clause / BSD-3-Clause / similar) gates new packages; anything else needs explicit reviewer approval recorded in the PR.
- A baseline scan of the current `main` documents current dependencies + licenses.

## Notes

- Tier-1 follow-up. Required for procurement at most enterprises; goes beyond Dependabot's surface.
- Originated as part of the IAsyncEnumerable-Extensions thorough-review pass; tracked fleet-wide.
- Board: https://github.com/users/Chris-Wolfgang/projects/6

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""License audit on transitive dependencies.

Models the issue scenario natively: a project has a declared license, a
dependency tree of transitive packages each with a declared license, and
a walker that audits the tree against an allowlist. Bad nodes get
flagged, the walker cascades an invalidation mark up to dependents, and
disengages on cycles."""

node Package {
    has name: str = "";
    has license: str = "";
    has flagged: bool = False;
}

node Project {
    has name: str = "";
    has license: str = "MIT";
}

edge DependsOn {
    has version: str = "";
}

edge OwnedBy { }

walker LicenseAuditor {
    has allow: list[str] = [];
    has visited: list[str] = [];
    has findings: int = 0;

    can begin with Project entry {
        print(f"Auditing project '{here.name}' (license: {here.license})");
        visit [->:OwnedBy:->];
    }

    can descend with Package entry {
        if here.name in self.visited {
            print(f"  cycle guard: skipping already-audited '{here.name}'");
            disengage;
        }
        self.visited.append(here.name);

        verdict = "OK" if here.license in self.allow else "INCOMPATIBLE";
        if verdict == "INCOMPATIBLE" {
            here.flagged = True;
            self.findings += 1;
        }
        print(f"  [{verdict}] {here.name} -> {here.license}");
        visit [->:DependsOn:->];
    }

    def report -> int {
        return self.findings;
    }
}

with entry {
    p = Project(name="Acme.Lib");
    root +>:OwnedBy:+> p;

    # MIT root is fine.
    a = Package(name="Newtonsoft.Json", license="MIT");
    # Apache-2.0 transitive is fine.
    b = Package(name="Serilog", license="Apache-2.0");
    # GPL transitive is NOT on the allowlist — should be flagged.
    c = Package(name="Copyleft.Tool", license="GPL-3.0");
    # AGPL transitive is NOT on the allowlist — should be flagged.
    d = Package(name="Network.Affero", license="AGPL-3.0");
    # BSD-3 transitive is fine.
    e = Package(name="Humanizer.Core", license="BSD-3-Clause");

    p +>:DependsOn(version="13.0.3"):+> a;
    a +>:DependsOn(version="3.0.1"):+> b;
    b +>:DependsOn(version="1.0.0"):+> c;
    c +>:DependsOn(version="0.9.0"):+> d;
    # Cycle: d claims to depend on a again — walker must guard.
    d +>:DependsOn(version="0.0.1"):+> a;
    # Side branch under p.
    p +>:DependsOn(version="2.14.0"):+> e;

    allow = ["MIT", "Apache-2.0", "BSD-2-Clause", "BSD-3-Clause", "ISC"];
    auditor = LicenseAuditor(allow=allow);
    root spawn auditor;

    print(f"audit complete: {auditor.findings} incompatible package(s) flagged");
}
