Task:
A maintainer on the project nijinomichi/BananaMoon-QuantumTrust-Review (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #18: Incident Record: Markdown Content Accidentally Materialized as Git Tree Path (2025-10-23)

## Incident Summary

**Title:** Markdown Content Accidentally Materialized as Git Tree Path  
**日本語タイトル:** Markdown本文がGitパスへ実体化した事例  
**Discovery date:** 2026-07-19  
**Incident date:** 2025-10-23  
**Commit:** to be confirmed from tree containing anomalous path  
**Severity:** Repository integrity / CI reproducibility

---

## Provenance (Reconstructed Sequence)

```
2025-10-23
    │
    ▼
GitHub Web UI (web-flow)
    │
    ▼
Create new file
    │
    ▼
Filename欄へ README / Notion本文が貼り付く
    │
    ▼
"/" を Git がパス区切りと解釈
    │
    ▼
巨大ディレクトリ生成
    │
    ▼
Blob 追加
    │
    ▼
Git tree へ永続保存
```

This sequence is consistent with all commit, tree, and patch data retrieved during the investigation.

---

## Key Evidence

The most decisive finding was the **filename field containing Markdown body text**:

```
filename:
# BananaMoon Quantum NFT...
...
### Title
...
### Subtitle
...
```

The filename contains `### Title`, `### Subtitle`, `### Overview` — these are **Markdown headings, not a file name**.

This means:

```
Content  →  Path
README   →  filename
```

Markdown body text was entered into the **path field** rather than the content field. Git interpreted every `/` as a directory separator, materializing the document structure as a real directory tree in the repository.

---

## Impact

- An abnormally long, structured path was registered in the Git object store
- The path contains what appears to be a full Markdown document as its filename
- `actions/checkout` encountered this path during CI runs, causing failures
- The anomalous tree entry persists in Git history and cannot be removed without history rewriting

---

## Classification of Claims

### Verified facts
- The anomalous path exists in the Git tree
- The path structure matches Markdown heading syntax (`# ...`, `### ...`)
- The commit was made via GitHub Web UI (web-flow committer)
- `actions/checkout` failed on affected branches
- The sequence above is consistent with all retrieved API data

### Reasonable hypotheses
- The content was pasted into the filename field of the GitHub Web UI "Create new file" dialog
- The source material was a README or Notion document
- The paste occurred during a routine file creation operation

### Unsupported speculation (explicitly excluded)
- Whether a human or a tool performed the paste
- Whether the action was intentional or accidental
- The exact source document

GitHub API data alone cannot distinguish human paste from automated tool input.

---

## Why This Is Noteworthy

This is an unusual case study for several reasons:

1. **Git allows arbitrarily long paths** — there is no filename length validation at the object store level
2. **GitHub Web UI does not prevent** entering multiline or slash-containing content into the filename field
3. **The resulting tree is valid Git** — it can be cloned on most systems but breaks tools with path-length limits (Windows, some CI runners)
4. **Provenance archaeology was required** to identify the root cause — standard CI logs alone were insufficient

---

## Research Boundary

This issue marks the **end of the investigation phase**.

The question *"why did actions/checkout fail?"* has been answered with sufficient evidence:

- An abnormally long, Markdown-structured path exists in the Git tree
- That path originates from Markdown body text entered as a filename
- That path caused `actions/checkout` to fail

Further byte-level or UTF-8 analysis is not required.

---

## Next Steps (Separate Issue)

Repair strategy is tracked separately to keep diagnosis and remediation distinct.

See: *(to be linked once remediation issue is opened)*

Repair must be:
- non-destructive to provenance records
- reviewed before execution
- documented with before/after SHA evidence

---

## References

- Investigation performed: 2026-07-19
- Repository: `nijinomichi/BananaMoon-QuantumTrust-Review`
- Related PRs: #6, #16, #17
- Diagnosis co-authored with GitHub Copilot (Perplexity / Claude Sonnet 4.6)

---

*This issue is a research record. It does not request immediate action.*  
*Preservation of this record takes priority over any cleanup.*

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""BananaMoon-QuantumTrust-Review incident model.

Models the scenario where Markdown body text was accidentally entered
as a Git path: a GitTree holds TreeEntry nodes whose name fields are
Markdown fragments (with embedded '/' separators and '#' headings),
which Git interprets as a deep directory tree.

A walker walks the tree and reports the suspicious paths so the
operator can decide which ones to invalidate without rewriting history.
"""

node TreeEntry {
    has name: str = "";
    has is_blob: bool = False;
    has is_markdown_text: bool = False;
    has body_preview: str = "";
}

node GitTree {
    has label: str = "";
}

edge Child {
    has depth: int = 0;
}

walker PathAuditor {
    has suspicious: list = [];
    has max_path_length: int = 80;

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can enter_tree with GitTree entry {
        print(f"scanning tree: {here.label}");
        visit [->:Child:->];
    }

    can inspect_entry with TreeEntry entry {
        slash_count = here.name.count("/");
        looks_like_md = (
            here.name.startswith("#")
            or "### " in here.name
            or here.name.startswith(" ")
        );
        too_long = len(here.name) > self.max_path_length;

        if slash_count > 0 or looks_like_md or too_long {
            severity = "HIGH" if (slash_count > 0 and looks_like_md) else "WARN";
            self.suspicious.append({
                "path": here.name,
                "slashes": slash_count,
                "md": looks_like_md,
                "long": too_long,
                "level": severity,
            });
            print(f"  [{severity}] path with {slash_count} slashes, "
                  f"md={looks_like_md}, len={len(here.name)}");
        }
        visit [->:Child:->];
    }

    can report with Root entry {
        print(f"audit complete: {len(self.suspicious)} suspicious paths");
    }
}

walker CascadeInvalidator {
    has target_path: str = "";

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can descend with GitTree entry {
        visit [->:Child:->];
    }

    can invalidate with TreeEntry entry {
        if here.name == self.target_path or self.target_path in here.name {
            here.is_blob = False;
            here.body_preview = "[INVALIDATED]";
            print(f"invalidated entry carrying path: {here.name[:60]}...");
        }
        visit [->:Child:->];
    }
}

with entry {
    t1 = GitTree(label="root-tree");
    t2 = GitTree(label="subdir-from-slash");
    t3 = GitTree(label="deep-notion-tree");
    root ++> t1 ++> t2 ++> t3;

    t1 +>:Child(depth=0):+> TreeEntry(name="README.md", is_blob=True);
    t1 +>:Child(depth=0):+> TreeEntry(
        name="src/main.py", is_blob=True
    );

    t2 +>:Child(depth=1):+> TreeEntry(
        name="# BananaMoon Quantum NFT",
        is_blob=True,
        is_markdown_text=True,
        body_preview="### Title\n### Subtitle\n### Overview",
    );

    t3 +>:Child(depth=2):+> TreeEntry(
        name="### Subtitle",
        is_blob=True,
        is_markdown_text=True,
    );
    t3 +>:Child(depth=2):+> TreeEntry(
        name="### Overview\nThis is the body that leaked into the path",
        is_blob=True,
        is_markdown_text=True,
    );
    t3 +>:Child(depth=2):+> TreeEntry(
        name=" ",
        is_blob=True,
        is_markdown_text=True,
    );

    root spawn PathAuditor(max_path_length=80);

    bad_path = "### Overview\nThis is the body that leaked into the path";
    root spawn CascadeInvalidator(target_path=bad_path);
}
```

