A maintainer on the project google-gemini/gemini-cli (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #29076: security(extensions): extension name path traversal allows write outside extensions directory

## Overview

A path traversal vulnerability exists in the gemini-cli extension installation flow (verified at upstream `main` commit `812f7a2bc`). Extension names taken from `extension.json` are used directly as filesystem path components, allowing a malicious extension to write files outside the intended `~/.gemini/extensions` directory.

## Vulnerable Code

In `packages/cli/src/config/extension-manager.ts` (lines 1266-1272) and `packages/cli/src/config/extensions/storage.ts`:

```ts
const extensionPath = path.join(extensionsDir, extensionName);
await fs.promises.cp(source, extensionPath, { recursive: true });
```

An extension.json containing:

```json
{
  "name": "../.config/autostart",
  "version": "1.0.0"
}
```

causes `gemini extensions install` or auto-update to write to `~/.config/autostart` or other sensitive locations.

## Root Causes

- `validateName` (regex `/^[a-zA-Z0-9-]+$/`) exists but is only applied in `inferInstallMetadata` for GitHub sources — not for local filesystem sources or when `extension.json` is manually crafted.
- `copyExtension` uses `fs.promises.cp` with `recursive: true` without verifying that `source` is within expected bounds.
- `makeWritableRecursive` follows symlinks without limit, enabling symlink attacks to overwrite arbitrary files.

## Repro Steps

1. Create a local extension dir with `extension.json` containing `{"name": "../evil", "version": "1.0.0"}`
2. Run `gemini extensions install /path/to/evil-extension`
3. Observe files written to the parent directory of the extensions dir

## Expected Fix

- Strictly validate `extensionName` against path traversal (`..`, `/`, `\`) at all entry points, not just GitHub inference, with explicit rejection if `path.join(extensionsDir, name)` resolves outside `extensionsDir` (via an `isWithinRoot` check).
- `copyExtension` should verify `source` is within the expected temp directory and `destination` is within the extensions directory before copying.
- `makeWritableRecursive` should limit symlink depth and not follow symlinks that escape the destination.

## Affected Scope

- Files: `packages/cli/src/config/extension-manager.ts`, `packages/cli/src/config/extensions/storage.ts`, `packages/cli/src/config/extension.ts`
- Affects all platforms; a malicious extension (e.g., from a compromised git repository) is the attack vector.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Extension install path-traversal defense model.

Demonstrates the core scenario from gemini-cli issue #29076: extension
names must be validated so that ``path.join(extensionsDir, name)``
cannot escape the extensions directory. The model uses a tree of
``Dir`` nodes connected by ``Contains`` edges, a ``File`` leaf, and an
``Extension`` request that proposes a target name. A walker traverses the
graph to verify the resolved location is still inside the root and rejects
any name that escapes via ``..`` segments or absolute prefixes.
"""

node Dir {
    has name: str = "";
    has depth: int = 0;
}

node File {
    has name: str = "";
}

node Extension {
    has title: str = "";
    has proposed_name: str = "";
    has is_safe: bool = True;
    has reject_reason: str = "";
}

edge Contains { }

walker PathAuditor {
    has segments: list[str] = [];
    has safe: bool = True;
    has reason: str = "";

    can start with Root entry {
        # Pull every extension request sitting directly on root.
        visit [-->];
    }

    can audit with Extension entry {
        # Reset per-extension state on the walker.
        self.safe = True;
        self.reason = "";
        # Tokenize the proposed name on '/' (also handle '\' segments).
        raw = here.proposed_name.replace("\\", "/");
        self.segments = raw.split("/");
        # Begin traversal from root into the extensions directory.
        visit [-->];
    }

    can walk with Dir entry {
        # If the walker is already unsafe, stop descending.
        if not self.safe {
            return;
        }
        # Pop the next segment; an empty segment is a no-op.
        if len(self.segments) == 0 {
            return;
        }
        head = self.segments[0];
        rest = self.segments[1:];
        self.segments = rest;
        # Reject absolute escapes up front.
        if here.depth == 0 and head == "" {
            self.safe = False;
            self.reason = "absolute path rejected";
            return;
        }
        # ".." attempts to climb above the current directory.
        if head == ".." {
            if here.depth == 0 {
                self.safe = False;
                self.reason = "traversal escapes extensions root";
            } else {
                here.depth = here.depth - 1;
            }
            return;
        }
        # A concrete name component descends one level.
        if head != "" {
            here.depth = here.depth + 1;
        }
        # Continue traversal into the matching child Dir.
        visit [-->];
    }

    can land with File entry {
        # Files are valid terminal nodes — the copy destination is fine.
        return;
    }

    can record with Root entry {
        # Walker returns to root; stamp the audit verdict on the
        # originating Extension node and report once.
        label = "ACCEPT" if self.safe else "REJECT";
        print(f"[{label}] '{here.title}' -> '{here.proposed_name}'"
            + (f" ({self.reason})" if not self.safe else ""));
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build a small directory tree representing the extensions layout:
    #   root/
    #     extensions/
    #       tools/
    #         linters/
    #     cache/
    extensions_dir = Dir(name="extensions", depth=0);
    tools_dir = Dir(name="tools", depth=1);
    linters_dir = Dir(name="linters", depth=2);
    cache_dir = Dir(name="cache", depth=0);

    root ++> extensions_dir;
    extensions_dir +>:Contains:+> tools_dir;
    tools_dir +>:Contains:+> linters_dir;
    root ++> cache_dir;

    # Craft a handful of extension requests to exercise the auditor.
    good_ext = Extension(
        title="safe-ext",
        proposed_name="tools/linters",
        is_safe=True
    );
    traversal_ext = Extension(
        title="evil-ext",
        proposed_name="../../etc/passwd",
        is_safe=True
    );
    absolute_ext = Extension(
        title="abs-ext",
        proposed_name="/usr/local/bin",
        is_safe=True
    );
    sibling_ext = Extension(
        title="sibling-ext",
        proposed_name="../cache/payload",
        is_safe=True
    );

    root ++> good_ext;
    root ++> traversal_ext;
    root ++> absolute_ext;
    root ++> sibling_ext;

    # Spawn the auditor once; it visits each Extension and returns to
    # root to print a single per-extension verdict line.
    PathAuditor() spawn root;
}
```

