Issue #431: Add liveness_primer as a blast radius CI job

## Background

`vulture` should consider integrating a CI workflow that can help detect regressions before they occur, and allow more efficient/thorough review loops on working changes. 

I created a tool [`liveness_primer`](https://github.com/mcdigman/liveness_primer), inspired by [`mypy_primer`](https://github.com/hauntsaninja/mypy_primer), that helps measure the *blast radius* of a PR: which findings appeared, disappeared, changed, or failed to analyze at all. To achieve this, `liveness_primer` does two full runs of `vulture` over a corpus of open-source, permissively licensed GitHub repositories, and outputs the *diff* of the resulting findings produced by a PR or commit. 

The primary intended use is as a per-PR CI job, or it can be run locally using the CLI to test the expected downstream impact of the commit before creating a PR. With the CLI, it can also be used to analyze *any* GitHub repo, and any pair of vulture commits, not just a specific PR or an existing corpus source. 

Github Comment Workflow:

<img width="783" height="497" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/48160406-3d58-47f6-bd9a-7265936cc24e" />

## Use Cases

In order to both test `liveness_primer` on vulture and demonstrate the value of a `liveness_primer` CI job to the repo, I simulated the outputs on several historical or active vulture PRs, and explained in comments on each how they demonstrate the value:

1. [Simulated PR 1](https://github.com/mcdigman/vulture/pull/1) demonstrates a real historical vulture PR #378 that introduced a regression. It caused several downstream problems and later had to be fixed in a commit represented by [simulated PR 2](https://github.com/mcdigman/vulture/pull/2). As shown, if liveness_primer had been a CI job originally, the regression could have been spotted on review; the second simulated PR shows that the fix removed exactly the same false-positive findings that it identified on the first.
2. [Simulated PR 6](https://github.com/mcdigman/vulture/pull/6) Demonstrates a (still-live) PR #390 that *introduces* the exact kind of false positives it was trying to suppress.
3. [Simulated PR 9](https://github.com/mcdigman/vulture/pull/9) Demonstrates using `liveness_primer` to analyze a (still-live) PR #417 with a *very large blast radius*, including the value of the package's interactive report explorer for making what would be a very large report much more tractable for human analysis. Using `liveness_primer` in a case like this could help close the review loop on a large PR faster, and close many real-world edge cases before the PR actually merges. 

[Simulated PR 3](https://github.com/mcdigman/vulture/pull/3) (based on #403) and [Simulated PR 7](https://github.com/mcdigman/vulture/pull/7) (based on #312) demonstrate further use cases, described in comments on the simulated PRs.

Note: I will report the issues on the still-live PRs separately.

## CI Cost
With the current corpus and workflow configuration, liveness_primer runs on the entire vulture corpus in <2 minutes on a standard GitHub runner, comparable to the walltime of each of the large matrix of test jobs, so the marginal cost in CI minutes or CI walltime is not very significant. The corpus enabled for vulture can easily be expanded or trimmed as desired. 

## CI Behavior
Currently, liveness_primer is a non-blocking advisory workflow. The analysis workflow requires read-only permissions, and executes the pinned vulture commits in a no-network sandbox on separate bit-identical copies of the corpus. The separate comment workflow needs read/write permissions, excerpts the output markdown without running anything for security. Future extensions analogous to `mypy_primer` could make it blocking in desired cases, like if a known-clean repository stops being clean.

