A maintainer on the project Madan2248c/ariadne (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #6: get_call_path: BFS loads all edges into memory

## Problem

`getCallPath()` in `src/graph/queries.ts` loads ALL `calls` edges from the DB into an in-process adjacency list before running BFS:

```typescript
const edgeRows = db.prepare(
  `SELECT from_symbol, to_symbol, line FROM edges WHERE kind = 'calls'`
).all()
```

For large repos (300K+ edges, ~100K of which are `calls`), this is a full table scan loaded into a JS Map on every `get_call_path` call.

## Impact

- ~50–100ms extra latency per call on large repos
- Unnecessary memory allocation on every call

## Proposed fix

Use a bidirectional BFS that queries the DB iteratively — only fetching neighbours of the current frontier instead of all edges upfront:

```sql
SELECT to_symbol, line FROM edges
WHERE from_symbol = $id AND kind = 'calls'
```

This trades more DB round-trips for much lower memory usage. With SQLite's in-process speed, each hop query is <1ms.

## Files to change

- `src/graph/queries.ts` — `getCallPath()` function

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Call graph: model symbols and 'calls' edges, then traverse via a walker.

This demo captures the essential scenario from the issue: instead of loading
ALL 'calls' edges into an in-process adjacency list and running BFS in memory,
traversal walks the graph one hop at a time through typed edges. The walker
mimics a bidirectional frontier expansion while only ever visiting the
neighbours reachable from the current node.
"""

node Symbol {
    has name: str = "";
    has kind: str = "";   # function, method, class, etc.
    has loaded: bool = False;
}

edge Calls {
    has line: int = 0;
}

walker CallPath {
    has source: str = "";
    has target: str = "";
    has max_depth: int = 5;
    has visited: list[str] = [];
    has found: bool = False;

    can start with Root entry {
        # Begin by walking into the start symbol from the graph root.
        visit [-->];
    }

    can descend with Symbol entry {
        # Cycle guard: walkers in jac 0.36.1 do not dedupe on cyclic
        # graphs (verified by probe), so we track visited symbols here.
        if here.name in self.visited {
            visit [-->] else { disengage; }
        }
        self.visited.append(here.name);

        if here.name == self.target {
            self.found = True;
            print(f"FOUND {self.target} at depth {len(self.visited) - 1}");
            disengage;
        }

        if len(self.visited) > self.max_depth {
            visit [-->] else { disengage; }
        }

        # Frontier expansion: only the neighbours of the CURRENT node
        # are walked — never the whole adjacency list at once.
        print(f"hop {len(self.visited)}: at {here.name} ({here.kind})");
        visit [->:Calls:->];
    }

    can report with Root entry {
        if self.found {
            path = " -> ".join(self.visited);
            print(f"call path: {path}");
        } else {
            print(f"no call path from {self.source} to {self.target} "
                  f"within depth {self.max_depth}");
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    a = Symbol(name="main", kind="function");
    b = Symbol(name="parse", kind="function");
    c = Symbol(name="tokenize", kind="function");
    d = Symbol(name="scan", kind="function");
    e = Symbol(name="emit", kind="function");

    # Wire the call graph with typed 'Calls' edges carrying line numbers.
    a +>:Calls(line=10):+> b;
    a +>:Calls(line=11):+> c;
    b +>:Calls(line=22):+> d;
    c +>:Calls(line=33):+> d;
    d +>:Calls(line=41):+> e;

    # Hang everything off root so the walker has a spawn point.
    root ++> a ++> b ++> c ++> d ++> e;

    # Find a path from main to emit (expected hops: main -> parse -> scan -> emit).
    w = CallPath(source="main", target="emit", max_depth=6);
    root spawn w;

    # Second probe: target that is unreachable from main within the depth cap.
    w2 = CallPath(source="main", target="unrelated", max_depth=6);
    root spawn w2;
}
```

