- Adding a visited set in bfs - 

<img width="1404" height="708" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/69c3d7fa-8e00-4f89-9613-36f4e78eeabe" />

The visited set acts as the algorithm's memory, preventing it from re-processing the same node multiple times and ensuring the search remains efficient.

It is a crucial safety measure that prevents infinite loops if your data ever contains "cycles" (where a child node accidentally links back to a parent).

By marking nodes as seen, the BFS can guarantee it explores the shortest path to your target and terminates correctly once all reachable options are exhausted.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Course recommendation graph: model courses as nodes and traverse prereq chains.

Scenario lifted from issue #1 about hand-rolled BFS in a course/job
recommender. The maintainer added a visited set to stop the BFS from
looping on cycles (a child accidentally pointing back at a parent).

In idiomatic Jac the relationship is a typed edge and the traversal is
a walker; because walkers in jac 0.36.1 have no built-in once-per-node
visitation, the cycle policy (visited set) is domain logic and survives
the lift. ``visit [...] else { ... }`` is dead-end handling, not a
cycle guard, so we keep the explicit seen-set in the walker.
"""

node Course {
    has title: str = "";
    has year: int = 0;
    has available: bool = True;
}

edge Prereq {
    has required: bool = True;
}

walker CourseSearch {
    has target: str = "";
    has seen: list[str] = [];
    has path: list[str] = [];
    has found: bool = False;

    can start with Root entry {
        self.seen = [];
        self.path = [];
        self.found = False;
        visit [-->];
    }

    can walk with Course entry {
        if self.found {
            disengage;
        }
        if here.title in self.seen {
            # Cycle guard: a child prereq accidentally points back at
            # an ancestor. Skip instead of looping forever.
            visit [-->] else { disengage; }
        }
        self.seen.append(here.title);
        self.path.append(here.title);

        if here.title == self.target {
            self.found = True;
            print(f"FOUND '{self.target}' after visiting "
                  f"{self.seen.length} course(s): {self.path}");
            disengage;
        }

        if not here.available {
            print(f"  [closed] {here.title} ({here.year}) -- not offered");
            visit [-->] else { disengage; }
        }

        status = "OPEN" if here.available else "CLOSED";
        print(f"  [{status}] {here.title} ({here.year})");
        visit [->:Prereq:->] else { disengage; }
    }
}

walker CatalogCloser {
    # Cascade invalidation: when a course is taken off the schedule,
    # every course that lists it as a required prereq is also closed.
    has to_close: list[str] = [];
    has closed_count: int = 0;

    can start with Root entry {
        print("Cascade-closing prereq tree from 'CS101'");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can invalidate with Course entry {
        if here.title in self.to_close {
            # Already closed in this run; skip without re-firing.
            visit [->:Prereq:->] else { disengage; }
        }
        self.to_close.append(here.title);
        here.available = False;
        self.closed_count += 1;
        print(f"  closed {here.title} ({here.year}) "
              f"[{self.closed_count} total]");
        visit [->:Prereq:->] else { disengage; }
    }
}

