Issue #147: Followup #118 D1: triggered_by cycle detection

**Origin:** [#118](https://github.com/lantisprime/episodic-memory/issues/118) negative-scenario cross-product gap #3. Plan-agent 2nd-opinion verdict: safe to defer.

## Background

#118 ships `triggered_by` envelope on `workflow.lifecycle` review-request events. The validator's `resolveEpisodeRef` (em-workflow-validate.mjs:393-414) checks: (a) id exists, (b) status=active, (c) not self via `SELF_REFS`, (d) timestamp ordering, (e) optional category. **It does not detect cycles** at depth ≥ 2 (e.g. A → B → A).

## Why deferred

User-cooperative threat model. Same-task review-request chains are expected linear. Cycles only matter under deliberate gaming. Pre-rule defensive check, low real-world risk.

## S1 (#118) impact: **none**

- `triggered_by` field IS the carrier; future cycle detection is a pure traversal added to validator (zero schema change).
- `SELF_REFS` already covers depth-0 self-reference.
- Folded gap #2 task-binding already drains the cross-task attack surface.

## Acceptance criteria

- Validator detects cycles in `triggered_by` chain at any depth via DFS / visited-set.
- Test: A.triggered_by → B, B.triggered_by → C, C.triggered_by → A → reject with cycle path in error.

## References

Parent: #118 · Sibling shape: #102 chain-walk model (different chain — predecessor refs vs causal refs).

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Cycle detection in ``triggered_by`` review-request chains.

Models the validator scenario from lantisprime/episodic-memory #147:
``triggered_by`` envelopes on workflow review-request events form a
graph where each episode may reference a predecessor episode. The
validator must reject cycles at any depth (>=2), reporting the cycle
path so the caller can surface it.

The graph machinery is lifted from hand-rolled parent_id recursion
(``resolveEpisodeRef`` walking ``triggered_by`` ids) to a typed-edge
walker that traverses ``TriggeredBy`` edges with an explicit visited
set and depth cap. Walkers in jac 0.36.1 have no built-in dedupe, so
cycle safety remains explicit code — that is preserved domain logic.
"""

node Episode {
    has title: str = "";
    has eid: str = "";
    has status: str = "active";
    has category: str = "";
}

edge TriggeredBy {
    has reason: str = "";
}

# Self-references (depth-0) are rejected up front, matching the
# original ``SELF_REFS`` guard in resolveEpisodeRef.
node SelfRefError {
    has title: str = "";
}

# Cycle detection result nodes the validator produces. They carry
# the cycle path so the caller can log it.
node CycleDetected {
    has path: list[str] = [];
    has message: str = "";
}

walker ChainAuditor {
    has max_depth: int = 16;
    has trail: list[str] = [];

    can begin with Root entry {
        visit [->:TriggeredBy:->];
    }

    can walk_chain with Episode entry {
        # Walk the chain forward via TriggeredBy edges. We track the
        # trail for reporting and the visited set for cycle safety.
        self.trail.append(here.eid);
        next_ok = False;
        for child in [->:TriggeredBy:-> here] {
            next_ok = True;
            if child.eid in self.trail {
                # Cycle at depth >= 2. Build path = trail + child and
                # surface a CycleDetected node the validator returns.
                cycle_path = self.trail + [child.eid];
                report CycleDetected(
                    path=cycle_path,
                    message=f"cycle detected: {' -> '.join(cycle_path)}",
                );
                disengage;
            }
            if len(self.trail) >= self.max_depth {
                report CycleDetected(
                    path=self.trail,
                    message=f"depth cap {self.max_depth} reached",
                );
                disengage;
            }
            visit [child];
        }
        if not next_ok {
            # Dead-end: legitimate end of chain, normal completion.
            report Episode(title=f"chain ok: {' -> '.join(self.trail)}");
            disengage;
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    # Linear chain: A -> B -> C. Validator walks it, reaches C,
    # C has no outgoing TriggeredBy edge -> normal completion.
    a = Episode(title="review-A", eid="A", category="spec");
    b = Episode(title="review-B", eid="B", category="spec");
    c = Episode(title="review-C", eid="C", category="spec");

    root +>:TriggeredBy(reason="predecessor"):+> a;
    a +>:TriggeredBy(reason="predecessor"):+> b;
    b +>:TriggeredBy(reason="predecessor"):+> c;

    print("=== Linear chain A -> B -> C ===");
    walker_a = ChainAuditor();
    root spawn walker_a;
    if walker_a.report {
        for rep in walker_a.report {
            print(f"[result] {rep}");
        }
    }

    # Cycle chain: D -> E -> F -> D. Validator must reject and
    # report the cycle path.
    d = Episode(title="review-D", eid="D", category="spec");
    e = Episode(title="review-E", eid="E", category="spec");
    f = Episode(title="review-F", eid="F", category="spec");

    root +>:TriggeredBy(reason="predecessor"):+> d;
    d +>:TriggeredBy(reason="predecessor"):+> e;
    e +>:TriggeredBy(reason="predecessor"):+> f;
    f +>:TriggeredBy(reason="predecessor"):+> d;

    print("=== Cyclic chain D -> E -> F -> D ===");
    walker_b = ChainAuditor();
    root spawn walker_b;
    if walker_b.report {
        for rep in walker_b.report {
            print(f"[result] {rep.message}");
        }
    }
}
