A maintainer on the project open-policy-agent/opa (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #9088: make `*ast.Compiler` context-aware

## What is the underlying problem you're trying to solve?

Our use case for OPA involves accepting user-defined code through a REST API at runtime before evaluating it, which means we end up invoking the compiler on arbitrary, untrusted Rego code via `bundle.Activate()`. We pass a custom `*ast.Compiler` object already to handle print statements and restricting capabilities already, and `bundle.Activate()` itself takes a context. However, `*ast.Compiler.Compile()` itself takes no context, and none is available in [`compileModules()`](https://github.com/open-policy-agent/opa/blob/54896f9f28515d14b31a93e6ef5737bb85866e30/v1/bundle/store.go#L993). As a result, a compilation process which takes a long time could hang a request indefinately, even after its context has been canceled. 

## Describe the ideal solution

OPA's compiler would accept a context and respect cancellation.

I believe we'll need to punch the context all the way down into the individual compile stages. We can pass it in where the stage gets invoked [here](https://github.com/open-policy-agent/opa/blob/54896f9f28515d14b31a93e6ef5737bb85866e30/v1/ast/compile.go#L1963). Minimally, the context should be checked for cancellation before each stage runs. Some stages which have long running loops or which recurse should check each loop iteration or during each recursion step (e.g. `WalkRules()`).

API compatibility can be maintained by introducing new methods like `CompileWithContext()`. Existing methods can become wrappers that use `context.Background()`.

## Describe a "Good Enough" solution

As a stopgap, our current approach is to bound the size of the input bundle, but bundle size is an imperfect proxy for the time it will actually take to complete the compilation.

## Additional Context

I'm open to working on this but will need some guidance on the approach, I haven't worked much with the compiler, and this is potentially a cross cutting change.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Compiler pipeline with cancellable stages and per-iteration cycle safety.

Models the OPA ast.Compiler scenario: user-defined code flows through a chain
of compilation stages, each of which may recurse over rule/import graphs and
must respect cancellation. A Budget token bounds total work; each stage checks
it before stepping, so a runaway compile (e.g. deep or cyclic rules) aborts
cleanly instead of hanging a request.
"""

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Entities
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# A single unit of code submitted through the REST API. `rules` is the
# adjacency representation of the rule tree; in idiomatic Jac it becomes the
# set of outgoing DependsOn edges instead.
node Module {
    has name: str = "";
    has rules: list[str] = [];
}

# The bounded execution context. `remaining` is the budget the stages draw
# from; `canceled` is the trip wire the walker checks at each iteration.
node Budget {
    has remaining: int = 1000;
    has canceled: bool = False;
}

# An empty marker: a Module merely declares its existence. The compile
# stages perform the real work; their relationship to the modules they
# process is this edge.
edge CompiledBy { }

# A typed dependency edge between modules. A rule in module A may reference
# symbols defined in module B; that reference is the dependency. The compile
# walk follows these edges, so they must be declared at module level.
edge DependsOn { }

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Walker
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# Compiler walks the module graph one stage at a time. Each ability checks
# the Budget before doing work and before recursing, so cancellation is
# honored per iteration -- the exact behaviour the issue is asking for.
walker Compiler {
    has budget_ref: Budget = None;
    has stage_name: str = "";
    has modules_seen: list[str] = [];

    # Walk every module reachable from root through DependsOn edges,
    # running the configured stage on each one. Cycle safety is explicit:
    # we track visited module names and short-circuit when a cycle is
    # detected, instead of recursing forever.
    can start with Root entry {
        print(f"[stage:{self.stage_name}] beginning compilation pass");
        visit [->:DependsOn:->];
    }

    can compile_module with Module entry {
        if self.budget_ref.canceled {
            print(f"[stage:{self.stage_name}] canceled before {here.name}");
            disengage;
        }

        # Cycle guard: if we've already processed this module on this
        # pass, skip it. Without this a DependsOn loop would hang forever.
        already_done = False;
        for prior in self.modules_seen {
            if prior == here.name {
                already_done = True;
            }
        }
        if already_done {
            print(f"[stage:{self.stage_name}] skip already-seen {here.name}");
            disengage;
        }
        self.modules_seen.append(here.name);

        # Per-iteration budget check: long-running loops in the real
        # compiler check cancellation each step; we mirror that here.
        if self.budget_ref.remaining <= 0 {
            self.budget_ref.canceled = True;
            print(f"[stage:{self.stage_name}] budget exhausted at {here.name}");
            disengage;
        }
        self.budget_ref.remaining = self.budget_ref.remaining - 1;

        print(
            f"[stage:{self.stage_name}] processing {here.name} "
            f"(budget left: {self.budget_ref.remaining})"
        );

        # Recurse into this module's dependencies. The typed edge
        # ``DependsOn`` is the relationship; we don't read a rules
        # list, we follow edges.
        visit [here->:DependsOn:->];
    }
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Demo wiring
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

with entry {
    # Three modules that form a dependency graph. main pulls in helpers
    # and util; helpers pulls in util; util is a leaf. No cycles here.
    main = Module(name="main.rego", rules=["helpers", "util"]);
    helpers = Module(name="helpers.rego", rules=["util"]);
    util = Module(name="util.rego", rules=[]);

    # Connect via the typed DependsOn edge so the walker traverses by
    # relationship, not by reading an adjacency list off the node.
    main +>:DependsOn:+> helpers;
    helpers +>:DependsOn:+> util;
    root +>:DependsOn:+> main;

    # A shared budget. Make it small so the third stage demonstrates the
    # "long compile gets cut off" behaviour from the issue.
    shared_budget = Budget(remaining=3, canceled=False);

    # Stage 1: parse -- plenty of budget, should complete.
    c1 = Compiler(
        budget_ref=shared_budget,
        stage_name="parse",
        modules_seen=[]
    );
    root spawn c1;
    print(f"after parse: budget={shared_budget.remaining} canceled={shared_budget.canceled}");
    print("---");

    # Reset for the next stage so we can demonstrate cancellation cleanly.
    shared_budget.remaining = 3;
    shared_budget.canceled = False;

    # Stage 2: type-check -- walks again, uses the same three modules.
    c2 = Compiler(
        budget_ref=shared_budget,
        stage_name="typecheck",
        modules_seen=[]
    );
    root spawn c2;
    print(f"after typecheck: budget={shared_budget.remaining} canceled={shared_budget.canceled}");
    print("---");

    # Stage 3: a tiny budget that runs out mid-walk, proving the
    # per-iteration check kicks in instead of hanging the request.
    tight_budget = Budget(remaining=1, canceled=False);
    c3 = Compiler(
        budget_ref=tight_budget,
        stage_name="eval",
        modules_seen=[]
    );
    root spawn c3;
    print(f"after eval: budget={tight_budget.remaining} canceled={tight_budget.canceled}");
}
```

