A maintainer on the project chaoz23/charactercheck (Python) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #13: INPUT-001 — Validate input and guarantee stable, non-traceback failures

**Priority:** P0  
**Type:** API robustness/security  
**Size:** M

### Problem

Syntactically valid but incomplete JSON such as `{}` raises `KeyError`; the documented diff baseline raises `TypeError`; arbitrary first digits in a missing path are treated as a character ID; container cycles can loop; files and responses lack size/depth limits. The CLI catches only typed retrieval errors despite claiming no traceback.

### Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Explicitly parse one of: numeric ID, allowlisted D&D Beyond URL shape, local file URI/path when that capability is enabled, or canonical snapshot.
- [ ] A missing/invalid path is never reinterpreted as a character ID.
- [ ] Validate top-level envelope and required shapes before derivation; include schema fingerprint/version and migration policy.
- [ ] Set maximum file/response size, nesting depth, item/modifier counts, and string lengths.
- [ ] Detect container/reference cycles with a visited set and bounded traversal.
- [ ] Close all files/responses deterministically; test suite emits no `ResourceWarning`.
- [ ] Boundary catches all unexpected exceptions, produces a stable redacted `internal_error`, and logs a correlation ID without leaking source data.
- [ ] Library APIs expose typed exceptions; CLI/MCP translate them without losing kind/action.

### Test plan

- Empty/list/scalar/truncated/oversized/deep JSON; missing fields; wrong types; digit-bearing missing path; symlink/path policy; cyclic containers; invalid Unicode; upstream wrong envelope; fuzz corpus; no traceback/path leak assertions.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Character-check validation graph: cycle-aware input validation with depth/size caps.

Models the essential scenario from INPUT-001: characters are nodes, their
character-builder containers form a tree of typed edges, and a validation
walker walks the structure once with explicit visited-set + depth/size
limits (no recursion plumbing, no N+1 loops). It also handles the
"first-digit path mistaken for character ID" trap by treating a bare digit
string as an explicit ID type rather than a path, and rejects anything else.
"""

# ---------- Entity archetypes ----------

node Character {
    has title: str = "";
    has level: int = 1;
    has valid: bool = True;
}

node Container {
    has name: str = "";
    has depth: int = 0;
}

# Container may legally point back to a character (cycle attempts).
# Cycle detection is a domain invariant — the walker must enforce it,
# because in jac 0.36.1 walkers have no built-in once-per-node visit.

edge Holds {
    has weight: int = 1;
}

# ---------- Walker: cycle-aware validator ----------

walker Validate {
    has max_depth: int = 8;
    has max_nodes: int = 64;
    has visited: list[str] = [];
    has cycles: int = 0;
    has checked: int = 0;
    has errors: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        # Parse the input kind explicitly. A bare digit string is a Character ID;
        # anything else is either a Container path or invalid — never both.
        raw_input = "char-7";
        kind = self.classify(raw_input);
        if kind == "invalid" {
            self.errors.append("input could not be parsed as id or path");
            report self;
            disengage;
        }
        print(f"input kind: {kind}");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can descend with Container entry {
        # Depth cap: explicit domain invariant, not scaffolding.
        if here.depth > self.max_depth {
            self.errors.append(f"depth cap exceeded at {here.name}");
            disengage;
        }
        # Node cap: bounded walk.
        if self.checked >= self.max_nodes {
            self.errors.append("node cap exceeded");
            disengage;
        }
        # Cycle guard: domain invariant preserved explicitly because
        # the language does not dedupe walker visits on cyclic graphs.
        if here.name in self.visited {
            self.cycles += 1;
            print(f"[CYCLE] revisited {here.name}");
            disengage;
        }
        self.visited.append(here.name);
        self.checked += 1;
        print(f"[CONTAINER] {here.name} (depth {here.depth})");
        visit [->:Holds:->];
    }

    can inspect with Character entry {
        if not here.valid {
            self.errors.append(f"invalid character: {here.title}");
        }
        print(f"[CHARACTER] {here.title} lvl {here.level}");
    }

    """Classify input as explicit ID, container path, or invalid.

    A bare numeric string is treated as a Character ID (never a path);
    a slash-bearing string is a container path. Anything else is invalid
    so a missing/invalid path is never reinterpreted as a character ID.
    """
    def classify(raw: str) -> str {
        if raw == "" {
            return "invalid";
        }
        is_digits = True;
        for ch in raw {
            if ch not in "0123456789" {
                is_digits = False;
                break;
            }
        }
        if is_digits {
            return "id";
        }
        has_slash = False;
        for ch in raw {
            if ch == "/" {
                has_slash = True;
                break;
            }
        }
        if has_slash {
            return "path";
        }
        return "invalid";
    }
}

# ---------- Demonstration graph ----------

with entry {
    # A small container tree with an attempted cycle at the leaf.
    c1 = Container(name="party/A", depth=1);
    c2 = Container(name="party/B", depth=2);
    c3 = Container(name="party/C", depth=3);
    c4 = Container(name="party/D", depth=4);

    hero_a = Character(title="Ranger", level=5);
    hero_b = Character(title="Cleric", level=3);
    hero_c = Character(title="Rogue", level=7);
    hero_d = Character(title="Wizard", level=4);

    # Typed edges carry the "contains" relationship.
    root +>:Holds:+> c1;
    c1 +>:Holds:+> c2;
    c2 +>:Holds:+> c3;
    c3 +>:Holds:+> c4;
    c4 +>:Holds:+> hero_a;
    c1 +>:Holds:+> hero_b;
    c3 +>:Holds:+> hero_c;
    c4 +>:Holds:+> hero_d;

    # Container cycle: c4 holds c2, forming c2 -> c3 -> c4 -> c2 ...
    c4 +>:Holds:+> c2;

    print("=== validating structure ===");
    v = Validate(max_depth=8, max_nodes=64);
