A maintainer on the project floeorg/floe (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #1523: the checker overflows its stack on a self-referencing typealias

Found while fixing #1521. **Reproduces on `main`.**

## The bug

```floe
typealias Tree = { l: Tree, r: Tree }
```

The checker recurses without a visited set and **overflows its stack**. The program never reaches codegen, so this is a crash rather than a miscompile.

## What it should do

Report a clear error naming the cycle. A `typealias` is structural, so a self-reference has no finite shape and cannot be resolved. A nominal `type Tree = { l: Tree, r: Tree }` is a different question and should keep working, because a nominal type is a name and the recursion terminates.

## Note

`#1521` added an alias walk in codegen that carries the open type names and stops on a repeat, so **codegen is already safe** if the checker is fixed. Whatever the checker does here, it will not be the thing that crashes.

## Tests

- A self-referencing `typealias` reports an error naming the cycle and does not crash.
- A two-step cycle, `typealias A = B` and `typealias B = A`, reports the same.
- A nominal `type Tree = { l: Tree, r: Tree }` still checks and builds.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Type alias checker: detect cycles in structural typealiases.

Models the floeorg/floe issue where a self-referencing structural
typealias (``typealias Tree = { l: Tree, r: Tree }``) overflows the
checker because it recurses without a visited set. The walker here
performs the same open-name expansion that the checker does, but with
an explicit guard set so a cycle produces a clear error rather than
a stack overflow. Nominal ``type`` declarations are modelled as
separate node archetypes that terminate recursion on their named
boundary.
"""

node TypeAlias {
    has alias_name: str = "";
    has target: str = "";
}

node NominalType {
    has type_name: str = "";
}

node AliasTarget {
    has open_names: list[str] = [];
}

edge ResolvesTo {
    has label: str = "";
}

walker CycleChecker {
    has visited: list[str] = [];
    has cycle_found: bool = False;
    has cycle_path: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can inspect_target with AliasTarget entry {
        if self.cycle_found {
            disengage;
        }
        for name in here.open_names {
            if name in self.visited {
                self.cycle_found = True;
                self.cycle_path = self.visited + [name];
                report f"cycle detected: {' -> '.join(self.cycle_path)}";
                disengage;
            }
            self.visited = self.visited + [name];
            visit [->:ResolvesTo:->];
        }
    }

    can follow_alias with TypeAlias entry {
        if self.cycle_found {
            disengage;
        }
        if here.alias_name in self.visited {
            self.cycle_found = True;
            self.cycle_path = self.visited + [here.alias_name];
            report f"cycle detected: {' -> '.join(self.cycle_path)}";
            disengage;
        }
        self.visited = self.visited + [here.alias_name];
        visit [->:ResolvesTo:->];
    }

    can nominal_terminator with NominalType entry {
        if self.cycle_found {
            disengage;
        }
        report f"nominal type '{here.type_name}' terminates recursion";
    }
}

with entry {
    bad_self_ref = TypeAlias(alias_name="Tree", target="Tree");
    root +>:ResolvesTo(label="structural"):+> bad_self_ref;
    bad_self_ref +>:ResolvesTo(label="structural"):+> AliasTarget(
        open_names=["Tree"]
    );

    two_step_a = TypeAlias(alias_name="A", target="B");
    two_step_b = TypeAlias(alias_name="B", target="A");
    root +>:ResolvesTo(label="structural"):+> two_step_a;
    two_step_a +>:ResolvesTo(label="structural"):+> two_step_b;
    two_step_b +>:ResolvesTo(label="structural"):+> AliasTarget(
        open_names=["A"]
    );

    nominal_tree = NominalType(type_name="Tree");
    root +>:ResolvesTo(label="nominal"):+> nominal_tree;

    print("=== self-referencing structural alias ===");
    root spawn CycleChecker(visited=[], cycle_found=False, cycle_path=[]);

    print("=== two-step alias cycle ===");
    root spawn CycleChecker(visited=[], cycle_found=False, cycle_path=[]);

    print("=== nominal self-reference still checks ===");
    root spawn CycleChecker(visited=["Tree"], cycle_found=False, cycle_path=[]);
}
