Task:
A maintainer on the project tirth8205/code-review-graph (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #908: watch: ignored trees created after startup are watched recursively, re-creating #811's exposure

Found in a read-only audit of v2.3.8. Verified against current `main`.

## What happens

`_plan_watch_subtree` calls a directory "clean" when the ignored trees under it weigh less than `_WATCH_SPLIT_MIN_DIRS` (4 directories), and a clean directory is covered by one **recursive** watch (`code_review_graph/incremental.py:1665`). The planner runs once, at startup.

So the split that keeps `node_modules` and `target/` out of the kernel's hands only happens if those trees are already big when the watcher starts. Start `watch` on a fresh clone and then run `npm install`, and the new `node_modules` grows underneath a recursive watch on its parent — the resource-exhaustion path #811 is about.

The same applies to the budget fallback: when no plan fits, the root is watched recursively (`:1703`), and everything created later is inside it.

## Why it matters

The release notes say ignored trees are never registered with the OS. That is true for a repository whose ignored trees already exist at startup, and false for one where they arrive afterwards — which is the normal order for CI, containers, and a fresh clone.

## Suggested fix

Re-plan when a shallow watch's children change materially: `sync_watches` already scans the children of every non-recursive watch each tick, so a recursive watch whose subtree has grown an ignored directory above the split threshold could be released and re-planned. Failing that, document the limitation and log once when a recursive watch is installed over a directory that has no ignored children *yet*.

## Related

- The startup budget fallback never sets `degraded = True`, so `crg-daemon status` reports `ok` where `docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md:189` says it should report degraded coverage. Only the runtime promotion path sets that flag.


Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Watch-planner re-plan demo: ignored subtrees created AFTER startup under a
recursive watch escape the split planner and stay watched recursively.

Models the essential scenario from issue #908: a planner decides at startup
which subtrees are ignored-heavy enough to deserve their own shallow watch
(``has_ignored_trees`` + ``Ignored`` edge). A directory without enough ignored
children at planning time gets a ``Recursive`` watch edge. If ignored trees
later grow under it, the original plan is stale and the new trees are watched
recursively by the kernel — the exposure the issue describes.
"""

node Directory {
    has name: str = "";
    # Total child directories observed under this node (used by the planner).
    has child_count: int = 0;
    # How many of those children are ignored (node_modules, target/, etc).
    has ignored_child_count: int = 0;
}

# The threshold below which a directory is considered "clean" and gets one
# recursive watch covering everything underneath.
glob WATCH_SPLIT_MIN_DIRS: int = 4;

edge Ignored {
    has reason: str = "";
}

# A recursive watch installed over a directory. The kernel will pick up every
# file/folder created under it later — including newly-grown ignored trees.
edge Recursive {
    has installed_at: str = "startup";
}

# A shallow watch on a directory (the kernel only sees immediate children).
edge Shallow {
    has installed_at: str = "startup";
}

walker Planner {
    has reentry: bool = False;

    can plan with Root entry {
        print(f"[plan] startup pass (re-entry={self.reentry})");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can plan_dir with Directory entry {
        heavy_enough = here.ignored_child_count >= WATCH_SPLIT_MIN_DIRS;
        if heavy_enough {
            print(f"[plan]   {here.name}: "
                  f"{here.ignored_child_count} ignored dirs >= "
                  f"{WATCH_SPLIT_MIN_DIRS} → Shallow watch installed");
            visit [-->];
        } else {
            tag = "stale-recursive" if self.reentry else "startup-recursive";
            print(f"[plan]   {here.name}: "
                  f"{here.ignored_child_count} ignored dirs < "
                  f"{WATCH_SPLIT_MIN_DIRS} → Recursive watch installed "
                  f"({tag})");
            visit [-->];
        }
    }
}

walker Auditor {
    can audit with Root entry {
        print("[audit] walking watch graph to find ignored trees under recursive watches");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can audit_dir with Directory entry {
        # Look at every recursive edge leaving this directory and check whether
        # ignored subtrees grew underneath it after the planner ran. This is
        # the re-plan opportunity the issue suggests.
        recs = [here ->:Recursive:->];
        for r in recs {
            name = here.name;
            grew = here.ignored_child_count >= WATCH_SPLIT_MIN_DIRS;
            if grew {
                print(f"[audit] EXPOSURE: {name} has a recursive watch but "
                      f"{here.ignored_child_count} ignored children now exist "
                      f"under it — should release + re-plan");
            } else {
                print(f"[audit] ok: {name} recursive watch covers "
                      f"{here.ignored_child_count} ignored dirs (under threshold)");
            }
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    # --- Repository layout at STARTUP ---
    #
    #   root
      #   ├── src/        (0 ignored children)
      #   ├── vendor/     (0 ignored children)
      #   ├── build/      (0 ignored children)
      #   └── docs/       (0 ignored children)
    src = Directory(name="src");
    vendor = Directory(name="vendor");
    build = Directory(name="build");
    docs = Directory(name="docs");
    root ++> src ++> vendor ++> build ++> docs;

    # Plan once at startup. None of these has an ignored tree yet, so each
    # gets a recursive watch — that is the *correct* startup decision given
    # the source state.
    root spawn Planner();

    # Simulate the bug: after `watch` started, `npm install` runs and grows
    # `node_modules` under `vendor/`, and `cargo build` produces `target/`
    # under `src/`. The counts reflect post-install reality, but the
    # recursive watch from the startup plan still holds.
    src.ignored_child_count = 5;    # target/ grew under src/
    vendor.ignored_child_count = 6;  # node_modules grew under vendor/

    # The watcher does NOT re-plan, so the recursive edges from startup are
    # still in force. Audit the graph to make the exposure visible.
    print("");
    root spawn Auditor(reentry=False);

    # Now demonstrate the suggested fix: re-plan. The planner sees the new
    # ignored-child counts and would install shallow watches instead.
    print("");
    root spawn Planner(reentry=True);

    # After a re-plan, the recursive edges should be gone. The audit would
    # no longer flag these directories — but the source code doesn't do this
    # today, which is exactly the issue.
    print("");
    src.ignored_child_count = 0;
    vendor.ignored_child_count = 0;
    src +>:Ignored(reason="target/"):+> Directory(name="target_stub");
    vendor +>:Ignored(reason="node_modules/"):+> Directory(name="node_modules_stub");
}
```

