Issue #1521: Emit HLSL helper declarations before their call sites

## Problem

Materialized helper definitions can be emitted after HLSL functions that call them. HLSL does not provide C-style implicit function declarations, so DXC reports the call as an undeclared identifier even when an exact definition appears later in the same artifact.

This is separate from helper materialization and overload binding: the concrete symbol and body both exist, but declaration order makes the artifact invalid.

## Expected behavior

Emit HLSL functions in dependency order or generate legal forward declarations for reachable helpers. Preserve overload identity, parameter direction, arrays, resources, and generated names exactly between declarations, definitions, and calls.

## Acceptance criteria

- Build a call dependency graph for generated HLSL helpers and emit callees before callers when acyclic.
- Emit deterministic forward declarations for recursion and mutually recursive strongly connected components where HLSL supports the signature.
- Keep overload sets, generated mangled names, resource parameter types, fixed-array extents, and `in`/`out`/`inout` qualifiers identical.
- Scope each declaration to the entry-point package that uses it and avoid duplicate or conflicting prototypes.
- Reject signatures that cannot be declared legally in HLSL with a structured target diagnostic.
- Add DXC coverage for a late-defined helper, nested dependencies, overloads, and mutual recursion.

## Real-world impact

At MLX commit `968d264f2903d578e699c4452a4dbf48633921aa`, generated GEMV `run` helpers call specialized `load_safe` and `load_unsafe` functions around lines 6,480-16,000, while the exact definitions begin around line 16,506. Official DXC reports hundreds of undeclared helper calls even though the concrete definitions are present. Resolving the separate pointer and array contracts must not leave these declaration-order failures behind.

## Related work

- #1495 materializes the concrete sibling helpers and binds their canonical symbols; this issue covers legal HLSL declaration order after those bodies exist.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""HLSL helper declaration-order problem modeled with typed edges and walkers.

The issue: HLSL helpers can be emitted after their callers, producing
unresolved identifiers because HLSL has no implicit forward declarations.
We model the dependency graph between generated HLSL functions and use a
walker to compute either a topological emission order (acyclic case) or
detect a strongly connected recursion component (mutual recursion case).
"""

node Helper {
    has name: str = "";
    has kind: str = "function";     # "function" or "entry"
    has signature_ok: bool = True;  # false => cannot be forward-declared
}

# Typed edge meaning: `caller` invokes `callee` somewhere in its body.
edge Calls {
    has call_site: int = 0;         # line index of the call
}

# Typed edge meaning: the helper is emitted as part of this entry-point
# package; declarations are scoped to the package that needs them.
edge BelongsTo { has package: str = ""; }

walker EmitOrder {
    has order: list[str] = [];
    has pending: list[str] = [];
    has cycles: list[list[str]] = [];
    has rejected: list[str] = [];
    has visited: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        # Find every entry point and emit its package in order.
        visit [-->];
    }

    can plan with Helper entry {
        if here.kind != "entry" {
            # Skip non-entry helpers at the top level; they are reached
            # only by walking Calls edges from an entry.
            visit [-->];
            return;
        }
        if here.name in self.visited {
            visit [-->];
            return;
        }
        self.visited.append(here.name);
        pkg = "main";
        # Look up the package label on the incoming BelongsTo edge.
        for e in [<-:BelongsTo:-] {
            pkg = e.package;
        }
        # Depth-first topo emit: emit callees before the caller.
        self.pending.append(here.name);
        self._emit_deps(here);
        self.order.append(here.name);
        print(f"[emit] {pkg} :: {here.name} after deps "
              f"{self.pending[:-1] if len(self.pending) > 1 else []}");
        self.pending = [];
        visit [-->];
    }

    def _emit_deps(self, caller: Helper) -> None {
        for callee in [->:Calls:->] {
            # Reject signatures that cannot be legally forward-declared.
            if not callee.signature_ok {
                self.rejected.append(callee.name);
                print(f"[reject] {callee.name} cannot be forward-declared");
                continue;
            }
            # Mutual recursion: the callee (transitively) calls back into
            # the pending set => an SCC. Emit a forward declaration stub
            # and continue.
            if callee.name in self.pending {
                scc = [caller.name, callee.name];
                self.cycles.append(scc);
                print(f"[fwd-decl] SCC {scc} (mutual recursion) "
                      f"=> emit prototype for {callee.name}");
                continue;
            }
            if callee.name in self.visited {
                # Already emitted earlier; no need to re-emit.
                continue;
            }
