Task:
A maintainer on the project HarperFast/harper (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #879: MCP: remove lazy require() circular-dependency workarounds once TypeScript migration lands

## Context

Follow-up from review of #856 (MCP v1 tools surface).

Several MCP modules reach Harper internals through **lazy `require()` inside try/catch** rather than top-level `import`, to dodge circular-dependency / boot-order init (RocksDB lock acquisition, schema preload, eager graph init) that breaks when the module is loaded from a unit test that hasn't booted Harper:

- `loadServerUtilities()` — `components/mcp/tools/operations.ts` (`require('../../../server/serverHelpers/serverUtilities')`)
- `loadResources()` — `components/mcp/tools/application.ts` (`require('../../../resources/Resources')`)
- `loadRequestTarget()` — `components/mcp/tools/application.ts` (`require('../../../resources/RequestTarget')`)
- `loadItcHandlers()` — `components/mcp/listChanged.ts` (`require('../../../server/itc/serverHandlers')`)

Each is paired with a test-seam override (`_set*ForTest`) and a graceful no-op-on-failure path.

## Problem

- These are workarounds, not intent — they obscure the real dependency graph and defeat static analysis.
- The graceful-degrade-to-no-op paths can mask genuine wiring failures (a real "Resources unavailable" looks the same as "not in a Harper process").

## Proposed direction

Once the broader TypeScript migration / module-graph PR lands (which should mitigate the circular-dependency and eager-init issues), revisit these sites and convert them to top-level `import`s where possible. Keep test seams only where genuinely needed for isolation. Confirm each removal doesn't reintroduce a boot-order or circular-import failure.

## Acceptance

- Lazy `require()` workarounds replaced with static imports where the dependency graph now allows.
- Test seams retained only where required; no silent no-op masking of real failures.

_Filed by Claude on behalf of @kriszyp during #856 review._

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""MCP lazy-require circular-dependency workaround model.

Models the scenario from issue #879: modules that hand-roll lazy
``require()`` inside try/catch to dodge circular-dependency / boot-order
problems, plus the graceful no-op-on-failure fallback that can mask real
wiring failures.

In idiomatic Jac the relationship between a tool, its target module, and
the dependency edges between modules is encoded as typed edges. A walker
traverses the dependency graph (typed ``DependsOn`` edges) and reports
either a clean resolution or a circular-dependency / missing-module
detection. A second walker performs cascade invalidation when a module
is removed from the graph, so its dependents lose their reachability.
"""

node Module {
    has name: str = "";
    has available: bool = True;
    has lazy_loaded: bool = False;
}

node Tool {
    has title: str = "";
    has requires_resource: bool = False;
}

edge DependsOn {
    has weight: int = 1;
}

edge Calls {
    has method: str = "";
}

# Diagnostic walker: walks the typed dependency graph from a tool and
# reports whether every module it depends on is reachable + available.
# A real circular dependency or an unavailable module is reported as an
# error instead of being silently swallowed.
walker DependencyAuditor {
    has path: list[str] = [];
    has errors: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can audit_tool with Tool entry {
        self.path = [here.title];
        visit [->:DependsOn:->];
    }

    can audit_module with Module entry {
        if here.name in self.path {
            cycle = self.path[self.path.index(here.name):];
            cycle_str = " -> ".join(cycle + [here.name]);
            self.errors.append(f"circular dependency: {cycle_str}");
            disengage;
        }
        self.path.append(here.name);
        if not here.available {
            self.errors.append(f"module '{here.name}' unavailable");
            disengage;
        }
        next_targets = [here->:DependsOn:->];
        if next_targets {
            visit next_targets;
        } else {
            disengage;
        }
    }
}

# Cascade invalidation walker: a module marked unavailable pulls down
# every tool that called into it, mimicking "remove the workaround".
walker CascadeInvalidator {
    has invalidated: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can inspect_tool with Tool entry {
        targets = [here->:Calls:->];
        if targets {
            visit targets;
        } else {
            disengage;
        }
    }

    can inspect_module with Module entry {
        if not here.available {
            self.invalidated.append(here.name);
            # disconnect tools that were wired to this module
            for t in [->:Calls:->] {
                t.del->:Calls:->here;
            }
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build the dependency graph. Three modules form a chain, and a tool
    # lazily requires one of them through a Calls edge.
    resources = Module(name="Resources");
    request_target = Module(name="RequestTarget");
    server_utilities = Module(name="ServerUtilities");

    load_resources_tool = Tool(title="loadResources", requires_resource=True);
    load_target_tool = Tool(title="loadRequestTarget");
    load_utils_tool = Tool(title="loadServerUtilities");

    root ++> resources ++> request_target ++> server_utilities;

    # Typed dependency edges between modules.
    resources +>:DependsOn(weight=1):+> server_utilities;
    request_target +>:DependsOn(weight=1):+> server_utilities;

    # Typed "calls" edges from tools into the modules they lazily
    # require() at runtime.
    load_resources_tool +>:Calls(method="loadResources"):+> resources;
    load_target_tool +>:Calls(method="loadRequestTarget"):+> request_target;
    load_utils_tool +>:Calls(method="loadServerUtilities"):+> server_utilities;

    root ++> load_resources_tool;
    root ++> load_target_tool;
    root ++> load_utils_tool;

    # --- Demo 1: dependency audit on the tools, all modules available.
    auditor = DependencyAuditor();
    root spawn auditor;
    print("audit errors (clean run):", auditor.errors);

    # --- Demo 2: mark a module unavailable and re-audit. The graceful
    # no-op-on-failure path would silently swallow this; the auditor
    # surfaces it instead.
    server_utilities.available = False;
    auditor2 = DependencyAuditor();
    root spawn auditor2;
    print("audit errors (missing module):", auditor2.errors);

    # --- Demo 3: cascade invalidation. Tools wired to the now-broken
    # module lose their Calls edge so they can be replaced with static
    # imports without dragging the broken node along.
    invalidator = CascadeInvalidator();
    root spawn invalidator;
    print("invalidated modules:", invalidator.invalidated);

    # restore for the final summary
    server_utilities.available = True;
}
```

