A maintainer on the project pelaggio/pelaggio (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #575: Enforce declared write-sets so a scoped item cannot silently expand its blast radius

**Evidence: an explicit, detailed prohibition failed to constrain the implement step twice in a row on the same ticket.**

#548 was specced as a message-only change to two files. Attempt 1 (scope M, WITH a plan step) built a new fail-closed `dependency-integrity.ts` probe, rewrote seat isolation, and modified the pipeline's park path — hard-blocked. The ticket was then narrowed to scope S with an explicit **'Do not'** section naming each prohibited file and prohibited pattern. Attempt 2 (no plan step) delivered the requested 63-line slice **plus ~490 lines of exactly the prohibited work** — and was hard-blocked for the same class of defect as attempt 1 (a new probe that self-flags legitimate state as corruption).

So neither a plan step nor an explicit prohibition constrained scope. The constraint is currently **persuasion in a prompt**, and the harness has no mechanism that enforces it. That is the gap.

## The mechanism already exists in the design, unbuilt

AGENTS.md carries the planned invariant: *'Declared write-sets are enforced by the worktree write-guard; the scheduler will not co-schedule intersecting write-sets.'* Realize the enforcement half for scoped items:

- An item declares its write-set (the spec's 'Files to change' is already this, in prose).
- The worktree write-guard refuses writes outside it — the same capability-denial posture the project uses everywhere else, rather than asking the model to comply.
- Out-of-scope work becomes a **visible, early, deterministic** failure ('refused write to worktree-deps.ts, not in declared write-set') instead of a $40 review round that discovers it after the fact.

Note the harness already computes a host-side write-set for contained execution (ADR-0023: 'a host-computed write-set, enforced by capability denial — never an agent-declared set'). This item is the read-side counterpart: the *item* declares intent, the guard enforces it, and the host still computes what actually changed.

## Why it pays for itself

Two $40+ review rounds on #548 were spent discovering scope violations that a write-guard would have refused in seconds. The reviews were not wrong — they correctly blocked both attempts — but they are an expensive way to enforce a boundary that is mechanically checkable.

## Design notes

- Escape hatch must be explicit and recorded (an item legitimately discovering it needs another file should surface that as a decision, not a silent write).
- Harness-managed paths (`docs/decision-log/<id>.md`, checkpoints) are always permitted.
- Relates to #572: capability-denial over persuasion is the same principle applied to a different surface.
Scope: M

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Scoped-item write-set guard.

Issue #575 — enforce declared write-sets so a scoped item cannot silently
expand its blast radius. Models the host-side capability-denial posture:
each scoped item declares a write-set of permitted files; the guard
refuses writes outside it deterministically; the harness still records
what actually changed.
"""

# A scoped work item: declares intent via a write-set of file paths.
node ScopedItem {
    has title: str = "";
    has declared_writes: list[str] = [];
    has attempted_writes: list[str] = [];
    has refused_writes: list[str] = [];
    has blocked: bool = False;
}

# A file the item wants to mutate. Edges encode the "is in declared set"
# relationship; the *attempted* writes live as plain state on the item.
node File {
    has path: str = "";
    has in_scope: bool = False;
}

# Typed edge: "this file is declared as in-scope for this item."
edge DeclaredIn { }

# Typed edge: "this file was the target of an attempted write."
edge AttemptedOn { }

walker WriteGuard {
    has current_path: str = "";

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can audit with ScopedItem entry {
        # Two-phase mutation: collect every AttemptedOn target first,
        # then judge. Avoids mid-iteration mutation surprises.
        attempted = [];
        for f in [->:AttemptedOn:->] {
            attempted.append(f);
        }
        for f in attempted {
            self.current_path = f.path;
            f in_scope = self.current_path in here.declared_writes;
            visit [f];
        }
        # A single blocked attempt is enough to flip the item.
        for path in here.refused_writes {
            here.blocked = True;
        }
        visit [-->];
    }

    can judge with File entry {
        # Capability-denial posture: if the file isn't in the declared
        # write-set, refuse the write and record it on the item. The
        # only "soft" path is the harness-managed escape hatch (docs/).
        item = [-->];
        owner = item[0] if len(item) > 0 else None;
        if owner is None {
            return;
        }
        if here.in_scope {
            return;
        }
        # Harness-managed paths are the recorded escape hatch.
        is_harness = here.path.startswith("docs/decision-log/")
            or here.path == "checkpoints";
        if is_harness {
            return;
        }
        owner.refused_writes.append(here.path);
        print(f"refused write to {here.path}, not in declared write-set");
    }
}

with entry {
    # The spec for #548: message-only change to two files. The
    # declaration is the prose write-set; the guard enforces it.
    item = ScopedItem(
        title="#548 message-only patch",
        declared_writes=["src/parser.ts", "src/format.ts"]
    );
    root ++> item;

    # Two permitted files.
    f1 = File(path="src/parser.ts", in_scope=True);
    f2 = File(path="src/format.ts", in_scope=True);
    item +>:AttemptedOn:+> f1;
    item +>:AttemptedOn:+> f2;

    # The prohibited work that blew both attempts: a brand-new probe
    # file plus modifications the ticket explicitly forbade.
    f3 = File(path="dependency-integrity.ts", in_scope=False);
    f4 = File(path="seat-isolation.ts", in_scope=False);
    f5 = File(path="worktree-deps.ts", in_scope=False);
    item +>:AttemptedOn:+> f3;
    item +>:AttemptedOn:+> f4;
    item +>:AttemptedOn:+> f5;

    # Harness-managed path: always permitted, the recorded escape hatch.
    f6 = File(path="docs/decision-log/548.md", in_scope=False);
    item +>:AttemptedOn:+> f6;

    # Typed declarations of the permitted set — what the spec named.
    f1 +>:DeclaredIn:+> item;
    f2 +>:DeclaredIn:+> item;

    item spawn WriteGuard();

    print(f"item.blocked = {item.blocked}");
    print(f"declared_writes = {item.declared_writes}");
    print(f"refused_writes = {item.refused_writes}");
}
