Task:
A maintainer on the project sparkfn/hyperP (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #250: Extract Bitrix deal intelligence and training data from Neo4j

﻿## Summary

Extract Bitrix CRM deal intelligence from HyperP's Neo4j-backed identity-resolution ingestion into a dedicated service/bounded context that owns deal/activity history, analytical datasets, model training, evaluation, and prediction.

This changes the architecture proposed by #123: HyperP/Neo4j remains the identity and relationship graph, while Bitrix deal-history and prediction workloads use their own operational and artifact storage.

Related:

- #123 — explainable 30-day CRM win prioritization roadmap
- #211 — serialized CRM Gate 1 reads after Neo4j transaction-memory pressure

## Problem

Bitrix deal activities, stage histories, calls, meetings, tasks, comments, and similar high-volume analytical records are not primarily identity-resolution data. Persisting the full history in Neo4j:

- increases graph volume and transaction-memory pressure;
- makes identity-resolution ingestion compete with backfill, feature extraction, and training reads;
- couples prediction availability to HyperP ingestion success;
- gives analytical records the wrong lifecycle, query model, and retention boundary; and
- risks turning Neo4j into a general CRM warehouse.

The prediction workload has a separate purpose and operating profile: historical synchronization, point-in-time analysis, dataset generation, training, evaluation, and advisory scoring.

## Architectural decision

Create a dedicated Bitrix Deal Intelligence service. Do not use Neo4j as its primary historical or training store.

### HyperP / Neo4j owns

Only Bitrix data needed for identity resolution and durable relationship traversal, including:

- contact and company source identifiers;
- names, email addresses, phone numbers, and other approved identity evidence;
- contact-to-company relationships;
- stable resolved Person/Entity identifiers;
- resolution lineage and provenance; and
- only lightweight deal references when a demonstrated graph traversal requires them.

Do not write full deal activity/event history to Neo4j merely to support training or prediction.

### Bitrix Deal Intelligence owns

- deals and current deal state;
- pipeline/stage definitions and stage-change history;
- activities, calls, meetings, tasks, comments, and communications;
- salesperson and assignment history;
- training labels and point-in-time feature snapshots;
- dataset manifests and training-run metadata;
- model versions, evaluation results, and active-model selection;
- immutable prediction history and current predictions; and
- Bitrix synchronization checkpoints, retries, and source watermarks.

## Proposed storage

### PostgreSQL

Use a service-owned PostgreSQL database for structured operational and analytical metadata. Expected tables/bounded equivalents include:

- `bitrix_deals`
- `bitrix_deal_stage_history`
- `bitrix_activities`
- `bitrix_activity_participants`
- `bitrix_users`
- `bitrix_companies`
- `bitrix_contacts`
- `deal_identity_links`
- `feature_snapshots`
- `training_runs`
- `model_versions`
- `deal_predictions`
- `sync_checkpoints`

Partition high-volume event/activity tables when justified by measured volume.

### Bind-mounted artifact volume

Use a persistent bind-mounted host directory rather than MinIO. Mount the same artifact root into the service components that need it, with prediction consumers read-only where practical.

Suggested layout:

```text
data/
├── datasets/
│   └── deal-win/<dataset-version>/
│       ├── train.parquet
│       ├── validation.parquet
│       ├── test.parquet
│       └── manifest.json
├── models/
│   └── deal-win/<model-version>/
│       ├── model.joblib
│       ├── metadata.json
│       └── metrics.json
├── evaluations/
├── exports/
└── tmp/
```

The exact host root should be deployment-configurable, but all containers must use a stable internal path such as `/app/data`.

Artifact rules:

- model and dataset versions are immutable;
- completed artifacts are published by temporary write plus atomic rename;
- PostgreSQL records authoritati

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Bitrix deal intelligence over an identity-resolution graph.

Models the split proposed in issue #250: HyperP/Neo4j keeps identity and
lightweight deal references, while a dedicated Bitrix Deal Intelligence
service owns deal histories, activities, model artifacts, and predictions.

A ``DealAuditor`` walker traverses deal -> activities -> stage history to
demonstrate the cascade-invalidation / dependency-ordering traversal the
issue warns about (no hand-rolled parent_id recursion). It also detects
cycles that would arise if activity links back to a deal via a participant
edge, surfacing the cycle as a domain error.
"""

# ---------- Nodes ----------------------------------------------------------

node Person {
    has person_id: str = "";
    has name: str = "";
}

node Company {
    has company_id: str = "";
    has name: str = "";
}

node Deal {
    has deal_id: str = "";
    has title: str = "";
    has amount: float = 0.0;
    has available: bool = True;
}

node Activity {
    has activity_id: str = "";
    has kind: str = "";
    has subject: str = "";
    has available: bool = True;
}

node StageHistory {
    has history_id: str = "";
    has stage: str = "";
    has available: bool = True;
}

node ModelVersion {
    has model_id: str = "";
    has version: str = "";
    has available: bool = True;
}

# ---------- Edges (typed where relationship carries meaning) --------------

edge ResolvesTo { }      # raw identity evidence -> resolved Person
edge EmployedBy { }       # Person -> Company
edge Owns { }             # Company -> Deal (lightweight reference kept here)
edge HasActivity { }      # Deal -> Activity
edge HasHistory { }       # Deal -> StageHistory
edge ParticipatedIn { }   # Activity -> Person (used for cycle probe)
edge TrainedOn { }        # ModelVersion -> Activity set

# ---------- Walker --------------------------------------------------------

walker DealAuditor {
    has order: list[str] = [];
    has seen: set[str] = set();
    has cycle: bool = False;

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    # One ability handles every audit-relevant node type; every node in
    # the union carries ``available`` so the body stays legal.
    can audit with Deal | Activity | StageHistory | ModelVersion entry {
        kind = here.__class__.__name__;
        self.order.append(kind);
        self.seen.add(kind);

        if kind == "Deal" {
            print(f"[DEAL] {here.deal_id} title={here.title} amount={here.amount} avail={here.available}");
            if not here.available {
                print(f"  -> deal invalidated; cascading to dependents");
            }
            visit [->:HasActivity:->];
            visit [->:HasHistory:->];
        } elif kind == "Activity" {
            print(f"  [ACT] {here.activity_id} kind={here.kind} subj={here.subject} avail={here.available}");
            visit [->:ParticipatedIn:->];
        } elif kind == "StageHistory" {
            print(f"  [HIST] {here.history_id} stage={here.stage} avail={here.available}");
        } elif kind == "ModelVersion" {
            print(f"[MODEL] {here.model_id} version={here.version} avail={here.available}");
            visit [->:TrainedOn:->];
        }
    }

    # Cycle probe: if a participant link ever lands back on a Deal we
    # already audited, raise the cycle flag (domain observable behavior).
    can probe with Deal entry {
        if "Deal" in self.seen {
            self.cycle = True;
            print(f"  [CYCLE] activity chain returned to Deal {here.deal_id}");
            disengage;
        }
        visit [-->];
    }

    can report with Root entry {
        print(f"audit order: {self.order}");
        print(f"cycle detected: {self.cycle}");
    }
}

# ---------- Demo wiring ---------------------------------------------------

with entry {
    # Identity graph (HyperP/Neo4j side): only lightweight deal references.
    alice = Person(person_id="P-1", name="Alice");
    acme = Company(company_id="C-1", name="Acme");
    globex = Company(company_id="C-2", name="Globex");

    # Bitrix deal intelligence graph: deals, activities, history, model.
    d1 = Deal(deal_id="D-1001", title="Acme Q4 renewal", amount=42000.0);
    d2 = Deal(deal_id="D-1002", title="Globex upsell", amount=18000.0, available=False);

    a1 = Activity(activity_id="A-1", kind="call", subject="Discovery");
    a2 = Activity(activity_id="A-2", kind="meeting", subject="Demo");
    a3 = Activity(activity_id="A-3", kind="task", subject="Follow-up");
    h1 = StageHistory(history_id="H-1", stage="Negotiation");
    h2 = StageHistory(history_id="H-2", stage="Closed Won");
