Task:
A maintainer on the project stanoddly/Pixely (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #415: Add a clean-slate retained-mode UI library

## Context

`Pixely.Pencuil` is an immediate-mode UI experiment. A separate historical `GameKit.Uiui` experiment explored a retained widget tree with measure, arrange, invalidation, and rendering, but its implementation is incomplete and coupled to the obsolete GameKit architecture.

Both projects are research material only. The retained-mode library must be designed for current Pixely without API compatibility, shared implementation, or a dependency on either experiment.

## Goals

- Retain element identity, state, layout results, and interaction state across frames.
- Recompute layout only when layout-affecting state or constraints change.
- Rebuild paint output only when visual state changes.
- Compose UI from a small set of layout, visual, and interaction primitives.
- Integrate with current Pixely rendering, input, component lifecycles, `ViewScope`, stages, and multiple windows.
- Keep tree, layout, painting, hit testing, focus, and routing independently testable without a GPU or window.

## Architectural decisions

### Project boundary

The tree, layout, draw-list, and hit-testing core must reference `Pixely.Core` only. GPU, window, font rasterization, DI, input-service, and component integration belong in adapter projects. Enforce this boundary with an architecture test.

### Retained element tree

Use mutable retained elements with object identity and exclusive parent ownership. Consumers build a tree once and mutate element properties or child collections; rebuilding the tree replaces element identity and therefore loses retained focus, scrolling, selection, and control state.

Parent removal and reparenting must have defined lifecycle behavior. Removing a subtree clears root state that refers to it, including hover, pressed state, focus, pointer capture, animation registration, and retained rendering resources.

Elements support:

- measurement under constraints;
- arrangement into final bounds;
- painting into an ordered, renderer-independent draw list;
- hit testing against arranged geometry.

Tree mutation during measure, arrange, paint, or hit testing is invalid and should fail in debug builds. Mutation during event routing is allowed but is applied after the current route completes.

### Layout and coordinates

Layout uses integer logical UI units. A root owns the conversion between window coordinates, logical UI coordinates, and device pixels. Input is converted exactly once at the root boundary; rendering applies the logical-to-device transform exactly once. The root recomputes its scale and invalidates layout when the window resolution or point-to-pixel ratio changes.

Measurement uses explicit min/max constraints rather than a single available-size vector. A measured size must satisfy those constraints. Each element caches the constraints and desired size from its last measurement; measurement with identical constraints must reuse the cached result. A child may be remeasured when a parent assigns different constraints, including tightened constraints for flex/stretch and height-for-width content.

Measure invalidation propagates toward the root until it reaches a relayout boundary whose outer desired size cannot change; that boundary becomes arrange-dirty. Arrange invalidation implies paint invalidation. Paint invalidation rebuilds paint output but does not invalidate layout. Properties declare which level they affect; hover, focus, and pressed state default to paint-only.

Initial layout primitives:

- padding;
- alignment;
- row and column with explicit flex/stretch semantics;
- overlay;
- constrained size;
- clipping;
- rectangle, text, and image leaves.

Bounds containment for layout and hit testing is half-open. Do not use the existing inclusive `Rectangle.Intersects` point test for adjacent UI elements.

### Paint and draw list

Elements paint through a GPU-independent canvas that records an ordered draw list. Commands carry the state required for later replay, including primitive kind, visual resource

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Retained-mode UI: tree of layout elements with measure/arrange/paint state.

Demonstrates the core retained-element machinery: a tree of element nodes
connected by typed ``HasChild`` edges, with measure, arrange, and paint
dirty flags that propagate toward the root. A walker cascades invalidation
across the subtree and prunes a removed branch — replacing hand-rolled
parent_id recursion and manual cascade deletes in the original issue.
"""

node Element {
    has name: str = "";
    has measure_dirty: bool = True;
    has arrange_dirty: bool = True;
    has paint_dirty: bool = True;
}

node Leaf(Element) {
    has text: str = "";
}

node Container(Element) {
    has direction: str = "row";
}

edge HasChild { }

edge Removed { }

walker InvalidationCascade {
    has visited: list = [];

    can start with Container | Leaf entry {
        self.visited.append(here.name);
        here.measure_dirty = True;
        here.arrange_dirty = True;
        here.paint_dirty = True;
        visit [->:HasChild:->];
    }
}

walker MarkRemoved {
    can start with Container | Leaf entry {
        here.measure_dirty = False;
        here.arrange_dirty = False;
        here.paint_dirty = False;
        visit [->:HasChild:->];
    }
}

walker PruneBranch {
    has cut: str = "";

    can start with Container | Leaf entry {
        if here.name == self.cut {
            print(f"pruning subtree rooted at {here.name}");
            visit [->:HasChild:->];
        } else {
            visit [->:HasChild:->];
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    root_container = Container(name="root", direction="column");
    header = Container(name="header", direction="row");
    body = Container(name="body", direction="column");
    footer = Container(name="footer", direction="row");
    title = Leaf(name="title", text="Pixely");
    btn_a = Leaf(name="btn_a", text="A");
    btn_b = Leaf(name="btn_b", text="B");
    para = Leaf(name="para", text="Hello");
    credit = Leaf(name="credit", text="(c)");

    root_container +>:HasChild:+> header;
    root_container +>:HasChild:+> body;
    root_container +>:HasChild:+> footer;
    header +>:HasChild:+> title;
    header +>:HasChild:+> btn_a;
    header +>:HasChild:+> btn_b;
    body +>:HasChild:+> para;
    footer +>:HasChild:+> credit;

    root ++> root_container;

    print("=== initial: all dirty ===");
    print(f"root_container: measure={root_container.measure_dirty} arrange={root_container.arrange_dirty} paint={root_container.paint_dirty}");

    cascade = InvalidationCascade();
    root_container spawn cascade;
    print("cascade visited: " + ", ".join(cascade.visited));

    print("=== after cascade: all dirty again ===");
    print(f"root_container: measure={root_container.measure_dirty} arrange={root_container.arrange_dirty} paint={root_container.paint_dirty}");
    print(f"title: measure={title.measure_dirty} arrange={title.arrange_dirty} paint={title.paint_dirty}");

    root_container spawn MarkRemoved();
    print("=== after MarkRemoved: all clean ===");
    print(f"root_container: measure={root_container.measure_dirty} arrange={root_container.arrange_dirty} paint={root_container.paint_dirty}");
    print(f"credit: measure={credit.measure_dirty} arrange={credit.arrange_dirty} paint={credit.paint_dirty}");

    root_container spawn PruneBranch(cut="body");
    print("=== after PruneBranch(cut='body'): only body subtree pruned ===");
    print(f"para: measure={para.measure_dirty} arrange={para.arrange_dirty} paint={para.paint_dirty}");
    print(f"credit: measure={credit.measure_dirty} arrange={credit.arrange_dirty} paint={credit.paint_dirty}");
}
```

