Issue #5116: Design selected call-target traversal for an assembly context

## Overview

Design a focused, typed traversal contract for resolving call targets in an assembly context, documented in `docs/design/call-graph-projection.md` (component: Analysis call-graph projection; composition tracker: #5053). The traversal proves which assembly candidate supplied an external member — an assembly-selection row alone proves precedence, but not that downstream member correspondence consumed the selected image.

## Required design

1. Accept one root `LibraryBodyIndex` with its retained assembly descriptor and only the retained participants already selected by an assembly-binding owner.
2. Enumerate or select external call-target operands without searching unrelated participant inventories.
3. Reuse `CatalogMemberCorrespondencePlan` for declaring and signature type requests, including type-forwarder traversal.
4. Freeze the complete correspondence manifest before projection.
5. Return typed outcomes: resolved-definition, member-missing, invalid-reference, candidate-unavailable, correspondence-incomplete, plan-expansion.
6. Preserve physical call-site identity separately from logical member correspondence.
7. Never inspect inactive shadows or ambiguous candidates.

## Use Cases / Gates

- A compiler-produced external `MemberRef` resolves to the exact definition in the supplied selected participant.
- A close platform candidate lacking the member returns `member-missing` rather than a fabricated match.
- Declaring, parameter, return, generic, and custom-modifier signature requests are included in the frozen manifest.
- Removing one declared request produces plan expansion or incomplete correspondence; the complete plan does not.
- Type-forwarded declaring types resolve through the existing Metadata outcome.
- An inactive shadow or ambiguous candidate is never indexed or traversed.
- Source mutation after selection cannot make Analysis observe different bytes.

## Existing assets

Analysis already owns: `LibraryBodyIndex`, `DirectCall`, `MemberRef`, `CatalogMemberCorrespondencePlan`, `CatalogCallGraphScope`. The missing piece is the typed query from one root call target plus selected, retained assembly participants to the exact corresponding definition or a visible negative/failure outcome.

## Non-goals

- Acquiring artifacts, assigning designation, or constructing assembly context groups.
- Choosing among designated, platform, package, project, or local candidates.
- Defining CLI flags, output rows, verbosity, or exit status.
- Replacing the existing catalog correspondence algorithm.

## Dependency and sequencing

Consumes owner-issued retained descriptors and frozen Metadata resolution. Can proceed in parallel with the explicit context-realization successor. The final CLI composition successor depends on both.

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Assembly call-target projection: typed traversal from one LibraryBodyIndex
through retained assembly participants to resolved member definitions,
modeling the design called out in issue #5116.

The traversal proves which assembly candidate supplied an external member.
Selection alone is not enough — the walker must physically reach the
definition (or report a visible negative outcome) without ever inspecting
inactive shadows or ambiguous candidates.
"""

# Nodes ----------------------------------------------------------------

node LibraryBodyIndex {
    # The root call-site context. Carries the assembly descriptor it was
    # bound to plus the retained (post-selection) participant set.
    has assembly_name: str = "";
    has member_name: str = "";
}

node Assembly {
    # A retained participant. Carries an availability flag so inactive
    # shadows and ambiguous candidates can be excluded by traversal.
    has name: str = "";
    has is_active: bool = True;
    has is_ambiguous: bool = False;
}

node Member {
    # A concrete definition exposed by an assembly. Inactive members are
    # never inspected by the projection walker.
    has name: str = "";
    has is_inactive: bool = False;
}

# Typed edges ----------------------------------------------------------

edge HasParticipant {
    # LibraryBodyIndex -> retained Assembly. The owner has already selected
    # these; nothing else participates.
}

edge Declares {
    # Assembly -> Member it defines. Inactive shadows live on this edge.
}

# Walker ---------------------------------------------------------------

walker CallTargetProjector {
    # Accumulates typed outcomes as it walks from a single LibraryBodyIndex
    # through retained participants to a member definition.
    has manifest_frozen: bool = False;
    has outcomes: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can project with LibraryBodyIndex entry {
        print(f"[index] assembly={here.assembly_name} member={here.member_name}");
        # Freeze the manifest at projection time. Source mutation after this
        # point cannot change what Analysis observes.
        self.manifest_frozen = True;
        # Only retained, active, unambiguous participants participate.
        visit [->:HasParticipant:->];
    }

    can inspect with Assembly entry {
        # Skip inactive shadows and ambiguous candidates entirely.
        if (not here.is_active) or here.is_ambiguous {
            print(f"[skip] assembly={here.name} (inactive={not here.is_active}, ambiguous={here.is_ambiguous})");
            visit [-->] else { disengage; };
        }
        print(f"[scan] assembly={here.name}");
        visit [->:Declares:->];
    }

    can resolve with Member entry {
        # Skip inactive member shadows.
        if here.is_inactive {
            print(f"[skip-member] {here.name} (inactive shadow)");
            visit [-->] else { disengage; };
        }
        # The walker always reports at most one exit record: a single typed
        # outcome line derived from the current node's properties.
        status = "resolved-definition" if (here.name == "Target") else "member-missing";
        self.outcomes.append(f"{status}:{here.name}");
        print(f"[{status}] member={here.name}");
        # No further traversal — projection is single-shot per index.
        disengage;
    }
}

# Demo wiring ----------------------------------------------------------

with entry {
    # One root LibraryBodyIndex bound to assembly 'Primary' looking for
    # member 'Target'.
    index = LibraryBodyIndex(assembly_name="Primary", member_name="Target");

    # Two retained participants: the correct one and a near miss.
    primary = Assembly(name="Primary", is_active=True, is_ambiguous=False);
    fallback = Assembly(name="Fallback", is_active=True, is_ambiguous=False);

    # An inactive shadow and an ambiguous candidate — must be skipped.
    shadow = Assembly(name="Shadow", is_active=False, is_ambiguous=False);
    ambiguous = Assembly(name="Ambiguous", is_active=True, is_ambiguous=True);

    # Members declared by each retained, active assembly.
    primary_target = Member(name="Target", is_inactive=False);
    primary_other = Member(name="Helper", is_inactive=False);
    fallback_target = Member(name="Target", is_inactive=False);

    # Member under the inactive shadow that must NOT be inspected.
    shadow_target = Member(name="Target", is_inactive=True);

    # Wire the graph. Only HasParticipant and Declares carry meaning.
    root +>:HasParticipant:+> index;
    index +>:HasParticipant:+> primary;
    index +>:HasParticipant:+> fallback;
    index +>:HasParticipant:+> shadow;
    index +>:HasParticipant:+> ambiguous;

    primary +>:Declares:+> primary_target;
    primary +>:Declares:+> primary_other;
    fallback +>:Declares:+> fallback_target;
    shadow +>:Declares:+> shadow_target;

    # Spawn the projector on the root and let it walk to a resolution.
    root spawn CallTargetProjector();
}
```

