Issue #6: Implement GraphVisualizer for BFS/DFS traversal state

Description
Graph traversal algorithms (BFS/DFS and variants) are defined by their evolving visited set and frontier. We need a `GraphVisualizer` that turns an `ExecutionTrace` into per-step traversal state over a graph.

Detailed Requirements

1. Graph detection:
Accept the graph as an explicit adjacency-list/adjacency-matrix argument (or `--graph-arg` override) rather than trying to infer graph structure from arbitrary code.

2. State tracking:
Track the `visited`/`seen` collection and the frontier structure (queue for BFS, stack/recursion for DFS) as they're mutated across steps, plus the "current node" being processed.

3. Frame emission:
Emit one `VisualizationFrame` per step containing: visited set, frontier contents (ordered), current node, and the edge just traversed (if any).

Technical Design Hints
Reuse the collection-mutation tracking approach already used for backtracking detection in `algorithm-pattern-classifier` (watching `append`/`add`/`pop`/`popleft` calls on the tracked collections) to identify frontier changes without requiring the traced code to be annotated.

Acceptance Criteria
 - [ ] Correctly visualizes BFS shortest-path style traversal (queue-based) on a small graph
 - [ ] Correctly visualizes DFS traversal (stack/recursion-based) on a small graph
 - [ ] Visited set and frontier are accurate at every step
 - [ ] Unit tests cover both an adjacency-list graph and a grid graph (common in matrix BFS/DFS problems)

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Graph traversal visualizer: BFS and DFS walkers emit per-step frames.

Models the issue's complaint by replacing hand-rolled recursion / queue /
stack machinery with native Jac walkers that carry typed-edge traversal
and emit one VisualizationFrame per visited node. The frontier and visited
state live on the walker itself (`has` fields), updated as the walker
traverses, so the per-step VisualizationFrame mirrors the issue's spec:
visited set, ordered frontier, current node, edge just traversed.
"""

# ---- Entity archetypes --------------------------------------------------

node GraphNode {
    has label: str = "";
    has visited: bool = False;
    has distance: int = -1;
    has parent_label: str = "";
}

# ---- Frame record -------------------------------------------------------

obj VisualizationFrame {
    has step: int = 0;
    has current: str = "";
    has visited: list[str] = [];
    has frontier: list[str] = [];
    has edge_traversed: str = "";
}

# ---- Typed edges --------------------------------------------------------

edge Adjacent {
    has weight: int = 1;
}

# ---- BFS walker: queue frontier -----------------------------------------

walker BFSVisualizer {
    has start_label: str = "";
    has frontier: list[str] = [];
    has visited_set: list[str] = [];
    has frames: list[VisualizationFrame] = [];
    has step: int = 0;
    has target: str = "";
    has found: bool = False;

    can start with Root entry {
        print(f"BFS start={self.start_label} target={self.target}");
        self.frontier = [self.start_label];
        # Seed frame at root so the first emit shows the initial state.
        seed = VisualizationFrame(
            step=self.step,
            current=self.start_label,
            visited=[self.start_label],
            frontier=[self.start_label],
            edge_traversed=""
        );
        self.frames.append(seed);
        visit [->:Adjacent:->];
    }

    can expand with GraphNode entry {
        if self.found {
            # Target already reached: walk without mutating state.
            visit [->:Adjacent:->];
        }
        label = here.label;
        is_target = label == self.target;

        # Only the first time we see a node do we mark + record it.
        if not here.visited {
            here.visited = True;
            here.distance = self.step;
            here.parent_label = "" if self.step == 0 else self.frontier[0];
            self.visited_set = self.visited_set + [label];
            self.step = self.step + 1;

            edge_note = "";
            if here.parent_label != "" {
                edge_note = f"{here.parent_label}->{label}";
            }

            # BFS frontier is a queue: append newly discovered neighbors
            # in typed-edge order, so the oldest entry is processed first.
            new_frontier = [label];
            for n in [->:Adjacent:->] {
                if not n.visited and n.label not in self.frontier
                        and n.label not in new_frontier {
                    new_frontier = new_frontier + [n.label];
                }
            }

            old_frontier = self.frontier;
            # Drop the head we just consumed, then enqueue discoveries.
            tail = [];
            i = 1;
            while i < old_frontier.length {
                tail = tail + [old_frontier[i]];
                i = i + 1;
            }
            self.frontier = tail + new_frontier;

            frame = VisualizationFrame(
                step=self.step,
                current=label,
                visited=self.visited_set,
                frontier=self.frontier,
                edge_traversed=edge_note
            );
            self.frames.append(frame);

            if is_target {
                self.found = True;
            }
        }

        # Continue traversal across typed adjacency edges.
        visit [->:Adjacent:->];
    }

    can finish with Root entry {
        print(f"BFS frames emitted: {self.frames.length}");
        for f in self.frames {
            front = ",".join(f.frontier);
            vis = ",".join(f.visited);
            print(
                f"  step={f.step} cur={f.current} "
                f"visited=[{vis}] frontier=[{front}] "
                f"edge={f.edge_traversed}"
            );
        }
    }
}

# ---- DFS walker: stack frontier -----------------------------------------

walker DFSVisualizer {
    has start_label: str = "";
    has frontier: list[str] = [];
    has visited_set: list[str] = [];
    has frames: list[VisualizationFrame] = [];
    has step: int = 0;
    has target: str = "";
    has found: bool = False;

    can start with Root entry {
        print(f"DFS start={self.start_label} target={self.target}");
        self.frontier = [self.start_label];
        visit [->:Adjacent:->];
    }

    can descend with GraphNode entry {
        if self.found {
            visit [->:Adjacent:->];
        }
        label = here.label;
        is_target = label == self.target;

        if not here.visited {
            here.visited = True;
            here.distance = self.step;
            here.parent_label = "" if self.step == 0 else self.frontier[self.frontier.length - 1];
            self.visited_set = self.visited_set + [label];
            self.step = self.step + 1;

            edge_note = "";
            if here.parent_label != "" {
                edge_note = f"{here.parent_label}->{label}";
            }

            # DFS frontier is a stack: push unvisited neighbors on top.
            new_top = [];
            for n in [->:Adjacent:->] {
                if not n.visited and n.label not in self.frontier
                        and n.label not in new_top {
                    new_top = new_top + [n.label];
                }
            }
            # Drop the head we just consumed, then push discoveries in
            # reverse so the first neighbor is processed first (LIFO).
            tail = [];
            i = 1;
            while i < self.frontier.length {
                tail = tail + [self.frontier[i]];
                i = i + 1;
            }
            reversed_top = [];
            j = new_top.length - 1;
            while j >= 0 {
                reversed_top = reversed_top + [new_top[j]];
                j = j - 1;
            }
            self.frontier = tail + reversed_top;

            frame = VisualizationFrame(
                step=self.step,
                current=label,
                visited=self.visited_set,
                frontier=self.frontier,
                edge_traversed=edge_note
            );
            self.frames.append(frame);

            if is_target {
                self.found = True;
            }
        }

        visit [->:Adjacent:->];
    }

    can finish with Root entry {
        print(f"DFS frames emitted: {self.frames.length}");
        for f in self.frames {
            front = ",".join(f.frontier);
            vis = ",".join(f.visited);
            print(
                f"  step={f.step} cur={f.current} "
                f"visited=[{vis}] frontier=[{front}] "
                f"edge={f.edge_traversed}"
            );
        }
    }
}

# ---- Demo wiring --------------------------------------------------------

with entry {
    # Build a small graph: A - B - C, A - D, D - E (grid-of-roads shape).
    a = GraphNode(label="A");
    b = GraphNode(label="B");
    c = GraphNode(label="C");
    d = GraphNode(label="D");
    e = GraphNode(label="E");

    root ++> a;
    a +>:Adjacent:+> b;
    a +>:Adjacent:+> d;
    b +>:Adjacent:+> c;
    d +>:Adjacent:+> e;
    c +>:Adjacent:+> a;
    e +>:Adjacent:+> a;

    # BFS from A looking for E.
    bfs = BFSVisualizer(start_label="A", target="E");
    root spawn bfs;

    print("---");

    # DFS from A looking for C.
    dfs = DFSVisualizer(start_label="A", target="C");
    root spawn dfs;
}
