A maintainer on the project PersonalClaw/PersonalClaw (Python) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #761: Tag merge can create a self-cycle (parent_id == id), the invariant set_tag_parent guards against

## Summary

`merge_tags` can leave the surviving tag pointing at itself (`parent_id == id`) — a self-cycle in the tag taxonomy. Its sibling `set_tag_parent` explicitly rejects exactly this (`raise ValueError("tag_cycle")`), but the merge path has no equivalent guard.

## Root cause

`merge_tags` re-parents the source's children to the target (`knowledge/store.py:1856-1858`):
```
UPDATE tags SET parent_id = ? WHERE parent_id = ?   -- (target_id, source_id)
```
When the merge SOURCE is the PARENT of the TARGET, the target's `parent_id` was `source_id`; this UPDATE rewrites it to `target_id` — i.e. the target now points at itself. There is no self-reference check here, unlike `set_tag_parent` (`store.py:1786-1795`) which raises `tag_cycle` for `parent_id == tag_id` and walks the ancestor chain to block multi-hop cycles.

## Repro (on a throwaway copy of the live knowledge.db — never the live DB)

Live taxonomy has `zfs` (id 2, root) and `homelab` (id 1, parent=2). On a `cp` of the DB, through the real `KnowledgeStore`:
```
merge_tags(source=2 /*zfs, the parent*/, target=1 /*homelab, the child*/)  -> {'moved':20,'already':3}
tag 1 (homelab): parent_id == 1   # SELF-CYCLE
```
Contrast (asymmetry): live `PATCH /api/knowledge/tags/2 {"parent_id":1}` is correctly rejected with `tag_cycle` (HTTP 400). Only the merge path is unguarded.

## Expected vs Actual

- **Expected:** merge should null the survivor's parent (or refuse) when the merge would point it at itself, mirroring `set_tag_parent`'s cycle guard.
- **Actual:** a `parent_id == id` row persists.

## Scope

Medium. No hang reproduced today (the store's own `set_tag_parent` ancestor-walk carries a `seen` set, and `TagManager.tsx` renders one level, no recursion), but: the row violates the taxonomy's core invariant; a self-cycled tag is not a `parent_id === null` root so it renders as an orphan "straggler" in the FE (`TagManager.tsx:78`); and any future recursive / `WITH RECURSIVE` consumer of `parent_id` inherits a latent infinite loop — the exact hazard the `set_tag_parent` guard exists to prevent. Distinct from #606 (that issue is merge's missing confirm + unknown-id-fails-open; this is the self-cycle).

## Environment

`origin/main` @ `9285070436b508431519647c898fb09775d5c486`. Reproduced on a discarded DB copy; the live instance was never mutated (baseline 26/154/175, 64 tags verified before and after).

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Tag taxonomy merge with cycle guard.

Models the essential scenario from PersonalClaw issue #761:
``merge_tags`` re-parents a source tag's children to the target tag, and if
the source happens to be the parent of the target, the survivor ends up
pointing at itself (parent_id == id). The sibling ``set_tag_parent`` already
rejects this with a ``tag_cycle`` error after walking the ancestor chain;
the merge path must mirror that guard.

The demo builds a small tag graph (zfs root -> homelab child -> two leaves),
then walks the ancestry from each leaf to surface the cycle as a list of
``Tag`` node ids — exactly the ancestor walk the original ``set_tag_parent``
guard performs.
"""

node Tag {
    has tag_id: int = 0;
    has label: str = "";
}

edge ChildOf {
    has order: int = 0;
}

walker AncestryAuditor {
    has trace: list[int] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [->:ChildOf:->];
    }

    can climb with Tag entry {
        # Record the current tag's id, then walk upward to its parent.
        # Guard against the self-cycle: if ChildOf points back to the
        # same node we just recorded, that's the bug the merge introduced.
        if self.trace.count(here.tag_id) > 0 {
            self.trace.append(here.tag_id);
            print(f"CYCLE detected at tag {here.tag_id}: trace = {self.trace}");
            disengage;
        }
        self.trace.append(here.tag_id);
        print(f"visited tag {here.tag_id} ({here.label}); trace so far = {self.trace}");
        visit [here->:ChildOf:->];
    }

    can climb with Tag exit {
        # Dead-end (root tag with no ChildOf) — nothing to do, just report.
        print(f"rooted at tag {here.tag_id} ({here.label})");
    }
}

walker MergeGuard {
    has source_id: int = 0;
    has target_id: int = 0;

    can start with Root entry {
        # Walk all tags, perform the merge-style re-parent, then audit.
        visit [->:ChildOf:->];
    }

    can merge with Tag entry {
        if here.tag_id == self.source_id {
            print(f"merge: source tag {self.source_id} is being folded into target {self.target_id}");
        }
        visit [->:ChildOf:->];
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build the taxonomy:
    #   zfs(2) --ChildOf--> homelab(1) --ChildOf--> backup(10)
    #                                     \--ChildOf--> snapshot(11)
    zfs = Tag(tag_id=2, label="zfs");
    homelab = Tag(tag_id=1, label="homelab");
    backup = Tag(tag_id=10, label="backup");
    snapshot = Tag(tag_id=11, label="snapshot");

    # Parent-of relationships (child --ChildOf--> parent).
    homelab +>:ChildOf:+> zfs;
    backup +>:ChildOf:+> homelab;
    snapshot +>:ChildOf:+> homelab;

    root ++> zfs ++> homelab ++> backup;
    root ++> snapshot;

    print("--- audit: walk ancestor chain from backup ---");
    backup spawn AncestryAuditor();

    print("--- audit: walk ancestor chain from snapshot ---");
    snapshot spawn AncestryAuditor();

    print("--- merge guard: try merge_tags(source=2 /*zfs*/, target=1 /*homelab*/) ---");
    print("expected: set_tag_parent-style rejection (tag_cycle) or null out survivor's parent");
    print("actual in buggy code: homelab.parent_id == homelab.id (self-cycle)");

    # Demonstrate the buggy merge by simulating it: re-wire homelab's
    # ChildOf edge so it points at itself, then re-audit to surface the cycle.
    print("--- simulating buggy merge: homelab now points at itself ---");
    # Disconnect homelab's existing ChildOf edge to zfs, then add a self-edge.
    homelab del ->:ChildOf:-> zfs;
    homelab +>:ChildOf:+> homelab;

    print("--- re-audit from backup after the buggy merge ---");
    backup spawn AncestryAuditor();
}
```

