Task:
A maintainer on the project rust-lang/cargo (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #11819: Timing report of `cargo build --timings` fails to register dependency resolution timings

### Problem

When I run `cargo build --timings` on a newly checked-out branch, it automatically tries to update the crates.io indexes and download the dependencies, but the timing of this is not included in the graph. On a slow network, this can take a long time.

As it is the first item that is executed, this can lead to extremely large gaps in the timings data where according to the graphs, nothing is happening, but where we're actually downloading the latest dependencies.

### Proposed Solution

It would be super useful if the report included, amongst others:

- The time it takes to update the crates.io index
- For each crate that we download a new version of, the time it took to download this crate.

### Notes

_No response_

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Cargo timings model: build phases and their dependencies, walked by a timings reporter.

Models the scenario from cargo issue #11819 where `cargo build --timings`
fails to register the time spent on the index update and on per-crate
downloads. We model BuildPhase nodes (index update, resolve, download,
compile, link) and a Crate node, connected by typed edges that carry
timing payloads, then walk the graph to emit a unified timing report.
"""

node BuildPhase {
    has name: str = "";
    has duration_ms: int = 0;
    has summary: str = "";
}

node Crate {
    has name: str = "";
    has version: str = "";
    has available: bool = True;
}

edge Precedes {
    has gap_ms: int = 0;
}

edge Downloads {
    has bytes: int = 0;
}

edge Resolves {
    has note: str = "";
}

walker TimingsReporter {
    has total_ms: int = 0;
    has reported: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [->:Precedes:->];
    }

    can report_phase with BuildPhase entry {
        self.total_ms += here.duration_ms;
        label = f"{here.name} took {here.duration_ms}ms";
        self.reported.append(label);
        print(label);
        visit [->:Precedes:->] else { disengage; }
    }

    can report_download with Crate entry {
        if not here.available {
            msg = f"download for {here.name}@{here.version} was missing";
            print(msg);
            self.reported.append(msg);
        } else {
            msg = f"downloaded {here.name}@{here.version}";
            print(msg);
            self.reported.append(msg);
        }
        visit [->:Precedes:->] else { disengage; }
    }
}

with entry {
    index_update = BuildPhase(
        name="update crates.io index",
        duration_ms=4200,
        summary="refreshed registry index from network"
    );
    resolve = BuildPhase(
