Issue #844: Separate zero-edge LSP symbol inventory from the traversal graph

## Goal

Keep repository-specific LSP symbols queryable without forcing large zero-edge inventories into every structural traversal graph and resident query process.

## Context

Profiling the cold exact-tree Django cache for #839 showed that its 301,300 persisted nodes are fully accounted, but not all are structural graph entities. The extractors produced 111,442 nodes and LSP consumers added 189,229 virtual nodes. Two zero-enrichable-node languages dominate the expansion:

- gettext / babel-lsp: 81,150 virtual nodes, 0 edges;
- plaintext / cohort language server: 6,290 virtual nodes, 0 edges.

Other contributors include Python (93,987 virtual nodes / 71,584 edges), CSS (1,618 / 0), TypeScript (1,398 / 0), and JSON (1,586 / 23).

These can be legitimate searchable repository entities (for example translation keys), so deleting them blindly would violate RNA's context-assembly purpose. But loading zero-edge inventory into the petgraph traversal projection costs startup time and resident memory even when an agent asks for one exact code node.

Discovered and dispositioned during #839; #839 fixes the unrelated unbounded diagnostics path and documents these counts.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Classify each LSP virtual-node producer by provenance, entity semantics, query value, and graph-edge participation.
- [ ] Measure cache bytes, graph-load time, and resident memory by producer on the retained Django cache.
- [ ] Define separate persisted/query projections when an entity is useful for search but has no structural graph role.
- [ ] Exact search continues to find useful non-code entities such as gettext keys after projection changes.
- [ ] Exact-node graph traversal does not hydrate zero-edge search-only inventories.
- [ ] Nodes with real LSP relationships remain available to neighbors/impact/path traversal.
- [ ] Add regression fixtures for high-cardinality gettext/plaintext inventories and mixed code/non-code roots.
- [ ] Demonstrate lower large-cache startup/RSS without weakening source identity or provenance.

## Non-goals

- Treating raw node count alone as a defect.
- Dropping non-code repository knowledge.
- Folding resident multi-request runtime work from #838 into this issue.


Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""Repo LSP inventory: keep zero-edge symbol nodes out of the structural
traversal projection while preserving exact search across them.

Models the scenario from RNA #844 in miniature: a repository has both
structural source files (with real LSP edges such as imports and calls)
and large zero-edge symbol inventories (e.g. gettext translation keys,
plaintext cohort entries) that are useful for exact lookup but should
NOT be hydrated during a code-graph traversal.
"""

node Repo {
    has name: str = "";
}

node SourceFile {
    has path: str = "";
    has language: str = "";
}

node Symbol {
    # A structural LSP symbol with real graph relationships (definitions,
    # references, imports). Lives on the traversal graph.
    has name: str = "";
    has kind: str = "";
}

node VirtualSymbol {
    # A zero-edge LSP "virtual" node (translation key, plaintext entry).
    # Searchable by exact name; carries no structural edges.
    has key: str = "";
    has provenance: str = "";
}

node TraversalRoot {
    # The single anchor exact-node graph traversal starts from. Marked
    # so walker's typed-edge filter ignores VirtualSymbol inventories.
    has label: str = "";
}

edge Owns {}
edge Imports {}
edge Calls {}
edge Holds {}

walker ExactLookup {
    # An exact-name search across the whole repo. Walks every node type
    # so search-only inventories stay reachable for queries.
    has query: str = "";
    has matches: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can search with Repo entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can search with SourceFile entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can search with Symbol entry {
        if here.name == self.query {
            self.matches.append(f"Symbol:{here.name}");
        }
        visit [-->];
    }

    can search with VirtualSymbol entry {
        if here.key == self.query {
            self.matches.append(f"VirtualSymbol:{here.key}");
        }
        visit [-->];
    }

    can search with TraversalRoot entry {
        visit [-->];
    }
}

walker CodeGraphTraversal {
    # Exact-node graph traversal. Follows ONLY structural edges
    # (Owns / Imports / Calls / Holds). Never hydrates VirtualSymbol
    # inventories even when they hang off a SourceFile via Holds.
    has visited_symbols: list[str] = [];
    has hydrated_virtual_count: int = 0;

    can start with TraversalRoot entry {
        print(f"Starting structural traversal from {here.label}");
        visit [->:Owns:->];
